// Rep Max questions: Biology (AQA 8461), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gbi-001","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A cell in a student's drawing is 6 mm long. The drawing shows the cell at ×200 magnification. What is the real length of the cell?","o":["1200 μm","3 μm","30 μm","300 μm"],"a":2,"x":"Real size = image size ÷ magnification. 6 mm = 6000 μm, and 6000 ÷ 200 = 30 μm.","w":["Multiplied by 200 instead of dividing: that gives the drawn size, not the real one.","Slipped on units: 6 mm is 6000 μm, not 600 μm, before dividing by 200.","","Divided by 20, not 200, or used 60 000 μm for 6 mm instead of 6000 μm."]}
{"id":"gbi-002","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1.6","lv":3,"q":"Paper discs soaked in antibiotics A and B are placed on a bacterial lawn. The clear zones are 8 mm (A) and 12 mm (B) across. Compared with A, the area of B's clear zone is...","o":["2.25 times larger","1.5 times larger","3 times larger","4 times larger"],"a":0,"x":"Area depends on the diameter squared: (12 ÷ 8)² = 2.25. A bigger clear zone means the antibiotic killed more bacteria, so B is more effective.","w":["","Used the ratio of diameters, 12 ÷ 8, but area needs that ratio squared.","Doubled the diameter ratio instead of squaring it.","Used the 4 mm difference in diameter as if it were the area ratio."]}
{"id":"gbi-003","s":"biology","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Which event happens in the cell cycle before mitosis starts?","o":["The chromosomes separate and move to opposite ends","The cell copies its DNA and makes more organelles","The cell membrane pinches in to make two cells","The nucleus is removed from the cell"],"a":1,"x":"Before mitosis the cell grows, copies its DNA and makes more organelles. The chromosomes only separate during mitosis itself.","w":["Chromosomes separate during mitosis (anaphase), not before it starts.","","Cytoplasm and membrane divide after mitosis, at the end of the cell cycle.","The nucleus is not removed: its membrane breaks down in mitosis, then reforms."]}
{"id":"gbi-004","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A cube of side 1 cm and a cube of side 2 cm are compared. Which row gives their surface area to volume ratios?","o":["1 cm cube 3 : 1, 2 cm cube 6 : 1","1 cm cube 6 : 1, 2 cm cube 12 : 1","1 cm cube 1 : 1, 2 cm cube 4 : 1","1 cm cube 6 : 1, 2 cm cube 3 : 1"],"a":3,"x":"1 cm cube: 6 cm² ÷ 1 cm³ = 6. 2 cm cube: 24 cm² ÷ 8 cm³ = 3. Smaller objects have a bigger ratio, so diffusion is faster for their volume.","w":["Swapped the ratios: the bigger cube has the smaller surface area to volume ratio.","Thought the ratio doubles with side length; volume grows faster than area.","Used one face only: a cube has 6 faces, so the 1 cm cube is 6 cm² : 1 cm³.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-005","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Potato cylinders gained 3% in mass in 0.2 mol/dm³ sucrose solution and lost 2% in 0.4 mol/dm³. What is the best estimate of the concentration that gives no change in mass?","o":["0.1 mol/dm³","0.5 mol/dm³","0.3 mol/dm³","0.7 mol/dm³"],"a":2,"x":"The change falls by 5 percentage points (+3% to −2%) from 0.2 to 0.4 mol/dm³. Zero is 3/5 of the way: 0.2 + 0.6 × 0.2 = 0.32 mol/dm³, so about 0.3. There is no net osmosis there.","w":["Too low: the potato still gains mass at 0.2, so the balance point is above it.","Too high: the potato already loses mass at 0.4, so zero change is below that.","","Far too high: it is well beyond 0.4, where the potato loses mass."]}
{"id":"gbi-006","s":"biology","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"The stomach is made of muscle, glandular and epithelial tissues that work together. What level of organisation is the stomach?","o":["A tissue","An organ system","A cell","An organ"],"a":3,"x":"An organ is made of several different tissues working together. Several organs form an organ system, such as the digestive system.","w":["A tissue is a group of similar cells; the stomach has several tissues.","An organ system is a group of organs, like the whole digestive system.","A cell is the smallest unit; the stomach is far bigger and has many cell types.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-007","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student tests how pH affects amylase digesting starch by adding iodine solution to samples. Which result shows the starch has been digested?","o":["The iodine turns blue-black","The iodine turns brick-red","The iodine turns purple","The iodine stays orange-brown"],"a":3,"x":"Iodine turns blue-black with starch. If it stays orange-brown there is no starch left, so the amylase has broken it down.","w":["Blue-black means starch is still there, so it has not been digested.","Brick-red is the Benedict's test colour for sugars, not iodine.","Purple is the biuret test for protein, not iodine for starch.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-008","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which blood vessel carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart?","o":["The pulmonary artery","The aorta","The pulmonary vein","The vena cava"],"a":2,"x":"The pulmonary vein brings oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium. The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs.","w":["The pulmonary artery takes deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs.","The aorta carries oxygenated blood out of the heart to the body, not from the lungs.","","The vena cava brings deoxygenated blood from the body to the heart."]}
{"id":"gbi-009","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Mature red blood cells have no nucleus. How does this help them?","o":["There is more room for haemoglobin to carry oxygen","They can divide more quickly to replace lost cells","They can change shape to engulf and digest pathogens","They can make antibodies against pathogens"],"a":0,"x":"With no nucleus, a red blood cell has more space for haemoglobin, which carries oxygen. Engulfing pathogens and making antibodies are jobs of white cells.","w":["","Red blood cells don't divide; new ones are made in bone marrow.","Engulfing pathogens is phagocytosis by white cells, not red blood cells.","Antibodies are made by white blood cells (lymphocytes), not red cells."]}
{"id":"gbi-010","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"What makes a tumour malignant?","o":["Its cells grow but stay in one place inside a membrane","Its cells have stopped dividing and will soon die","It is always caused by a virus infecting the cells","Its cells can invade tissues and spread in the blood"],"a":3,"x":"Malignant tumours are cancers: cells invade neighbouring tissues and spread in the blood to form secondary tumours. Benign tumours stay in one place.","w":["That describes a benign tumour, which stays in one place.","Cancer cells keep dividing uncontrollably; they do not stop and die.","Cancers have many causes, such as UV and smoking, not always a virus.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-011","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"On a hot, dry day a plant's guard cells lose water. What happens to the stomata and to water loss by transpiration?","o":["Stomata open wider, so more water is lost","Stomata close, so more water is lost","Stomata close, so less water is lost","Stomata open wider, so less water is lost"],"a":2,"x":"When guard cells lose water they become flaccid and the stomata close. This reduces water vapour escaping, so transpiration falls.","w":["Stomata open when guard cells are turgid; water loss makes them flaccid.","Closed stomata cut water loss, so 'more water lost' contradicts it.","","Water loss makes stomata close, not open, and open ones lose more water."]}
{"id":"gbi-012","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"How do viruses make us feel ill?","o":["They release toxins while living outside cells","They reproduce inside body cells and damage them","They use up the oxygen in the blood","They form a layer over the skin"],"a":1,"x":"Viruses live and reproduce inside cells, which damages the cells. Bacteria are the pathogens that often release toxins.","w":["Viruses can't live outside cells; toxins are mainly a bacterial problem.","","Viruses don't use up blood oxygen; they damage the cells they infect.","Viruses invade cells; they don't form a layer over the skin."]}
{"id":"gbi-013","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Malaria is spread by an insect vector. Which row is correct for malaria?","o":["Protist, spread by mosquitoes","Virus, spread by mosquitoes","Protist, spread by contaminated water","Bacterium, spread by flies"],"a":0,"x":"Malaria is caused by a protist. Mosquitoes carry it from one person to another when they feed on blood. Mosquito nets reduce its spread.","w":["","Mosquito is right, but malaria is caused by a protist, not a virus.","Protist is right, but it spreads by mosquito bites, not water.","Malaria isn't bacterial and flies aren't the vector; it's a protist and mosquitoes."]}
{"id":"gbi-014","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"What is the job of antitoxins made by white blood cells?","o":["To engulf and digest pathogens","To neutralise toxins released by pathogens","To help blood to clot at a wound","To kill pathogens by dissolving their cell walls"],"a":1,"x":"Antitoxins counteract the toxins that bacteria release. Engulfing is done by phagocytes, and antibodies bind to antigens on pathogens.","w":["That's phagocytosis, done by phagocytes, not antitoxins.","","Clotting is done by platelets, not antitoxins.","Antitoxins act on toxins, not by dissolving pathogen cell walls."]}
{"id":"gbi-015","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"Most people in a town are vaccinated against measles. Why are unvaccinated people also less likely to catch it?","o":["Vaccinated people pass their antibodies on by contact","The vaccine spreads through the air to other people","Unvaccinated people make antibodies when near vaccinated people","The pathogen has few people to spread to, so spread is reduced"],"a":3,"x":"This is herd immunity. When a large proportion is immune, the pathogen finds it hard to pass from person to person, which protects those who are not immune.","w":["Antibodies aren't passed on by contact; each person makes their own.","Vaccines are injected; they don't spread through the air.","Standing near vaccinated people doesn't make you produce antibodies.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-016","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":1,"q":"Why are antibiotics no use for treating a cold?","o":["Colds are caused by bacteria that are already resistant to them","Antibiotics only work against fungi, not other microbes","Colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics do not kill","Antibiotics only ease symptoms such as pain and fever"],"a":2,"x":"Antibiotics kill bacteria but do not work against viruses. Viruses live inside cells, so treatments must not damage the body's own cells.","w":["Colds aren't bacterial, so resistance isn't the reason.","Antibiotics target bacteria, not fungi; the real issue is that colds are viral.","","Easing symptoms is what painkillers do; antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses."]}
{"id":"gbi-017","s":"biology","t":"4.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is a chemical defence of plants against pests and microbes?","o":["A thick waxy cuticle covering the leaves","Layers of dead cells on the stem, such as bark","Tough cellulose walls around every cell","Making antibacterial chemicals or poisons"],"a":3,"x":"Plants make antibacterial chemicals and poisons to deter herbivores. Cell walls, waxy cuticles and bark are physical defences.","w":["A waxy cuticle is a physical barrier, not a chemical one.","Bark is a physical barrier made of dead cells.","Cellulose cell walls are a physical defence.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-018","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Plants use glucose and nitrate ions from the soil to make which substances?","o":["Amino acids, used to make proteins","Starch, used as an energy store","Cellulose, to strengthen cell walls","Fats and oils, used for storage"],"a":0,"x":"Glucose supplies carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. Nitrate ions supply nitrogen so that plants can make amino acids and then proteins.","w":["","Starch is made from glucose alone; no nitrogen is needed.","Cellulose is made from glucose only, so nitrate isn't needed.","Fats and oils contain no nitrogen, so nitrate isn't needed."]}
{"id":"gbi-019","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":3,"q":"On a graph of photosynthesis rate against light intensity, the rate levels off at high light intensity. Which factor could be limiting the rate at the plateau?","o":["The light intensity reaching the leaf","The volume of oxygen released","The amount of oxygen in the air","The carbon dioxide concentration"],"a":3,"x":"At the plateau, more light no longer increases the rate, so another factor is limiting. This could be carbon dioxide concentration or temperature.","w":["At the plateau more light doesn't raise the rate, so light isn't limiting.","Oxygen released is a product we measure, not a factor that limits the rate.","Oxygen is a product of photosynthesis, so it doesn't limit the rate.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-020","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Why do breathing rate and heart rate increase during exercise?","o":["To make the muscles respire anaerobically","To supply the muscles with more oxygen","To slow the rate of respiration in the muscles","To reduce the oxygen reaching the muscles"],"a":1,"x":"Active muscles respire faster. A higher heart and breathing rate supplies more oxygen and glucose and carries away carbon dioxide.","w":["The extra oxygen keeps respiration aerobic; it isn't to make it anaerobic.","","Respiration speeds up in exercise; it doesn't slow down.","Muscles need more oxygen, not less; the faster rates deliver it."]}
{"id":"gbi-021","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Each lipid molecule is built from which smaller molecules?","o":["Three glycerol and one fatty acid","One glycerol and three fatty acids","Many amino acids joined together","Many glucose molecules joined together"],"a":1,"x":"Lipids are made when one glycerol molecule joins to three fatty acids. This is a metabolic reaction that uses energy from respiration.","w":["Numbers swapped: it's one glycerol and three fatty acids.","","Many amino acids join to make proteins, not lipids.","Many glucose molecules make starch or glycogen, not lipids."]}
{"id":"gbi-022","s":"biology","t":"4.5.1","lv":1,"q":"In a control system that keeps a condition steady, what does an effector do?","o":["Carries out a response to restore the condition","Detects the change and sends information onwards","Receives information and coordinates the response","Carries nerve impulses from receptors to the brain"],"a":0,"x":"Receptors detect stimuli, coordination centres process the information, and effectors (muscles or glands) carry out the response that returns the condition to normal.","w":["","That's the receptor's job: detecting the change.","That's the coordination centre, such as the brain or spinal cord.","That's the job of a sensory neurone, not the effector."]}
{"id":"gbi-023","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student measures a person's reaction time by catching a falling ruler. What should the student do to reduce the effect of random errors?","o":["Repeat the test several times and calculate a mean","Drop the ruler from a greater height each time","Use a different person for each repeat of the test","Tell the person just before each time the ruler drops"],"a":0,"x":"Taking a mean of several repeats reduces the effect of random errors. Using different people or warning them first would bring in other variables, so the test would no longer be fair.","w":["","Changing the height adds a new variable; it doesn't reduce random error.","A different person each time adds a variable; it doesn't average out errors.","Warning the person changes the reaction time and makes the test unfair."]}
{"id":"gbi-024","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A person looks from a distant object to a book held close. What happens in the eye?","o":["Ciliary muscles relax; ligaments tighten; lens becomes thinner","Ciliary muscles contract; ligaments tighten; lens becomes thinner","Ciliary muscles contract; ligaments slacken; lens becomes thicker","Ciliary muscles relax; ligaments slacken; lens becomes thicker"],"a":2,"x":"For near objects the ciliary muscles contract, the suspensory ligaments slacken and the lens becomes thicker (more curved), so it refracts light more strongly.","w":["That's for distant objects: relaxed muscles, tight ligaments, thin lens.","Contracting muscles slacken the ligaments, so the lens gets thicker, not thinner.","","Relaxed ciliary muscles make the ligaments tight; for near vision they contract."]}
{"id":"gbi-025","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A person is in a cold room. Which pair of responses happens?","o":["Sweating and blood vessels near the skin dilate","Shivering and blood vessels near the skin constrict","Shivering and blood vessels near the skin dilate","Sweating and blood vessels near the skin constrict"],"a":1,"x":"When cold, muscles contract in shivering to release heat by respiration, and skin blood vessels constrict so less heat is lost from the skin.","w":["Both are cooling responses; the room is cold, so they are the wrong way round.","","Shivering is right, but dilated vessels would lose even more heat in the cold.","Constriction is right, but sweating cools you down, so it is for hot conditions."]}
{"id":"gbi-026","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":3,"q":"After a meal a patient's blood glucose stays high even though the pancreas makes normal insulin. What is the most likely cause?","o":["Body cells no longer respond properly to insulin","The pancreas has stopped making any insulin","The liver is turning too much glucose into glycogen","The kidneys are not filtering glucose out of the blood"],"a":0,"x":"This is type 2 diabetes: the pancreas still makes insulin but the body cells respond poorly, so glucose is not taken up. In type 1 the pancreas makes too little insulin.","w":["","That is type 1 diabetes; here the pancreas makes normal insulin.","Turning glucose into glycogen would lower blood glucose, not keep it high.","Healthy kidneys reabsorb all filtered glucose, so they don't lower blood glucose."]}
{"id":"gbi-027","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Which substance is completely reabsorbed from the filtrate in a healthy kidney, so none is found in urine?","o":["Glucose","Urea","Excess ions","Water"],"a":0,"x":"All the glucose is reabsorbed by active transport. Urea stays in the filtrate and leaves in urine, and water and ions are only partly reabsorbed.","w":["","Urea is a waste product; it stays in the filtrate and leaves in urine.","Excess ions are only partly reabsorbed; the extra leaves in urine.","Water is only partly reabsorbed, so some is still lost in urine."]}
{"id":"gbi-028","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which hormone stimulates the release of an egg from the ovary?","o":["Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH)","Progesterone","Insulin","Luteinising hormone (LH)"],"a":3,"x":"LH, from the pituitary gland, stimulates ovulation. FSH causes an egg to mature, and progesterone helps maintain the uterus lining.","w":["FSH makes the egg mature in the follicle; it does not trigger its release.","Progesterone maintains the uterus lining; it doesn't release the egg.","Insulin controls blood glucose, not the menstrual cycle.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-029","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A shoot is lit from one side. Where does auxin build up, and what happens?","o":["Shaded side: cells elongate more, so the shoot bends towards the light","Lit side: cells elongate more, so the shoot bends away from the light","Shaded side: cells elongate less, so the shoot bends away from the light","Lit side: cells elongate less, so the shoot bends towards the light"],"a":0,"x":"Auxin moves to the shaded side of the shoot and makes those cells elongate faster. The shaded side grows longer, so the shoot bends towards the light.","w":["","Auxin collects on the shaded side, not the lit side, and shoots bend towards light.","Auxin makes cells elongate more, not less, and the shoot bends towards light.","Wrong side and wrong effect: auxin is on the shaded side and speeds elongation."]}
{"id":"gbi-030","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A human body cell has 46 chromosomes. How many chromosomes does each sperm cell have, and how many cells does meiosis produce from one cell?","o":["46 chromosomes; two identical cells","23 chromosomes; two identical cells","23 chromosomes; four genetically different cells","46 chromosomes; four genetically different cells"],"a":2,"x":"Meiosis halves the chromosome number, so gametes have 23. One cell divides twice to make four cells that are genetically different from each other.","w":["Gametes are haploid, so 23 not 46, and meiosis makes four cells, not two.","Right number, but meiosis gives four different cells; two identical is mitosis.","","Chromosomes are halved in meiosis, so sperm have 23, not 46."]}
{"id":"gbi-031","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A gene has 90 bases in its coding region. Each amino acid is coded by a sequence of three bases. How many amino acids will the protein have?","o":["30","90","270","15"],"a":0,"x":"Three bases code for one amino acid, so 90 ÷ 3 = 30 amino acids. The order of the bases decides the order of amino acids in the protein.","w":["","Used the number of bases as the number of amino acids; divide by 3.","Multiplied by 3 instead of dividing: $90 \\times 3 = 270$.","Divided by 6, but each amino acid needs 3 bases, so divide by 3."]}
{"id":"gbi-032","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":3,"q":"Two parents without cystic fibrosis have a child with the disorder. What is the chance that their next child is an unaffected carrier of the recessive allele?","o":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"a":1,"x":"Both parents must be Ff. The cross Ff × Ff gives FF, Ff, Ff and ff. Two of the four outcomes are Ff, so the chance is 2 in 4, or 50%.","w":["25% is the chance of an affected child (ff), not an unaffected carrier.","","75% is the chance of not being affected (FF or Ff), including non-carriers.","Not certain: Ff × Ff gives only two carriers out of four."]}
{"id":"gbi-033","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement best describes natural selection?","o":["Individuals change in life to suit the environment and pass on changes","People choose which individuals breed for useful features","The best-suited individuals survive, breed and pass on their alleles","Mutations only happen when the environment changes"],"a":2,"x":"Variation exists. Individuals with characteristics that suit the environment are more likely to survive and breed, so those alleles become more common.","w":["That is Lamarck's idea; changes acquired during life are not inherited.","That is selective breeding, where humans choose; natural selection has no chooser.","","Mutations are random and happen anyway; they don't wait for the environment to change."]}
{"id":"gbi-034","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which problem can be caused by selective breeding?","o":["It makes the breed far more genetically varied","Inbreeding can raise the risk of inherited diseases","It directly changes the DNA in the animals' body cells","It always lowers the yield of crops or animals"],"a":1,"x":"Selective breeding uses closely related individuals, which reduces the gene pool. Harmful recessive alleles then show up more often, causing inherited defects.","w":["It reduces genetic variation by using related individuals, not increases it.","","That is genetic engineering; selective breeding just picks parents with useful alleles.","Not always: it is usually used to raise yield, such as more milk or bigger fruit."]}
{"id":"gbi-035","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"Which shows the correct order of steps for making a genetically engineered bacterium?","o":["Insert gene into plasmid; cut out gene; put plasmid into bacterium","Cut out gene; insert it into plasmid; put plasmid into bacterium","Put plasmid into bacterium; cut out gene; insert gene","Cut out gene; put gene into bacterium; make a plasmid"],"a":1,"x":"Enzymes cut out the required gene, which is inserted into a vector such as a plasmid. The vector then carries the gene into the bacterial cell.","w":["The gene must be cut out first; you can't insert it before you have it.","","The plasmid must contain the gene before it goes into the bacterium.","The gene goes into a plasmid vector, not straight into the bacterium."]}
{"id":"gbi-036","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"In adult cell cloning, what is done to the egg cell that is used?","o":["It is fertilised by a sperm cell before it is used","Its own nucleus is kept and an adult nucleus is added","It is split into many cells before any nucleus is added","Its nucleus is swapped for one from an adult body cell"],"a":3,"x":"The egg's nucleus is removed and the nucleus of an adult body cell is inserted. An electric shock makes it divide, and the embryo is placed in a womb.","w":["The egg is not fertilised; it gets an adult nucleus instead of a sperm.","The egg's own nucleus is removed, so the clone has only the adult's DNA.","Splitting into many cells is embryo splitting, not adult cell cloning.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-037","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.1","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about giraffe necks is Darwin's idea, not Lamarck's?","o":["Giraffes stretched their necks and passed on the longer neck to offspring","All giraffes were born with exactly the same neck length","Giraffes born with longer necks survived better and passed on the feature","Giraffes grew longer necks because they wanted to reach leaves"],"a":2,"x":"Darwin proposed natural selection: variation exists and those better suited survive and breed. Lamarck suggested that characteristics gained during life are inherited.","w":["That is Lamarck's idea: features gained in life are not inherited. Darwin used selection.","Darwin said individuals vary; identical necks would give nothing to select.","","Lamarck's idea: wanting or needing a feature does not make it grow."]}
{"id":"gbi-038","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Why are fossils of soft-bodied organisms rare?","o":["Fossils can only ever form inside igneous rock","Soft-bodied organisms only ever lived on dry land","They decayed quickly, so they left few traces","Their bodies were always eaten whole by predators"],"a":2,"x":"Soft bodies decay quickly and leave few traces, and geological activity destroys many that did form. Fossils usually form in sedimentary rock.","w":["Fossils mostly form in sedimentary rock; igneous rock's heat destroys remains.","Soft-bodied organisms lived in water too; the rarity comes from fast decay.","","Not always eaten whole; the key point is that soft tissue decays quickly."]}
{"id":"gbi-039","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":2,"q":"Why does overusing antibiotics lead to more resistant bacteria?","o":["The antibiotic causes the bacteria to mutate and resist it","The bacteria learn to resist the antibiotic during treatment","Non-resistant bacteria are killed; resistant ones multiply","The patient's immune system passes resistance on to bacteria"],"a":2,"x":"Resistance arises by random mutation. Antibiotics kill the non-resistant bacteria, so the resistant ones survive and reproduce. This is natural selection.","w":["Mutations are random, not caused by the antibiotic; it only selects the survivors.","Bacteria cannot learn; resistance comes from random mutation.","","The immune system does not pass resistance on; bacteria gain it by mutation."]}
{"id":"gbi-040","s":"biology","t":"4.6.4","lv":1,"q":"The scientific name of humans is Homo sapiens. What do the two words show?","o":["The species first, then the genus","The family first, then the genus","The kingdom first, then the species","The genus first, then the species"],"a":3,"x":"The binomial system names an organism by genus (capital letter) and species. Homo is the genus and sapiens is the species.","w":["Reversed: Homo is the genus, so the genus comes first.","Family is not part of the binomial name, which uses genus and species.","Kingdom is not in the binomial name, and the order is wrong.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-041","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is an abiotic factor that affects a plant community?","o":["Grazing by rabbits","Disease caused by a fungus","Light intensity","Competition from other plants"],"a":2,"x":"Abiotic factors are non-living, such as light, temperature and moisture. Grazing, disease and competition are biotic factors.","w":["Grazing is done by living things, so it is biotic, not abiotic.","Fungal disease involves a living organism, so it is biotic.","","Competition is between living organisms, so it is biotic."]}
{"id":"gbi-042","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Five 0.25 m² quadrats on a lawn contained 6, 4, 8, 2 and 5 daisies. About how many daisies are on the whole lawn of 200 m²?","o":["1000","4000","250","20 000"],"a":1,"x":"Mean per quadrat = 25 ÷ 5 = 5. Per m² = 5 ÷ 0.25 = 20. For 200 m², 20 × 200 = 4000. Quadrats must be placed randomly to avoid bias.","w":["Multiplied the mean 5 by 200 and ignored the 0.25 m² quadrat size.","","Multiplied by 0.25 instead of dividing; the quadrat area must be divided out.","Used the total 25, not the mean 5, before scaling up."]}
{"id":"gbi-043","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which process in the carbon cycle removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere?","o":["Respiration","Combustion","Decay by microorganisms","Photosynthesis"],"a":3,"x":"Plants and algae take in carbon dioxide for photosynthesis. Respiration, combustion and decay all return carbon dioxide to the air.","w":["Respiration releases CO₂ into the air rather than removing it.","Combustion releases CO₂ by burning fuels; it does not remove it.","Decay microbes respire and release CO₂; they do not take it in.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-044","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which conditions make food waste decay fastest in a compost heap?","o":["Warm, damp, with plenty of air","Warm, damp, with no air","Cold, damp, with plenty of air","Warm, dry, with plenty of air"],"a":0,"x":"Decay microbes need warmth, water and oxygen. Cold slows their enzymes, dry conditions stop them growing and without oxygen decay is much slower.","w":["","Without air, decay microbes lack oxygen, so decay is much slower.","Cold slows the enzymes in microbes, so decay is slower.","Dry conditions stop microbes growing; they need water."]}
{"id":"gbi-045","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"What is meant by biodiversity?","o":["The total number of living organisms in a habitat","The variety of species on Earth or in an ecosystem","The number of individuals of one species in an area","The total mass of living material in an ecosystem"],"a":1,"x":"Biodiversity is the variety of species. A high biodiversity makes ecosystems more stable because species depend on each other.","w":["That is the total number of organisms; biodiversity is the variety of species.","","That is the population of one species, not the variety of species.","Biomass is the mass of living material, not the variety of species."]}
{"id":"gbi-046","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Why does cutting down large areas of forest add to global warming?","o":["Fewer trees take in carbon dioxide, and burning or rotting releases it","Cut trees release oxygen, which is a greenhouse gas","Fewer trees means less rain, so the air traps more heat","Tree stumps carry out more photosynthesis and release carbon dioxide"],"a":0,"x":"Trees remove carbon dioxide by photosynthesis. Deforestation reduces this, and burning or decay of the wood adds carbon dioxide to the air.","w":["","Oxygen is not a greenhouse gas; the problem is extra CO₂.","Less rain is not the main link; fewer trees absorb less CO₂.","Stumps do little photosynthesis; the CO₂ comes from burning and decay."]}
{"id":"gbi-047","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.6","lv":1,"q":"Which action helps to maintain biodiversity?","o":["Removing hedgerows to make larger fields for crops","Breeding programmes for endangered species","Draining peat bogs to sell the peat as compost","Spreading more fertiliser on grassland and meadows"],"a":1,"x":"Breeding programmes, protecting rare habitats and reducing deforestation help to conserve species. The other actions destroy habitats.","w":["Removing hedgerows destroys habitats, so it lowers biodiversity.","","Draining peat bogs destroys habitats and releases CO₂.","Extra fertiliser lets a few plants dominate and cuts species variety."]}
{"id":"gbi-048","s":"biology","t":"4.7.4.1","lv":1,"q":"A field of grass has 5000 kg of biomass. The rabbits that eat it gain 400 kg of biomass. What percentage of the biomass was transferred?","o":["12.5%","8%","92%","80%"],"a":1,"x":"Efficiency = biomass transferred ÷ biomass available × 100 = 400 ÷ 5000 × 100 = 8%. The rest is lost as waste, heat from respiration and uneaten parts.","w":["Divided 5000 by 400, the wrong way round; divide transferred by available.","","That is 100 − 8, the percentage not transferred.","Decimal slip: 400 ÷ 5000 is 0.08, so 8%, not 80%."]}
{"id":"gbi-049","s":"biology","t":"4.7.5.1","lv":2,"q":"How do fishing quotas help to keep fish stocks sustainable?","o":["They only allow young fish to be caught and sold","They make the fish grow faster and breed more often","They stop all fishing in the sea for several years","They limit the catch so enough fish survive to breed"],"a":3,"x":"Quotas cap the catch so enough fish survive to breed and the population is maintained. Large mesh nets also let young fish escape.","w":["Backwards; catching only young fish stops them breeding. Quotas cap the total catch.","Confuses catch limits with changing biology; quotas do not affect growth or breeding.","Too extreme; quotas limit catches, they do not ban all fishing for years.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-050","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.7","lv":3,"q":"A person's blood thyroxine level falls below normal. Which describes the negative feedback response?","o":["The thyroid releases more thyroxine until the level is normal","The thyroid releases less thyroxine until the level falls further","The pancreas releases insulin to raise the thyroxine level","The adrenal glands release adrenaline to stop thyroxine release"],"a":0,"x":"Negative feedback reverses a change. A low thyroxine level stimulates the thyroid gland to release more, and a high level reduces its release.","w":["","Describes positive feedback; the change gets bigger instead of being reversed.","Wrong hormone; insulin controls blood glucose, not thyroxine level.","Wrong hormone; adrenaline is for fight or flight and does not control thyroxine."]}
{"id":"gbi-051","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"In the onion cell practical, why should you start with the lowest-power objective lens?","o":["It gives the highest magnification, so cells are clear straight away","It gives a wide view, so cells are easy to find and the lens is less likely to hit the slide","It stops the stain from fading under the lamp","It means you do not need to use the focus knobs"],"a":1,"x":"The lowest-power lens has the widest field of view, so you find the cells first. Then you switch to a higher power. The trap is thinking low power means the clearest image.","w":["Low power gives the lowest magnification, not the highest. Higher powers come after finding the cells.","","The lens power has no effect on how the stain fades. This is an invented reason.","You still focus at every power. Changing lens does not remove the need to focus."]}
{"id":"gbi-052","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Why can an electron microscope show ribosomes when a light microscope cannot?","o":["It has a higher resolving power, so it shows finer detail","It uses light with a longer wavelength","It can only be used on living cells","It has a wider field of view"],"a":0,"x":"Electron microscopes have a much higher resolving power, so they show tiny structures like ribosomes as separate, clear objects. The trap is thinking it only makes the image bigger.","w":["","Longer wavelengths give poorer detail. Electron beams have a shorter wavelength than light.","Electron microscope specimens are usually dead because they are placed in a vacuum. Living cells are not needed.","Field of view is not the reason. Seeing small detail depends on resolving power."]}
{"id":"gbi-053","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Which job matches a xylem cell, and which feature helps it do that job?","o":["Carries sugars; has sieve plates with small holes","Absorbs water from soil; has a long hair-like extension","Makes sugars; has many chloroplasts","Carries water up the plant; has lignin-strengthened walls and no end walls"],"a":3,"x":"Xylem cells form hollow tubes with no end walls, so water flows through. Lignin strengthens the walls. The trap is mixing xylem up with phloem (sugars) or root hair cells (absorption).","w":["Sieve plates and sugar transport belong to phloem, not xylem. This mixes up the two tissues.","This describes a root hair cell, which absorbs water. It does not carry water up the stem.","This describes a palisade cell, which photosynthesises. Xylem cells have no chloroplasts and are not alive when mature.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-054","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A microscope has an eyepiece lens of ×10 and an objective lens of ×40. A cell is 25 μm long. How long is its image?","o":["1 mm","100 mm","10 mm","0.25 mm"],"a":2,"x":"Total magnification is 10 × 40 = ×400. Image = 25 × 400 = 10 000 μm, which is 10 mm. The traps are using only one lens and slipping on μm to mm.","w":["Used only the ×40 objective lens, so the image is 1000 μm. Both lenses must be multiplied.","Slipped on units: 10 000 μm is 10 mm, not 100 mm.","","Used only the ×10 eyepiece lens, giving 250 μm. Both lenses must be multiplied."]}
{"id":"gbi-055","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A plant cell is 100 μm long. A ribosome is 25 nm long. How many times longer is the plant cell than the ribosome? (1 μm = 1000 nm)","o":["4","4000","400","40 000"],"a":1,"x":"100 μm = 100 000 nm. Then 100 000 ÷ 25 = 4000. The trap is comparing 100 with 25 without changing to the same unit.","w":["Divided 100 by 25 without converting units. Both lengths must be in μm or both in nm.","","Used 1 μm = 100 nm, giving 10 000 nm. The correct conversion is 1000 nm.","Used 1 μm = 10 000 nm, which is too many nanometres. 1 μm = 1000 nm."]}
{"id":"gbi-061","s":"biology","t":"4.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What is a chromosome in the nucleus of a human body cell?","o":["A single gene that codes for one protein","A protein that carries messages through the cytoplasm","A long coiled molecule of DNA carrying many genes","A small organelle where respiration happens"],"a":2,"x":"A chromosome is a long, coiled DNA molecule that carries many genes. Do not mix it up with a gene, which is just one short section of a chromosome.","w":["A gene is only a short section of a chromosome, so this confuses the part with the whole.","A chromosome is made of DNA and stays in the nucleus, so it is not a messenger protein.","","Respiration happens in mitochondria, and a chromosome is not an organelle."]}
{"id":"gbi-062","s":"biology","t":"4.1.2","lv":1,"q":"In a plant, where are stem cells found?","o":["In meristems at the tips of roots and shoots","In the guard cells around the stomata","In the xylem vessels that carry water","In the petals of the flower"],"a":0,"x":"Plant stem cells are found in meristem tissue, at the tips of roots and shoots. They can differentiate into any plant cell type throughout the plant's life. Specialised cells like xylem cannot.","w":["","Guard cells are already specialised for opening and closing stomata, so they are not stem cells.","Xylem vessels are dead, specialised cells, so this confuses a specialised cell with a stem cell.","Petals are made of differentiated cells, and only meristems keep stem cells for growth."]}
{"id":"gbi-063","s":"biology","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Doctors want to treat a patient with stem cells grown in a lab. Which is a risk of using these stem cells?","o":["Stem cells can only ever make one type of cell","Stem cells cannot divide outside the body","Stem cells would destroy the patient's own chromosomes","Viruses in the stem cells could pass to the patient"],"a":3,"x":"Stem cells grown in a lab may carry a virus. If they are transferred to the patient, the virus could spread and cause disease. The trap is thinking stem cells are limited to one cell type. They are not.","w":["Stem cells can become many cell types, which is why they are useful in medicine.","Stem cells divide by mitosis in a lab, which is how they are produced for treatment.","Stem cells do not attack chromosomes, so this is an invented risk.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-064","s":"biology","t":"4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A human skin cell with 46 chromosomes divides by mitosis. How many chromosomes does each new cell have, and how do the new cells compare?","o":["23 chromosomes, and the cells are different from each other","46 chromosomes, and the cells are genetically identical","92 chromosomes, and the cells are genetically identical","46 chromosomes, and the cells are genetically different"],"a":1,"x":"The DNA is copied, then shared out equally. Each new cell has 46 chromosomes and is genetically identical to the parent. The trap is halving the number, which happens in making gametes, not in mitosis.","w":["Halving to 23 happens when gametes are made, but mitosis keeps the full number.","","The DNA is copied before division, but the copies are shared between two cells, so each gets 46.","Mitosis makes identical copies, so the new cells are not genetically different."]}
{"id":"gbi-065","s":"biology","t":"4.1.2","lv":3,"q":"A patient needs new heart tissue. Why might doctors choose therapeutic cloning instead of using stem cells from an unrelated donor embryo?","o":["The cloned embryo's cells are already specialised heart cells","The cloned embryo's cells divide by meiosis, so they make more cells","The embryo has the patient's genes, so cells are less likely to be rejected","The cloned cells carry no risks and raise no ethical objections"],"a":2,"x":"In therapeutic cloning the embryo is made with the same genes as the patient, so its stem cells are not rejected by the body. Ethical objections to using embryos still exist, so do not say there are none.","w":["Embryonic stem cells are unspecialised and must be made to differentiate into heart cells.","Stem cells divide by mitosis to make more cells, whereas meiosis makes gametes.","","Some people still object to using embryos, and viral infection remains a possible risk."]}
{"id":"gbi-067","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"A root hair cell takes in nitrate ions from soil. The nitrate concentration is higher inside the cell than in the soil. Which process moves the ions in?","o":["Diffusion, because ions always move into cells","Osmosis, because water moves with the ions","Diffusion, because the cell membrane is permeable to ions","Active transport, because ions move against the concentration gradient"],"a":3,"x":"The ions move from low concentration (soil) to high concentration (cell). That is against the gradient, so it is active transport and needs energy from respiration. The trap is thinking all uptake is diffusion.","w":["Ions do not always move in; diffusion only goes down a gradient, and here that gradient points out of the cell.","Osmosis is the movement of water only, so it cannot explain ions moving into the cell.","Being permeable allows diffusion only down a gradient, but these ions move up the gradient, so it is not diffusion.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-068","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Cell A has an oxygen concentration of 2 units. Cell B has 6 units. Both sit in surroundings of 10 units, and are the same size and temperature. Which statement is correct?","o":["Oxygen diffuses faster into cell A, as its concentration difference is 8, not 4","Oxygen diffuses faster into cell B, because it already has more oxygen inside","Oxygen diffuses in at the same rate, because the outside concentration is the same","Oxygen moves out of cell A, because cell A has the lower concentration"],"a":0,"x":"Rate depends on the concentration difference. Cell A: 10 − 2 = 8 units. Cell B: 10 − 6 = 4 units. The steeper gradient gives faster diffusion into A. The trap is looking at the outside concentration only.","w":["","A cell with more oxygen inside has a smaller difference, so diffusion into it is slower, not faster.","The inside concentration also affects the gradient; looking at only one side of the membrane is the slip.","Oxygen moves from high to low concentration, so it moves into cell A; reversing the gradient's direction is the slip."]}
{"id":"gbi-069","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A plant stands in waterlogged soil with almost no oxygen. Its uptake of mineral ions slows down. Which is the best explanation?","o":["Oxygen is needed to carry the ions into the root hair cell","Ions diffuse more slowly because the soil water is colder","Less aerobic respiration means less energy for active transport of the ions","Active transport stops because the ions now move down the concentration gradient"],"a":2,"x":"Mineral ions are taken up by active transport, which needs energy from respiration. With little oxygen, aerobic respiration releases less energy, so uptake slows. The trap is thinking oxygen carries the ions.","w":["Oxygen is used in respiration to release energy; thinking it carries ions across the membrane is the slip.","Waterlogging need not mean colder soil, and ion uptake is active transport, not diffusion.","","Waterlogging does not reverse the gradient; the ions still move against it, and it is energy that is missing."]}
{"id":"gbi-070","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":3,"q":"Cells lining the small intestine have many mitochondria. Glucose is at 1 unit in the gut and 4 units inside these cells. How is glucose absorbed, and why do mitochondria help?","o":["Diffusion down the gradient; mitochondria make glucose to raise the concentration","Active transport against the gradient; mitochondria release energy by respiration","Active transport against the gradient; mitochondria store the glucose for the cell","Diffusion into the cell; mitochondria release energy to speed the diffusion up"],"a":1,"x":"Glucose moves from 1 unit to 4 units, which is against the gradient, so it is active transport. Active transport needs energy, and mitochondria are where aerobic respiration releases it. The trap is thinking diffusion needs energy.","w":["The gradient points out of the cell, so this is not diffusion, and mitochondria release energy rather than make glucose.","","The process is right, but mitochondria do not store glucose; they release energy by respiration for active transport.","Moving to a higher concentration is not diffusion, and diffusion is passive so needs no energy from mitochondria."]}
{"id":"gbi-071","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes osmosis?","o":["Solute particles move from high to low concentration across a membrane","Water moves from dilute to concentrated solution via a partially permeable membrane","Water moves from concentrated to dilute solution via a partially permeable membrane","Water moves against a concentration gradient using energy from respiration"],"a":1,"x":"Osmosis is the net movement of water from a dilute solution (high water concentration) to a more concentrated solution (low water concentration) across a partially permeable membrane. The trap is reversing the direction or confusing it with other processes.","w":["This describes diffusion of solute particles, not osmosis, which is the movement of water.","","The direction is reversed. Water moves towards the more concentrated solution, not away from it.","This describes active transport, which needs energy. Osmosis is passive and needs no energy from respiration."]}
{"id":"gbi-072","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"In the osmosis required practical, why are potato cylinders blotted dry before they are weighed?","o":["To stop osmosis carrying on once the cylinders are removed","To make the cylinders lose water so they weigh less","To kill the cells so the results are fairer","To remove water on the surface so only the mass of the potato is measured"],"a":3,"x":"Water left on the outside of the cylinder would add to its mass and make the result too high. Blotting dry means the mass change comes only from water that moved in or out of the cells by osmosis.","w":["Blotting does not affect osmosis; its purpose is to remove surface water that would add extra mass.","Blotting removes only surface water, not water from inside the cells. It is not meant to change the cells' water.","Killing cells is not the aim. Dead cells would not give a valid test of osmosis in the tissue.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-073","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A potato cylinder has a mass of 2.50 g at the start. After 30 minutes in a sucrose solution its mass is 2.15 g. What is the percentage change in mass?","o":["−14%","+14%","−16%","−0.35%"],"a":0,"x":"Change = 2.15 − 2.50 = −0.35 g. Percentage change = −0.35 ÷ 2.50 × 100 = −14%. The trap is dividing by the final mass instead of the starting mass, or forgetting the minus sign.","w":["","The mass went down, so the change is negative. Leaving out the minus sign gives the wrong sign.","This divides by the final mass (2.15 g). Percentage change must be divided by the starting mass.","This is the change in grams. It was not divided by the starting mass or multiplied by 100."]}
{"id":"gbi-074","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"An onion cell is placed in pure water. The solution outside is more dilute than the cell contents. What happens to the cell?","o":["It bursts, because the cell wall lets too much water in","It becomes flaccid, because water leaves the vacuole","It becomes turgid, because water enters and pushes on the cell wall","It stays the same, because plant cell membranes stop water moving"],"a":2,"x":"Water moves into the cell by osmosis, as the outside is more dilute. The vacuole swells and pushes on the strong cell wall, so the cell becomes turgid. The trap is thinking it bursts like an animal cell.","w":["The cell wall is strong and stops the cell bursting. Bursting is a risk for animal cells, which have no wall.","Water enters, not leaves, because the outside is more dilute. Flaccid cells lose water in concentrated solutions.","","Cell membranes are partially permeable, so water does move across them by osmosis."]}
{"id":"gbi-075","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Two potato cylinders were left for the same time in different sucrose solutions. P: 2.0 g to 2.3 g. Q: 5.0 g to 5.4 g. Which solution is more dilute?","o":["Q, because its cylinder gained more mass in grams","P, because its cylinder gained a greater percentage of its mass","Both are equally dilute, because both cylinders gained mass","It cannot be told, because the cylinders had different starting masses"],"a":1,"x":"Percentage change allows a fair comparison. P gained 0.3 ÷ 2.0 × 100 = 15%. Q gained 0.4 ÷ 5.0 × 100 = 8%. The bigger percentage gain shows more net water entering, so P is more dilute. The trap is comparing grams.","w":["Q gained more grams, but it started heavier. Comparing raw mass changes ignores the different starting masses.","","Both gained mass, but the amounts differ. A bigger percentage gain means a bigger difference in water concentration.","Converting to percentage change removes the effect of different starting masses, so a comparison can be made."]}
{"id":"gbi-076","s":"biology","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes a tissue?","o":["A single cell that has become specialised for one job","A group of different organs that work together","A group of similar cells that work together to do a job","A group of different tissues that work together to do a job"],"a":2,"x":"A tissue is a group of similar cells with the same job. A group of different tissues working together is an organ, not a tissue. That is the common trap.","w":["A tissue is a group of cells, not one cell, even a specialised one.","This describes an organ system, so the levels of organisation have been mixed up.","","This describes an organ, which is bigger than a tissue and has several tissue types."]}
{"id":"gbi-077","s":"biology","t":"4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which list shows the levels of organisation in a living thing in order, from smallest to largest?","o":["Cell, tissue, organ, organ system, organism","Cell, organ, tissue, organ system, organism","Tissue, cell, organ, organism, organ system","Cell, tissue, organ system, organ, organism"],"a":0,"x":"Cells make tissues, tissues make organs, organs make organ systems, and organ systems make the organism. The common slip is swapping organ and organ system.","w":["","Tissues are made of cells and organs are made of tissues, so tissue comes before organ.","Tissues are made of cells, so cell comes first, and the organism is the largest level.","An organ system is made of several organs, so it comes after organ, not before."]}
{"id":"gbi-078","s":"biology","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The stomach, liver, pancreas and small intestine work together to digest and absorb food. Which statement about them is correct?","o":["Together they form one organ made of four tissues","Each one is a tissue, and together they form the digestive organ","Each one is an organ system, and together they form an organism","Each one is an organ, and together they form the digestive system"],"a":3,"x":"Each of these is an organ made of several tissues. Organs that work together to do a job form an organ system, here the digestive system. Do not call a group of organs an organ.","w":["Each of these is already an organ, so they cannot be four tissues in one organ.","Each is made of several different tissues, so each is an organ, not a tissue.","These are single organs, not organ systems, and together they make one system, not a whole organism.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-080","s":"biology","t":"4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"The pancreas has glandular tissue that makes digestive juices. A student says, \"So the pancreas is a tissue.\" Which reply is best?","o":["Correct, because tissues are the parts of the body that make juices","Wrong, because it is an organ system, as it is part of the digestive system","Wrong, it is an organ, made of glandular and other tissues working together","Wrong, because it is a single large specialised cell that makes juices"],"a":2,"x":"The pancreas is an organ because it is made of different tissues working together. It is one organ in the digestive system. Having one important tissue does not make it a tissue.","w":["A tissue is a group of similar cells, and making juices is only one tissue's job, not the definition.","The digestive system is the organ system, so this confuses the pancreas, one part, with the whole system.","","The pancreas is made of many cells, not one, because a single cell is the smallest level."]}
{"id":"gbi-081","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Bile helps with digestion. Where is bile stored before it is released into the small intestine?","o":["Liver","Gall bladder","Pancreas","Stomach"],"a":1,"x":"Bile is made in the liver but stored in the gall bladder. It is then released into the small intestine. The trap is thinking it is stored where it is made.","w":["The liver makes bile but does not store it, so this mixes up where bile is made with where it is stored.","","The pancreas makes digestive enzymes such as amylase, protease and lipase, not bile.","The stomach makes protease and hydrochloric acid, and bile is not stored there."]}
{"id":"gbi-082","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Lipase is an enzyme in the digestive system. Which products does lipase make when it digests lipids (fats)?","o":["Amino acids","Glucose","Glycerol and fatty acids","Maltose"],"a":2,"x":"Lipase breaks lipids down into glycerol and fatty acids. Amino acids come from protein and glucose comes from starch, so do not mix up the enzymes and their substrates.","w":["Amino acids are the products of protein digestion by protease, not lipid digestion.","Glucose comes from carbohydrates being broken down by carbohydrase enzymes, not from lipids.","","Maltose is a sugar made when amylase digests starch, not a product of lipid digestion."]}
{"id":"gbi-083","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student heats a food sample with Benedict's solution. It stays blue. The student adds biuret solution to another sample of the same food. It turns purple. What does the food contain?","o":["Protein but no reducing sugar","Reducing sugar but no protein","Both protein and reducing sugar","Neither protein nor reducing sugar"],"a":0,"x":"Benedict's solution staying blue means no reducing sugar. Biuret turning purple means protein is present. The trap is swapping which test shows which food type.","w":["","This swaps the two tests: Benedict's staying blue shows no sugar, and biuret turning purple shows protein.","Benedict's solution stayed blue, so there is no reducing sugar; only the biuret result was positive.","Purple with biuret is a positive test for protein, so the food does contain protein."]}
{"id":"gbi-084","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Bile is made in the liver. How does bile help lipase to digest fat in the small intestine?","o":["It is an enzyme that breaks fat into glycerol and fatty acids","It is acidic, so it gives lipase the low pH it needs","It is made in the pancreas and carries lipase to the fat","It breaks fat into small droplets, giving a larger surface area"],"a":3,"x":"Bile emulsifies fat, breaking it into tiny droplets. This gives a larger surface area for lipase to work on. Bile is not an enzyme. It is also alkaline, not acidic.","w":["Bile is not an enzyme and does not chemically digest fat, so this confuses bile with lipase.","Bile is alkaline and neutralises stomach acid, so calling it acidic reverses its property.","Bile is made in the liver, not the pancreas, and it does not carry enzymes to the fat.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-085","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Amylase digests starch at three pH values. Time for the starch to disappear: pH 5 = 180 s, pH 7 = 45 s, pH 9 = 90 s. How many times faster is the rate at pH 7 than at pH 5?","o":["135 times faster","4 times slower","4 times faster","2 times faster"],"a":2,"x":"Rate is proportional to 1 ÷ time. 180 ÷ 45 = 4, so the rate at pH 7 is 4 times faster. The trap is thinking a shorter time means a slower rate, or subtracting the times.","w":["135 is the difference between the times (180 − 45), but rates are compared by dividing, not subtracting.","A shorter time means a faster rate, so saying slower inverts the comparison.","","2 times faster compares pH 7 with pH 9 (90 ÷ 45), but the question asks about pH 5."]}
{"id":"gbi-086","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Veins have valves inside them. What is the job of these valves?","o":["To make the blood flow faster","To stop the blood flowing backwards","To let oxygen pass into the tissues","To stop the walls bursting under high pressure"],"a":1,"x":"Blood in veins is at low pressure. Valves close if blood tries to flow backwards, so it keeps moving towards the heart. They do not speed up the blood or help gas exchange.","w":["Valves control the direction of flow, not its speed, so this confuses direction with speed.","","Oxygen diffuses out through thin capillary walls, so valves play no part in gas exchange.","Blood in veins is at low pressure; thick walls that resist high pressure are a feature of arteries."]}
{"id":"gbi-087","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which blood vessels supply the muscle of the heart wall itself with oxygen?","o":["The coronary arteries","The pulmonary veins","The vena cava","The pulmonary arteries"],"a":0,"x":"The coronary arteries run over the surface of the heart and supply the heart muscle with oxygenated blood. Blood passing through the chambers does not feed the muscle wall.","w":["","Pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs to the left atrium, and blood inside the chambers does not feed the heart wall.","The vena cava carries deoxygenated blood from the body into the right atrium, not to the heart muscle.","The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs, not to the heart muscle."]}
{"id":"gbi-088","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Blood returns from the body to the heart in the vena cava. Which list shows the correct path it takes next?","o":["Left atrium, left ventricle, lungs, right atrium, right ventricle, aorta","Right ventricle, right atrium, lungs, left ventricle, left atrium, aorta","Right atrium, left atrium, left ventricle, right ventricle, lungs, aorta","Right atrium, right ventricle, lungs, left atrium, left ventricle, aorta"],"a":3,"x":"Deoxygenated blood enters the right atrium, then the right ventricle. It goes to the lungs, returns to the left atrium, then the left ventricle, and leaves in the aorta. Atria receive blood; ventricles pump it out.","w":["This starts on the left side, but blood from the vena cava enters the right atrium first.","This swaps atria and ventricles; blood enters an atrium first, then passes down to the ventricle.","This crosses between the sides without visiting the lungs, but the septum keeps the two sides separate.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-089","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"The wall of the left ventricle is much thicker than the wall of the right ventricle. What is the reason for this?","o":["The left ventricle holds a much bigger volume of blood","The left ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood, which is thicker","The left ventricle pumps blood at higher pressure to the whole body","The left ventricle must pump blood against the valves in the veins"],"a":2,"x":"The left ventricle pumps blood all round the body, so it needs a thick muscular wall to make high pressure. The right ventricle only pumps blood to the nearby lungs. Both pump the same volume per beat.","w":["Both ventricles pump about the same volume each beat, so wall thickness is about pressure, not volume.","The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood, and deoxygenated blood is not thicker anyway.","","The left ventricle pushes blood into the aorta and round the body; valves in veins do not resist it."]}
{"id":"gbi-090","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A patient's valve between the left ventricle and the aorta does not close fully. What is the most likely effect when the left ventricle relaxes?","o":["Deoxygenated blood flows from the right ventricle into the aorta","Some blood flows back from the aorta into the left ventricle","Blood flows back from the left ventricle into the right atrium","Blood flows from the lungs straight into the aorta"],"a":1,"x":"Valves stop backflow. If the valve out of the left ventricle leaks, blood leaks back from the aorta into the ventricle, so less reaches the body. Blood does not cross to the right side.","w":["The septum separates the two sides, so blood cannot pass from the right ventricle into the aorta.","","This valve guards the exit to the aorta, and the septum stops blood crossing to the right side.","Blood from the lungs enters the left atrium first, so it cannot reach the aorta directly."]}
{"id":"gbi-091","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which job is done by blood plasma?","o":["Carrying oxygen from the lungs to the body cells","Sealing a wound with a scab","Carrying urea from the liver to the kidneys","Engulfing and digesting pathogens"],"a":2,"x":"Plasma is the liquid part of blood. It carries dissolved substances such as urea, carbon dioxide, digested food and hormones. Oxygen is carried by red blood cells, not plasma.","w":["Oxygen is carried by haemoglobin in red blood cells, not by the plasma.","Clotting and scabs are done by platelets, not plasma.","","Engulfing pathogens is phagocytosis, done by white blood cells."]}
{"id":"gbi-092","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":1,"q":"What happens during phagocytosis?","o":["A white blood cell engulfs a pathogen and digests it","A red blood cell takes in oxygen at the lungs","A platelet forms a clot over a cut","A white blood cell makes chemicals that neutralise toxins"],"a":0,"x":"In phagocytosis, a white blood cell surrounds a pathogen, takes it in and digests it. Making antitoxins is a different white cell job, and clotting is done by platelets.","w":["","Taking in oxygen is the job of red blood cells and haemoglobin, not phagocytosis.","Clotting is done by platelets, which are cell fragments, not by phagocytosis.","This describes antitoxins, which are a different defence from phagocytosis."]}
{"id":"gbi-093","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Red blood cells are shaped like discs with a dip in both sides (biconcave). How does this shape help them carry oxygen?","o":["It leaves space for a large nucleus","It gives a small surface area so less oxygen is lost","It makes the cell wall thick so it cannot burst","It gives a large surface area so oxygen moves in and out quickly"],"a":3,"x":"The biconcave shape gives a large surface area compared with the cell's volume. Oxygen can diffuse in and out quickly. Red blood cells have no nucleus, so the shape is not about fitting one.","w":["Mature red blood cells have no nucleus; the shape is not for fitting one.","A small surface area would slow diffusion. The shape gives a large one.","Red blood cells are animal cells, so they have no cell wall at all.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-094","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A patient has far fewer platelets than normal. Which problem is the patient most likely to have?","o":["Feeling very tired because too little oxygen is carried","Cuts that bleed for a long time and bruising easily","Catching infections more often than other people","Blood that cannot carry dissolved urea to the kidneys"],"a":1,"x":"Platelets help blood to clot at a wound. With too few, bleeding takes longer to stop and bruises form easily. Tiredness links to red cells, and infections link to white cells.","w":["Tiredness from low oxygen transport points to too few red blood cells, not platelets.","","More infections point to too few white blood cells, not platelets.","Urea is carried by plasma, which platelets do not affect."]}
{"id":"gbi-095","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":3,"q":"After weeks at high altitude, where air has less oxygen, a climber's red blood cell count rises. How does this help the climber?","o":["More white cells can fight pathogens in thin air","Oxygen dissolves faster in the plasma","More haemoglobin to carry oxygen from the lungs","Red blood cells release oxygen into the air faster"],"a":2,"x":"Each red blood cell holds haemoglobin, which picks up oxygen in the lungs. More red cells means more haemoglobin, so more oxygen is carried even when the air has less. White cells and plasma are not the oxygen carriers.","w":["White cells defend against pathogens; they do not carry oxygen.","Plasma carries very little oxygen; red blood cells carry most of it.","","Red cells pick up oxygen at the lungs and release it in body tissues, not into the air."]}
{"id":"gbi-096","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"A patient with coronary heart disease has a stent fitted in a coronary artery. What does the stent do?","o":["Lowers the level of cholesterol in the blood","Replaces a faulty heart valve","Holds the artery open so blood can reach the heart muscle","Takes over pumping blood while the heart rests"],"a":2,"x":"A stent is a small tube that keeps a narrowed coronary artery open, so blood carrying oxygen reaches the heart muscle. The trap is mixing it up with statins, which lower cholesterol.","w":["That is what statins do. A stent is a tube that holds an artery open.","Faulty valves are replaced with biological or mechanical valves. A stent works inside a coronary artery.","","That describes an artificial heart. A stent only keeps one artery open."]}
{"id":"gbi-097","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Statins are given to some people at risk of coronary heart disease. How do statins help?","o":["They lower blood cholesterol, slowing fatty build-up in arteries","They make the heart beat harder so blood is pushed past a blockage","They remove all the fatty material already inside the arteries","They open narrowed arteries by acting as a tube inside them"],"a":0,"x":"Statins reduce blood cholesterol, so fatty material builds up in the coronary arteries more slowly. They do not clear existing deposits. Opening an artery from inside is the job of a stent.","w":["","Statins do not change how hard the heart beats. They lower cholesterol in the blood.","Statins slow new build-up but do not remove all existing fatty material, so \"all\" is the slip.","That describes a stent, not a statin. Mixing up the two treatments is the common slip."]}
{"id":"gbi-098","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A patient's heart valve does not close fully, so blood leaks backwards. Which treatment fits this problem best?","o":["A stent placed in a coronary artery","Statins to lower blood cholesterol","Antibiotics to kill pathogens in the valve","Replacing the valve with a biological or mechanical valve"],"a":3,"x":"A faulty valve can be replaced with a biological valve (from an animal or human donor) or a mechanical valve. Stents and statins treat narrowed coronary arteries, not a valve that leaks.","w":["Stents hold coronary arteries open. They do not fix a valve that leaks.","Statins lower cholesterol to protect arteries. They do not repair a valve.","A leaking valve is a structural fault, not an infection, so antibiotics will not fix it.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-099","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Cancer cells from a tumour in the bowel travel in the blood and start a new tumour in the liver. What is the new tumour in the liver called?","o":["A primary tumour","A secondary tumour","A benign tumour","A carcinogen"],"a":1,"x":"Malignant cells that break away, travel in the blood and form a new tumour elsewhere make a secondary tumour. The first tumour, in the bowel, is the primary tumour.","w":["The primary tumour is the first one, in the bowel. The liver tumour formed later.","","Benign tumours stay in one place and do not spread. This one came from spreading cells.","A carcinogen is something that causes cancer, such as tobacco smoke. It is not a tumour."]}
{"id":"gbi-100","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.4","lv":3,"q":"In a trial, 40 of 1000 people on a statin had a heart attack in 5 years. In 1000 people on a dummy tablet, 70 did. By what percentage did the statin cut the number of heart attacks?","o":["43%","57%","30%","3%"],"a":0,"x":"The difference is 70 − 40 = 30 heart attacks. As a percentage of the dummy group: 30 ÷ 70 × 100 = 42.9%, about 43%. The trap is using 40 ÷ 70 or forgetting to divide by 70.","w":["","40 ÷ 70 gives 57%, which is what remained, not the reduction.","30 is the number of fewer heart attacks, not a percentage. It must be divided by 70.","3% is the difference in percentage points (7% − 4%), not the percentage reduction."]}
{"id":"gbi-101","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":1,"q":"What is translocation in a plant?","o":["Loss of water vapour from the leaves through the stomata","Movement of water up from the roots through the xylem","Movement of dissolved sugars through the phloem to where they are needed","Movement of mineral ions into the root hair cells by active transport"],"a":2,"x":"Translocation is the movement of dissolved sugars (sucrose) in the phloem from the leaves to growing or storage areas. The trap is mixing it up with transpiration, which is water loss from the leaves.","w":["This describes transpiration, not translocation; the two words are easy to confuse.","This is the transpiration stream in the xylem; translocation moves sugars, not water from roots.","","This describes mineral ion uptake by root hair cells, not the movement of sugars around the plant."]}
{"id":"gbi-102","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about xylem vessels is correct?","o":["They are hollow dead cells with walls strengthened by lignin","They are living cells joined by sieve plates","They carry dissolved sugars from the leaves to the roots","They contain many chloroplasts to make glucose"],"a":0,"x":"Xylem vessels are dead, hollow tubes, so water flows through easily. Lignin strengthens their walls. The trap is swapping xylem with phloem, which has sieve plates and carries sugars.","w":["","Sieve plates and living cells describe phloem, not xylem; the two tissues get swapped.","Xylem carries water and mineral ions up the plant; sugars travel in the phloem.","Xylem vessels are dead and hollow with no chloroplasts; photosynthesis happens in mesophyll cells."]}
{"id":"gbi-103","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A potted plant is moved from still air to in front of a fan. Why does its transpiration rate increase?","o":["The moving air makes the guard cells close the stomata","The moving air cools the leaf, so less water evaporates","The moving air makes the roots take in more water","The moving air removes water vapour, keeping the gradient steep"],"a":3,"x":"Moving air carries water vapour away from the leaf surface. This keeps the concentration gradient steep, so water vapour diffuses out faster. The trap is thinking roots drive it: more uptake is the result, not the cause, of faster transpiration.","w":["Closing stomata would reduce transpiration, not increase it; wind does not close them.","Less evaporation would lower transpiration, so this cannot explain the increase.","Roots do not drive the rate; uptake follows transpiration, so this reverses cause and effect.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-104","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A plant loses 120 cm³ of water by transpiration in 8 hours. Assuming the rate stays constant, how much water does it lose in 3 hours?","o":["15 cm³","45 cm³","40 cm³","320 cm³"],"a":1,"x":"Rate = 120 ÷ 8 = 15 cm³ per hour. In 3 hours: 15 × 3 = 45 cm³. The trap is stopping at the hourly rate or dividing by the wrong number.","w":["This is the rate per hour; the student forgot to multiply by 3 hours.","","This divides 120 by 3, ignoring the 8 hours that the 120 cm³ was lost over.","This is 120 ÷ 3 × 8; the two times are the wrong way round."]}
{"id":"gbi-105","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":3,"q":"At midday on a hot, windy day a plant wilts, even though the soil is very wet. What is the best explanation?","o":["The stomata are closed, so no water can reach the leaf cells","The phloem has stopped carrying water up from the roots","Transpiration loses water faster than roots replace it, so cells lose turgor","The leaf cells have too much water, so they become flaccid"],"a":2,"x":"In hot, windy conditions transpiration is fast. If water leaves the leaves faster than the xylem can supply it, cells lose turgor and the plant wilts. The trap is assuming wet soil means uptake always keeps pace.","w":["Closed stomata would reduce water loss; they do not stop water reaching the cells.","Phloem carries sugars, not water; the xylem carries water up from the roots.","","Too much water makes cells turgid, not flaccid; flaccid cells have lost water."]}
{"id":"gbi-106","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"How is the measles virus usually spread from person to person?","o":["By mosquito bites","By drinking dirty water","In droplets from coughs and sneezes","By sharing needles"],"a":2,"x":"Measles is spread through the air in droplets from coughs and sneezes. Mosquitoes carry malaria. Dirty water and shared needles spread other diseases, not measles.","w":["Mosquitoes are the vector for malaria, not measles. This mixes up two diseases.","Water spreads some bacterial diseases such as cholera, but measles is airborne.","","Sharing needles can spread HIV, but measles travels in the air."]}
{"id":"gbi-107","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Rose black spot is a fungal disease of plants. Which is a way to control it?","o":["Use a fungicide and strip off affected leaves","Spray the plant with antibiotics","Vaccinate the plant against the fungus","Water the plant more often"],"a":0,"x":"Fungicides kill the fungus, and removing affected leaves cuts down its spread. Antibiotics work on bacteria, not fungi, and plants are not vaccinated.","w":["","Antibiotics only work on bacteria. Thinking they kill any pathogen is a common slip.","Plants are not vaccinated. This wrongly treats plants like animals.","Extra water does not kill the fungus. Wet leaves can even help the spores spread."]}
{"id":"gbi-108","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"HIV is spread by sexual contact and by exchange of body fluids such as blood. Which action helps to reduce its spread?","o":["Covering the mouth when sneezing","Washing hands before eating","Wearing a mask in crowded places","Using a condom during sex"],"a":3,"x":"HIV passes through body fluids, so a condom acts as a barrier. Covering coughs, masks and hand washing stop airborne or food-borne pathogens, not HIV. The trap is using the wrong control for the route of spread.","w":["HIV is not spread in sneeze droplets. This control suits airborne diseases like measles.","Hand washing helps against germs from food and touch, but HIV is not spread this way.","Masks block airborne droplets. HIV is not spread through the air.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-109","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Leaves on a tomato plant show a yellow and green mosaic pattern. The pathogen is tobacco mosaic virus. Why does this make the plant grow poorly?","o":["The virus blocks the roots so they cannot take up water","The discoloured patches have less chlorophyll, so less photosynthesis happens","The virus makes the plant produce too much chlorophyll, which poisons it","Insects eat the infected leaves, so fewer leaves remain"],"a":1,"x":"Tobacco mosaic virus causes patches of discolouration. These parts of the leaf have less chlorophyll, so the plant photosynthesises less and makes less glucose for growth.","w":["TMV affects the leaves, not the root's water uptake. This is a guess not linked to the symptoms.","","The patches are yellow because chlorophyll is lost, not gained.","Insects are not the cause of the damage. The virus itself damages the leaf."]}
{"id":"gbi-110","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Disease X spreads only by direct contact between people. No animal carries it. Which control measure would be least useful?","o":["Isolating people who have the disease","Washing hands carefully after touching patients","Killing insects in the area","Vaccinating people who are not yet infected"],"a":2,"x":"Killing insects controls vectors. It only helps if an animal carries the pathogen, as mosquitoes do for malaria. Here there is no vector, so it is least useful. Isolation, hygiene and vaccination all cut contact spread.","w":["Isolation keeps infected people away from others, which directly reduces contact spread.","Good hygiene removes pathogens from hands and reduces contact spread.","","Vaccination makes people immune, so it helps whatever the route of spread."]}
{"id":"gbi-111","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A child has a fever and a red skin rash. Which disease would a doctor most likely suspect?","o":["Gonorrhoea","Malaria","Measles","Rose black spot"],"a":2,"x":"Measles is a viral disease that causes a fever and a red skin rash. It spreads in droplets from coughs and sneezes. The MMR vaccine prevents it. The trap is picking malaria because of the fever.","w":["Gonorrhoea causes pain when urinating and a thick discharge, not a fever with a red skin rash.","Malaria causes repeated fevers but no red skin rash, so fever alone does not point to malaria.","","Rose black spot is a fungal disease of rose plants, so it cannot infect a child."]}
{"id":"gbi-112","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about HIV is correct?","o":["Antiretroviral drugs can control the virus, which attacks immune cells","Antibiotics cure HIV once the flu-like symptoms appear","It is spread to people by mosquito bites","It first attacks red blood cells and causes anaemia"],"a":0,"x":"HIV is a virus that attacks the body's immune cells. Antiretroviral drugs control it. The trap is thinking antibiotics help: they only work on bacteria, not viruses.","w":["","Antibiotics only kill bacteria, so they cannot cure HIV, which is a virus.","Mosquitoes spread malaria; HIV spreads by sexual contact or by exchange of body fluids such as blood.","HIV attacks the body's immune cells, not red blood cells, so it does not cause anaemia."]}
{"id":"gbi-113","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Leaves on a tobacco plant have a mosaic of discoloured patches. The plant grows poorly. What is the best explanation?","o":["The virus eats the leaves, so the plant cannot make seeds","The virus blocks the roots, so no water can be absorbed","Spores of the virus grow on the stem and release toxins","The virus discolours leaves, so less photosynthesis and less growth"],"a":3,"x":"Tobacco mosaic virus discolours parts of the leaves. Those parts cannot photosynthesise as well. The plant makes less glucose, so it grows poorly. The trap is thinking the virus physically eats the leaves.","w":["Viruses do not eat leaves; they reproduce inside plant cells and damage them.","TMV affects the leaves, not the roots, so it reduces photosynthesis rather than water uptake.","Spores belong to fungi, not viruses, and TMV does not grow on the stem.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-114","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A family have vomiting, diarrhoea and stomach cramps after eating undercooked chicken. What most likely caused this, and why do they feel ill?","o":["A virus in cough droplets, which destroys cells as it reproduces","Salmonella bacteria, which make toxins that cause the symptoms","Fungal spores on the meat, which digest the lining of the gut","A protist from mosquito bites, which destroys red blood cells"],"a":1,"x":"Salmonella food poisoning is bacterial. The bacteria secrete toxins, causing fever, cramps, vomiting and diarrhoea. Cooking meat well and vaccinating poultry help prevent it. The trap is thinking the illness is viral.","w":["Food poisoning from undercooked chicken is caused by Salmonella bacteria, not a virus spread in cough droplets.","","Fungal spores spread diseases like rose black spot; undercooked chicken carries Salmonella bacteria.","Mosquitoes carry the malaria protist; this illness comes from Salmonella bacteria in undercooked food."]}
{"id":"gbi-115","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":3,"q":"Gonorrhoea is now hard to treat with penicillin. Which pair of statements explains this and gives a way to reduce its spread?","o":["People's bodies became resistant to penicillin; condoms reduce spread","The virus became resistant to penicillin; condoms reduce spread","Resistant strains of bacteria have appeared; condoms reduce spread","Resistant strains of bacteria have appeared; mosquito nets reduce spread"],"a":2,"x":"Gonorrhoea is a sexually transmitted bacterial disease. Many strains are now resistant to penicillin. Using a barrier method such as a condom reduces spread. The trap is saying people become resistant. Bacteria do.","w":["People do not become resistant to antibiotics; the bacteria develop resistant strains.","Gonorrhoea is caused by bacteria, not a virus, and antibiotics do not work on viruses anyway.","","Mosquitoes spread malaria, not gonorrhoea, which spreads by sexual contact."]}
{"id":"gbi-116","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"How do the trachea and bronchi help to stop pathogens from reaching the lungs?","o":["Hairs filter pathogens out of the air we breathe in","Hydrochloric acid kills pathogens in the air passages","Mucus traps pathogens and cilia move the mucus upwards","Platelets form a scab that seals off the air passages"],"a":2,"x":"The trachea and bronchi have mucus that traps pathogens, and cilia that move the mucus away from the lungs. Hairs are in the nose and acid is in the stomach, so those are traps.","w":["Hairs and mucus work in the nose; the trachea and bronchi use cilia and mucus instead.","Hydrochloric acid is found in the stomach, not the air passages.","","Platelets help blood clot at wounds. They do not block the air passages."]}
{"id":"gbi-117","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.6","lv":2,"q":"Antibodies made against one type of bacterium do not work against a virus. What is the reason?","o":["Each antibody has a shape that fits only one type of antigen","Antibodies can only attack pathogens that release toxins","Viruses are so small that antibodies can never bind to them","Antibodies are made by phagocytes, which only engulf bacteria"],"a":0,"x":"Antibodies are specific. Each one has a shape that fits the antigen of one pathogen only. A common trap is thinking one antibody can attack any pathogen.","w":["","Antibodies bind to antigens on pathogens. Toxins are dealt with by antitoxins, so this mixes them up.","Antibodies can bind to viruses. Size is not the reason for specificity.","Lymphocytes make antibodies, not phagocytes. Phagocytes also engulf more than bacteria."]}
{"id":"gbi-118","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.6","lv":2,"q":"Which shows the correct order of events when the body makes antibodies against a new pathogen?","o":["Antibodies made, lymphocytes detect antigens, antibodies bind to pathogen","Antibodies bind to antigens, lymphocytes stimulated, antibodies made","Phagocytes detect antigens, phagocytes make antibodies, pathogen destroyed","Lymphocytes detect antigens, make antibodies, antibodies help destroy pathogen"],"a":3,"x":"Lymphocytes detect the pathogen's antigens. This stimulates them to make antibodies, which then help to destroy the pathogen. The trap is putting antibodies before detection or giving the job to phagocytes.","w":["Antibodies cannot be made before the antigens are detected, so the order is wrong.","Antibodies are only made after lymphocytes are stimulated, so they cannot bind first.","Lymphocytes make antibodies, not phagocytes. Phagocytes engulf pathogens.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-119","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.6","lv":2,"q":"A patient takes a medicine that greatly reduces the acid in their stomach. Why might they be more likely to get food poisoning?","o":["Less mucus is made, so pathogens are not trapped in the stomach","Fewer of the pathogens in swallowed food are killed by the acid","Fewer white blood cells are made in the stomach lining","Cilia in the stomach no longer move pathogens out of the body"],"a":1,"x":"Stomach hydrochloric acid kills pathogens that are swallowed in food and drink. With less acid, more survive. The trap is thinking the stomach has cilia or makes white blood cells.","w":["The acid is the stomach's defence here. Trapping with mucus is a job of the airways.","","The stomach lining does not make white blood cells, and the medicine only affects acid.","The stomach has no cilia. Cilia are found in the airways."]}
{"id":"gbi-120","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.6","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about white blood cells is correct?","o":["Phagocytes make antibodies that fit only one type of antigen","Lymphocytes engulf any pathogen they meet and digest it","Phagocytes engulf many types of pathogen, but each antibody fits one antigen","Antibodies neutralise toxins, while antitoxins bind to antigens on pathogens"],"a":2,"x":"Phagocytosis is not specific, so phagocytes can engulf many kinds of pathogen. Antibodies are specific, each fitting one antigen. The common slip is mixing up which cell does which job.","w":["Antibodies are made by lymphocytes, not phagocytes.","Engulfing and digesting is phagocytosis, done by phagocytes. Lymphocytes make antibodies.","","This swaps the two. Antitoxins neutralise toxins, and antibodies bind to antigens on pathogens."]}
{"id":"gbi-121","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.7","lv":1,"q":"A vaccine is made to protect people from a disease. What does the vaccine contain?","o":["Ready-made antibodies from another person","Antibiotics that kill the pathogen","Dead or inactive forms of the pathogen","Small amounts of fully active pathogen"],"a":2,"x":"Vaccines contain dead or inactive pathogens. These have antigens, so white blood cells make antibodies, but they cannot cause the disease. Vaccines do not contain antibodies or antibiotics.","w":["Vaccines do not contain ready-made antibodies; the person's own white blood cells make them after vaccination.","Antibiotics treat bacterial infections and are not in vaccines; this confuses vaccines with antibiotics.","","Fully active pathogens could cause the disease; vaccines use dead or inactive forms to stay safe."]}
{"id":"gbi-122","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"A child has had the measles vaccine. Why does this vaccine not protect them against mumps?","o":["The measles vaccine kills the white blood cells needed for mumps","Mumps is caused by bacteria and vaccines only work on viruses","The vaccine only works for a few weeks, so protection has run out","Antibodies are specific, so measles antibodies cannot recognise mumps antigens"],"a":3,"x":"Antibodies are specific to one antigen. Measles antibodies do not fit mumps antigens, so protection against mumps needs its own vaccine. The trap is thinking a vaccine gives general protection against other diseases.","w":["Vaccines do not kill white blood cells; they stimulate them to make antibodies.","Mumps is caused by a virus, and vaccines also work against some bacterial diseases.","Protection from a vaccine lasts much longer than weeks; the real reason is specificity.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-123","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"Sam was vaccinated against measles last year. He is now exposed to the live measles virus. Why is he unlikely to become ill?","o":["His white blood cells quickly make the correct antibodies","The vaccine left antibiotics in his blood to kill the virus","Measles viruses cannot enter the body of a vaccinated person","The vaccine antibodies stay at high levels, so none need to be made"],"a":0,"x":"After vaccination, white blood cells respond quickly to the same pathogen and make the correct antibodies. The infection is stopped before he becomes ill. The trap is thinking the vaccine itself keeps working.","w":["","Vaccines do not contain antibiotics, and antibiotics do not work on viruses.","The virus can still enter the body; the immune system destroys it quickly.","Antibody levels fall after vaccination; protection comes from white blood cells responding quickly."]}
{"id":"gbi-124","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"A disease stops spreading when 90% of a town of 4000 people are vaccinated. At the moment 2800 people are vaccinated. How many more people need vaccinating?","o":["400","800","1200","3600"],"a":1,"x":"90% of 4000 is 3600. Subtract the 2800 already vaccinated: 3600 − 2800 = 800. The traps are giving the total needed, or counting everyone not yet vaccinated.","w":["This is 10% of 4000; it ignores the 90% target and the 2800 already vaccinated.","","This is everyone not yet vaccinated (4000 − 2800), but only 90% of the town needs to be vaccinated.","This is the total needed; it forgets to subtract the 2800 already vaccinated."]}
{"id":"gbi-125","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.7","lv":3,"q":"A new flu vaccine is made each year. Why is last year's vaccine often not enough to protect people from this year's flu?","o":["Flu viruses become resistant to antibiotics, so vaccines stop working","Vaccines only protect people for a few days after the injection","The flu virus can change, so its antigens no longer match the old antibodies","The dead viruses in the vaccine grow back into active viruses each winter"],"a":2,"x":"Flu viruses change (mutate), so their antigens can be different. Antibodies are specific, so old antibodies may not fit the new antigens. The trap is mixing this up with antibiotic resistance.","w":["Antibiotics do not work on viruses, and resistance is not why flu vaccines are updated.","Vaccine protection lasts much longer than days; the problem is the virus changing.","","Dead or inactive viruses in a vaccine cannot become active again."]}
{"id":"gbi-126","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":1,"q":"Many useful drugs began as chemicals found in plants or microorganisms. Which natural source was the painkiller aspirin first taken from?","o":["Foxglove plants","Penicillium mould","Willow bark","Poppy seeds"],"a":2,"x":"Aspirin was first taken from willow bark. Foxgloves gave digitalis, a heart drug, and Penicillium mould gave penicillin. Do not mix up which natural source gave which drug.","w":["Foxgloves gave digitalis, a heart drug, not aspirin. This mixes up two plant-based drugs.","Penicillium mould gave penicillin, an antibiotic. This confuses a painkiller with an antibiotic.","","Poppies are not the source of aspirin. This is a guess based on poppies being linked to other painkillers."]}
{"id":"gbi-127","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":1,"q":"Why are doctors told not to prescribe antibiotics for mild bacterial infections that will clear up on their own?","o":["Antibiotics stop the immune system working properly","Antibiotics make viruses mutate into bacteria","A patient's body becomes resistant to antibiotics","Overuse makes resistant strains of bacteria more common"],"a":3,"x":"Using antibiotics too often gives resistant bacteria a survival advantage, so they spread. The trap is thinking the person becomes resistant. It is the bacteria that become resistant.","w":["Antibiotics do not damage the immune system. The problem is resistance in bacteria.","Viruses do not turn into bacteria. This is a made-up effect.","The person does not become resistant. It is the bacteria that evolve resistance.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-128","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"A patient has a bacterial throat infection and takes only painkillers. What is the most likely result?","o":["Pain is eased but the bacteria keep multiplying","The bacteria are killed and the infection clears","The bacteria become resistant to the painkillers","The painkillers kill the bacteria, but only slowly"],"a":0,"x":"Painkillers treat symptoms such as pain. They do not kill pathogens. Only an antibiotic would kill the bacteria. The trap is thinking that feeling better means the infection has been cured.","w":["","Painkillers do not kill pathogens. Feeling better does not mean the bacteria have gone.","Bacteria become resistant to antibiotics, not painkillers. This mixes up two types of drug.","Painkillers do not kill bacteria at any speed. They only relieve symptoms."]}
{"id":"gbi-129","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"A new drug is developed. Which list shows the correct order of testing?","o":["Healthy volunteers, live animals, cells and tissues, patients","Cells and tissues, live animals, healthy volunteers, patients","Cells and tissues, live animals, patients, healthy volunteers","Live animals, cells and tissues, patients, healthy volunteers"],"a":1,"x":"Preclinical testing comes first, on cells and tissues and then live animals. Clinical trials follow, first on healthy volunteers to check safety, then on patients. Drugs are not given to people before preclinical tests.","w":["Humans are not tested before cells and animals. Safety is checked in the lab first.","","Healthy volunteers come before patients. Safety is checked in healthy people before sick people.","Cells and tissues are tested before live animals, and healthy volunteers before patients."]}
{"id":"gbi-130","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":3,"q":"In a trial of 200 patients with a bacterial infection, 100 get a new antibiotic and 100 get a placebo. 80 recover with the drug and 30 with the placebo. Which conclusion is best?","o":["The drug has no effect, because 30 patients recovered without it","The placebo group shows that the new drug is unsafe for patients","The drug helped, because more patients recovered with it than with the placebo","The placebo was not needed, because 80 patients recovered with the drug"],"a":2,"x":"The placebo group is the comparison. 80 recovered with the drug and only 30 with the placebo, so the drug probably helped. Some people recover or feel better anyway, so you need the placebo group to tell.","w":["Some patients recover without the drug, but far more recovered with it. Ignoring the comparison is the slip.","Recoveries on the placebo say nothing about safety. Safety is judged from side effects.","","Without the placebo group you could not tell how many would have recovered anyway."]}
{"id":"gbi-131","s":"biology","t":"4.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Hybridoma cells are made by fusing a mouse lymphocyte with a tumour cell. The lymphocyte makes the wanted antibody. What does the tumour cell add to the hybridoma?","o":["The ability to make many different types of antibody","The ability to divide quickly, making many identical cells","The ability to make antibodies against any antigen","The ability to survive when injected into patients"],"a":1,"x":"The lymphocyte gives the specific antibody. The tumour cell gives rapid, repeated division, so one hybridoma forms a clone that makes lots of identical antibody. The trap is thinking the tumour cell changes which antibody is made.","w":["The lymphocyte makes only one type of antibody, and the hybridoma clone makes identical copies, not different types.","","Specificity comes from the lymphocyte, which was stimulated by one antigen; the tumour cell does not widen it.","Hybridomas are grown in the lab to make antibody, not injected; the tumour cell adds rapid division."]}
{"id":"gbi-132","s":"biology","t":"4.3.2","lv":3,"q":"A cancer drug is attached to a monoclonal antibody instead of being injected alone. Why does this usually cause fewer side effects for the patient?","o":["The antibody makes the drug less toxic to every cell","The antibody comes from the patient's own cells, so no side effects occur","The antibody carries the drug to every cell, so the dose is diluted","The antibody binds only to antigens on cancer cells, so the drug is targeted"],"a":3,"x":"Each monoclonal antibody binds one specific antigen found on the cancer cells. The drug is delivered to them, not to healthy cells. The trap is thinking the drug becomes weaker. It stays toxic but is targeted.","w":["The drug is just as toxic; the antibody changes where it goes, not how toxic it is.","The antibodies come from mouse lymphocytes, not the patient's cells, and side effects can still happen.","The antibody is specific, so it does not go to every cell or dilute the dose.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-133","s":"biology","t":"4.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Monoclonal antibodies against protein X carry a fluorescent dye. They are added to a tissue sample, and glow shows where protein X is. Why does the glow appear only at protein X?","o":["The antibody's binding site fits only protein X, so it attaches only there","The dye binds only to protein X, and the antibody simply carries it","The antibodies destroy all other proteins, so only protein X is left to glow","The tissue makes the antibodies, so they gather where protein X is made"],"a":0,"x":"A monoclonal antibody has a binding site that is complementary to one specific antigen. It attaches only to protein X, and the dye shows where. The trap is thinking the dye chooses the target; it is only a label.","w":["","The dye is only a marker; the antibody's specific shape decides where it binds.","Antibodies bind to their target and do not destroy other proteins, which stay in the tissue.","The antibodies are made in the lab, not by the tissue; they bind because their shape fits protein X."]}
{"id":"gbi-135","s":"biology","t":"4.3.3","lv":1,"q":"The leaves of a plant fold down when an insect lands on them. This helps to protect the plant. What type of defence is this?","o":["A chemical defence","A physical barrier, like bark","A response to a lack of minerals","A mechanical adaptation"],"a":3,"x":"Leaves that curl or droop when touched are a mechanical adaptation, like thorns and hairs. Physical barriers are non-moving structures, such as bark, cell walls and the waxy cuticle.","w":["Chemical defences are poisons or antibacterial chemicals, but folding leaves is a movement, not a chemical.","Bark is a non-moving layer of dead cells, whereas folding leaves is a mechanical adaptation.","Mineral shortages cause symptoms like yellow leaves or stunted growth and are not a defence against insects.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-136","s":"biology","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A gardener finds purple-black spots on rose leaves. The leaves turn yellow and fall off early. This is rose black spot. Which action helps to control it?","o":["Remove and destroy infected leaves and use a fungicide","Leave the fallen leaves on the soil so they can rot down","Spray the plant with antibiotics to kill the cause","Add extra nitrate fertiliser to the soil"],"a":0,"x":"Rose black spot is a fungal disease spread by wind and water. Destroying infected leaves and using a fungicide limits its spread. Antibiotics only work on bacteria, and leaving leaves lets spores spread.","w":["","Fallen infected leaves carry fungal spores that spread by wind and water, so leaving them spreads the disease.","Antibiotics kill bacteria, not fungi, so black spot needs a fungicide instead.","Nitrate fertiliser treats a mineral shortage, not a fungal disease, so it will not stop the spots."]}
{"id":"gbi-137","s":"biology","t":"4.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A scientist tests a sick plant with a kit containing monoclonal antibodies. The result is positive. What does this show?","o":["The plant is short of mineral ions","The plant has made its own antibodies against the disease","The antibodies have bound to antigens from a specific pathogen","The antibodies in the kit have killed the pathogen"],"a":2,"x":"The kit's monoclonal antibodies bind only to antigens from one pathogen. A positive result means that pathogen is present. The kit identifies the pathogen. Plants do not make antibodies, and the kit does not treat the plant.","w":["Mineral shortages are found from symptoms or soil tests, while antibody kits detect pathogens.","Plants do not make antibodies, so the antibodies must come from the kit itself.","","This confuses testing with treating, because the kit's antibodies only detect the pathogen and do not kill it."]}
{"id":"gbi-139","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which word equation shows photosynthesis?","o":["glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water","carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen","glucose + water → carbon dioxide + oxygen","carbon dioxide + oxygen → glucose + water"],"a":1,"x":"Photosynthesis uses light to make glucose and oxygen from carbon dioxide and water. Aerobic respiration is the reverse reaction, so don't mix up the two equations.","w":["This is aerobic respiration, where the reactants and products of photosynthesis are swapped round.","","Glucose is a product of photosynthesis, not a reactant, and oxygen is a product too.","Oxygen is a product of photosynthesis and water is a reactant, so these substances are muddled."]}
{"id":"gbi-140","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Photosynthesis is an endothermic reaction. What does this mean?","o":["It releases heat energy into the air around the leaf","It only happens when the air is very warm","It needs oxygen to be present in the leaf","It transfers energy from the surroundings to the chloroplasts"],"a":3,"x":"Endothermic reactions take in energy from the surroundings. In photosynthesis, light transfers energy from the environment to the chloroplasts. Don't confuse this with exothermic, which releases energy.","w":["This describes an exothermic reaction, which gives energy out instead of taking it in.","Endothermic describes the direction of energy transfer, not a high temperature the reaction needs.","Photosynthesis produces oxygen rather than needing it, so this mixes it up with aerobic respiration.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-141","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Plants turn some of their glucose into starch for storage. Why is starch better than glucose for storing in cells?","o":["Starch is soluble, so it moves easily around the plant","Starch is used to make the cell walls stronger","Starch is insoluble, so it does not affect water movement into cells","Starch releases more energy than glucose in respiration"],"a":2,"x":"Starch is insoluble, so it does not dissolve in the cell and does not draw in water by osmosis. Glucose is soluble and would. Cellulose, not starch, strengthens cell walls.","w":["Starch is insoluble, and a soluble store would upset water movement into cells by osmosis.","Cell walls are strengthened by cellulose, not starch, so this mixes up two uses of glucose.","","Starch must be broken down to glucose before respiration; its storage advantage is being insoluble."]}
{"id":"gbi-142","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The equation is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. How many molecules of glucose and oxygen form when 12 molecules of CO₂ react fully?","o":["2 glucose and 12 oxygen","2 glucose and 6 oxygen","12 glucose and 12 oxygen","1 glucose and 6 oxygen"],"a":0,"x":"12 CO₂ is two lots of the equation (6 × 2). So you get 2 glucose and 2 × 6 = 12 oxygen. Remember to multiply both products, not just one.","w":["","The glucose has been doubled but the oxygen has not, yet both products must be multiplied by 2.","Each CO₂ does not make one glucose; six CO₂ molecules are needed for one glucose molecule.","These are the amounts made from only 6 CO₂, but 12 CO₂ is twice the equation."]}
{"id":"gbi-143","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A young tree is grown in a pot of soil and watered for five years. Its dry mass rises by 60 kg. The soil's dry mass falls by only 0.1 kg. Where does most of the new mass come from?","o":["Minerals taken in from the soil","Carbon dioxide from the air and water","Oxygen taken in from the air","Light energy absorbed by chlorophyll"],"a":1,"x":"Glucose, and so cellulose and starch, is built from carbon dioxide and water. The soil lost almost no mass, so minerals can't be the main source. Light is energy, not matter.","w":["The soil mass hardly changed, so minerals cannot account for 60 kg; plants do not 'eat' soil.","","Oxygen is released by photosynthesis, not used to build the plant's mass.","Light is energy with no mass to add; it drives the reaction but does not become plant material."]}
{"id":"gbi-144","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":1,"q":"The rate of photosynthesis in a plant rises as the temperature goes up. Above about 45 °C the rate falls quickly. What is the best reason for this fall?","o":["The leaf receives less light at high temperatures","Carbon dioxide becomes too hot to be absorbed","The enzymes that control photosynthesis are denatured","Chlorophyll is used up faster than it is made"],"a":2,"x":"Photosynthesis is controlled by enzymes. At high temperatures their shape changes and they are denatured, so the rate falls. The trap is thinking heat only ever speeds reactions up.","w":["High temperature does not reduce the light reaching the leaf; this confuses temperature with light intensity.","Carbon dioxide is a gas that is not 'too hot to absorb'; the real cause is the enzymes.","","Chlorophyll is not used up like a reactant; this misunderstands its role in absorbing light."]}
{"id":"gbi-145","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"In required practical 6, a beaker of water is placed between the lamp and the pondweed. Why is this done?","o":["To make the light from the lamp brighter","To add more carbon dioxide to the pondweed","To stop oxygen bubbles escaping from the water","To absorb heat so the temperature stays constant"],"a":3,"x":"Moving the lamp changes light intensity, but the lamp also gives out heat. The water absorbs this heat, so temperature stays a control variable and only light intensity changes.","w":["Water does not make light brighter; it would not help the investigation.","The water beaker does not supply carbon dioxide; sodium hydrogencarbonate does that.","Oxygen bubbles are meant to be counted; the beaker is not about keeping gas in.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-146","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A grower burns paraffin in a heater inside a greenhouse. How does this help the plants to photosynthesise faster?","o":["It releases carbon dioxide and warms the air","It releases oxygen and warms the air","It releases carbon dioxide and cools the air","It absorbs carbon dioxide and warms the air"],"a":0,"x":"Burning paraffin gives out heat and carbon dioxide. Both can limit photosynthesis, so raising them can raise the rate. The trap is thinking plants need extra oxygen for photosynthesis.","w":["","Burning uses up oxygen, and oxygen is a product of photosynthesis, not a reactant.","Burning gives out heat, so the air is warmed, not cooled.","Burning paraffin releases carbon dioxide into the air; it does not absorb it."]}
{"id":"gbi-147","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":3,"q":"Pondweed at 20 °C gives the same bubble rate when the lamp is moved closer. When the water is warmed to 30 °C, the rate rises. What was limiting the rate at 20 °C?","o":["Light intensity","Temperature","Carbon dioxide concentration","The oxygen produced"],"a":1,"x":"More light made no difference, so light was not limiting. Warming the water raised the rate, so temperature was the limiting factor. The trap is picking light because the lamp was moved.","w":["Moving the lamp closer gave no increase, so light was not limiting.","","Carbon dioxide was not changed, and the rate rose when only temperature changed.","Oxygen is a product we measure; it is not a factor that limits the rate."]}
{"id":"gbi-148","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":1,"q":"In the pondweed investigation, which substance is dissolved in the water to increase the carbon dioxide concentration?","o":["Sodium chloride","Calcium carbonate","Sodium hydrogencarbonate","Glucose"],"a":2,"x":"Sodium hydrogencarbonate dissolves and releases carbon dioxide into the water, so pondweed can photosynthesise. Glucose is a product of photosynthesis, not a source of carbon dioxide.","w":["Sodium chloride is common salt and does not release carbon dioxide.","Calcium carbonate hardly dissolves in water, so it is not used to add carbon dioxide.","","Glucose is made by photosynthesis; it is a product, not the raw material carbon dioxide."]}
{"id":"gbi-149","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is the correct word equation for aerobic respiration?","o":["glucose + oxygen → lactic acid + water","glucose + carbon dioxide → oxygen + water","glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water","carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen"],"a":2,"x":"Aerobic respiration uses glucose and oxygen and makes carbon dioxide and water. Do not mix it up with photosynthesis, which is the reverse. Lactic acid only forms in anaerobic respiration.","w":["Lactic acid is made in anaerobic respiration, but aerobic respiration makes carbon dioxide and water.","Carbon dioxide is a product of respiration, not a reactant, so the gases have been swapped.","","This is the photosynthesis equation, which is the reverse of respiration."]}
{"id":"gbi-150","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Anaerobic respiration in muscles releases much less energy than aerobic respiration. Why?","o":["Glucose is only partly broken down","Muscles use less glucose in anaerobic respiration","Oxygen is a fuel, so its absence means no energy","Carbon dioxide is made, which takes energy away"],"a":0,"x":"Without oxygen, glucose is not fully broken down. It forms lactic acid, which still holds energy. So less energy is released per glucose molecule. The trap is thinking oxygen is a fuel.","w":["","The amount of glucose is not the reason; each glucose molecule releases less energy because it is only partly broken down.","Oxygen is not a fuel; the energy comes from glucose, and oxygen lets it be fully broken down.","Muscle anaerobic respiration makes lactic acid, not carbon dioxide, and carbon dioxide does not take energy away."]}
{"id":"gbi-151","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Yeast in a sugar solution is kept with no oxygen. It respires anaerobically. What are its products?","o":["Lactic acid and carbon dioxide","Lactic acid only","Ethanol and oxygen","Ethanol and carbon dioxide"],"a":3,"x":"Yeast anaerobic respiration is fermentation. It makes ethanol and carbon dioxide. Lactic acid is the product in animal muscle cells. The trap is using the muscle equation for yeast.","w":["Lactic acid is the product in muscle cells; yeast makes ethanol, so this mixes up two organisms.","Lactic acid only is the muscle product; yeast makes ethanol and carbon dioxide.","Oxygen is never a product of respiration; yeast releases carbon dioxide alongside the ethanol.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-152","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"After a hard sprint, a girl keeps breathing deeply for several minutes. What is the main reason?","o":["To make more lactic acid in her muscles","To take in oxygen to break down the lactic acid","To breathe out the lactic acid from her lungs","To switch her muscles back to anaerobic respiration"],"a":1,"x":"Extra oxygen is needed after exercise to react with lactic acid built up in anaerobic respiration. This repays the oxygen debt. Lactic acid is not breathed out; it is taken to the liver and converted to glucose.","w":["Lactic acid is made when oxygen is short, so deep breathing helps remove it rather than make more.","","Lactic acid is not a gas; it is carried in the blood to the liver, not breathed out.","Deep breathing supplies oxygen for aerobic respiration, so it does not switch muscles to anaerobic respiration."]}
{"id":"gbi-153","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":3,"q":"During a 400 m race, a runner's legs begin to feel heavy and stop working well. Which is the best explanation?","o":["Aerobic respiration makes lactic acid, which builds up in the muscles","Anaerobic respiration stops, so no energy is released in the muscles","Anaerobic respiration makes lactic acid, which builds up in the muscles","Anaerobic respiration makes ethanol, which builds up in the muscles"],"a":2,"x":"When oxygen supply cannot keep up, muscles also respire anaerobically. This makes lactic acid, which builds up and causes muscle fatigue. The traps are naming the wrong process or the wrong product for human muscles.","w":["Aerobic respiration makes carbon dioxide and water; lactic acid comes from anaerobic respiration.","Anaerobic respiration does release some energy; the problem is the lactic acid it produces building up.","","Ethanol is made by yeast and plant cells in anaerobic respiration; human muscles make lactic acid."]}
{"id":"gbi-154","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"What does the word metabolism mean?","o":["Only the reactions that break molecules down","Only the reactions that release energy","All the chemical reactions in a cell or the body","Only the reactions that happen during digestion"],"a":2,"x":"Metabolism is the sum of all the reactions in a cell or the body. It includes building up and breaking down molecules. The trap is thinking it covers only one type of reaction.","w":["Metabolism also includes reactions that build bigger molecules, so breakdown alone is too narrow.","Many metabolic reactions use energy, for example making lipids, so this is too narrow.","","Digestion is only one small part of metabolism, which includes reactions in all cells."]}
{"id":"gbi-155","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Plant cell walls are strong. Which substance, made from glucose, is used to build them?","o":["Cellulose","Glycogen","Starch","Urea"],"a":0,"x":"Plants use energy from respiration to join glucose molecules into cellulose for cell walls. Starch is a food store and glycogen is an animal store. The trap is mixing up these glucose products.","w":["","Glycogen is the storage carbohydrate in animals, not a plant cell wall material.","Starch is a plant energy store, not the strong material of cell walls.","Urea is a waste made from excess proteins, not from glucose."]}
{"id":"gbi-156","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"The body has more protein than it needs. What happens to the excess?","o":["It is joined together to make glycogen","It is broken down and urea is made for excretion","It is stored unchanged as spare protein","It is used to make cellulose for cell walls"],"a":1,"x":"Excess protein is broken down in a metabolic reaction and urea is formed. Urea is then excreted. The trap is thinking the body stores spare protein, or that glycogen comes from protein.","w":["Glycogen is made from glucose, not from protein, so this mixes up the building molecules.","","The body does not store spare protein unchanged; it breaks it down and removes the waste as urea.","Cellulose is made from glucose in plants, and animals do not make it from protein."]}
{"id":"gbi-157","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A student says, \"Respiration is not part of metabolism because it breaks molecules down.\" Which statement is correct?","o":["The student is right, as metabolism only includes reactions that use energy","The student is wrong, because respiration builds new molecules from glucose","The student is right, as metabolism only builds bigger molecules","The student is wrong, as metabolism includes building and breakdown reactions"],"a":3,"x":"Metabolism is all the reactions in a cell. This includes respiration, which breaks glucose down, and reactions that build larger molecules. The trap is thinking metabolism means only building molecules.","w":["Respiration releases energy and is still metabolic; metabolism covers reactions that use energy and those that release it.","Respiration breaks glucose down to release energy; it does not build new molecules from it.","Metabolism is not limited to building; breakdown reactions like respiration count too, so the student is wrong.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-158","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.3","lv":3,"q":"A plant has light, water, carbon dioxide and glucose, but no nitrate ions in the soil. Which substance would it be unable to make in large amounts?","o":["Starch","Cellulose","Proteins","Lipids"],"a":2,"x":"Plants use glucose and nitrate ions to make amino acids, which are joined to make proteins. Without nitrate ions this cannot happen. Starch, cellulose and lipids are built from glucose-based molecules without nitrate ions.","w":["Starch is made by joining glucose molecules, so it does not need nitrate ions.","Cellulose is made by joining glucose molecules, so missing nitrate ions does not stop it.","","Lipids are made from glycerol and fatty acids, which do not need nitrate ions in this course."]}
{"id":"gbi-159","s":"biology","t":"4.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement best describes homeostasis?","o":["Keeping internal conditions the same as the surroundings","Regulating internal conditions at the best level for cells to work","Making the body respond only to changes outside it","Removing waste products from the body in urine"],"a":1,"x":"Homeostasis is the regulation of internal conditions, in response to internal and external changes, to keep them at the optimum for cells and enzymes. The trap is thinking it matches the surroundings or only deals with outside changes.","w":["The body keeps its conditions steady even when the surroundings change, so it does not copy them.","","Homeostasis also responds to internal changes, such as a rise in blood glucose after a meal.","That describes excretion, which is only one small part of keeping conditions steady."]}
{"id":"gbi-160","s":"biology","t":"4.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which list contains only coordination centres in the body's automatic control systems?","o":["Receptors, brain, muscles","Spinal cord, glands, pancreas","Sensory neurones, brain, spinal cord","Brain, spinal cord, pancreas"],"a":3,"x":"AQA names the brain, spinal cord and pancreas as coordination centres. They receive information and organise the response. Receptors detect stimuli. Muscles and glands are effectors.","w":["Receptors detect changes and muscles are effectors, so only the brain is a coordination centre here.","Glands are effectors that carry out responses, so this list is mixed up.","Sensory neurones only carry impulses. They are not coordination centres.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-161","s":"biology","t":"4.5.1","lv":2,"q":"On a hot day, receptors in the skin detect a rise in temperature. The brain processes this. Sweat glands then release sweat. Which part of this system is the effector?","o":["The brain","Sweat glands","Temperature receptors in the skin","The rise in body temperature"],"a":1,"x":"Effectors are muscles or glands that carry out the response. Sweat glands release sweat, which cools the body. The brain is the coordination centre and the skin receptors detect the change.","w":["The brain is the coordination centre. It processes information but does not carry out the response itself.","","Receptors detect the stimulus, so this is the receptor, not the effector.","The rise in temperature is the stimulus that starts the response, not a part of the body."]}
{"id":"gbi-162","s":"biology","t":"4.5.1","lv":3,"q":"On a hot day, a drug blocks the signals sent from the brain to the effectors. Receptors and the nerves leading to the brain still work. What is the likely result?","o":["Receptors stop detecting the rise, so no information is sent","Effectors respond as normal because receptors still detect the change","Change is detected but effectors don't respond, so temperature isn't restored","The brain stops receiving information, so it cannot detect the change"],"a":2,"x":"The control chain is receptor, coordination centre, effector. If the signal to the effectors is blocked, the change is still detected but no response happens. The trap is thinking blocking one step stops them all.","w":["The drug does not affect the receptors, so they still detect the rise.","Effectors need signals from the coordination centre. Detection alone cannot make them respond.","","Only the outgoing signals are blocked. Information still reaches the brain along the working nerves."]}
{"id":"gbi-163","s":"biology","t":"4.5.1","lv":2,"q":"Body temperature rises to 41 °C and stays there. Why is this dangerous for the body's cells?","o":["Enzymes are used up faster and run out","Enzymes are living cells that die when it is too hot","Enzymes work much faster and use up all the substrate at once","Enzymes change shape, so substrates no longer fit their active sites"],"a":3,"x":"Too high a temperature changes the shape of an enzyme's active site, so it is denatured and the substrate no longer fits. Reactions in cells then slow or stop. The trap is saying enzymes are 'killed'.","w":["Enzymes are catalysts and are not used up in reactions. Heat changes their shape instead.","Enzymes are proteins, not living things, so they cannot die. They denature.","Above the optimum, enzymes lose their shape and work more slowly. They do not speed up.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-164","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which shows the correct order for an impulse travelling through a reflex arc?","o":["receptor → motor neurone → relay neurone → sensory neurone → effector","receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector","receptor → sensory neurone → motor neurone → relay neurone → effector","effector → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → receptor"],"a":1,"x":"A receptor detects the stimulus. The impulse goes along a sensory neurone to a relay neurone in the CNS, then along a motor neurone to the effector. The trap is mixing up the order of the neurones.","w":["The neurones are in the wrong order. The sensory neurone carries the impulse from the receptor towards the CNS.","","The relay neurone sits between the sensory and motor neurones. The motor neurone is the one that reaches the effector.","This is backwards. The receptor detects the stimulus first and the effector produces the response last."]}
{"id":"gbi-165","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":1,"q":"How is a nerve impulse passed from one neurone to the next across a synapse?","o":["The electrical impulse jumps across the gap like a spark","A hormone is carried across the gap by the blood","The two neurones join together so the impulse flows straight through","A chemical diffuses across the gap and starts a new impulse"],"a":3,"x":"At a synapse there is a small gap between neurones. A chemical is released, diffuses across the gap and triggers a new electrical impulse in the next neurone. The impulse does not cross the gap itself.","w":["The electrical impulse cannot cross the gap. Thinking it jumps across is a common mistake.","Hormones travel in the blood, but synapses use chemicals that diffuse across a tiny gap.","The neurones do not touch. There is a gap between them, which is why a chemical is needed.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-166","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A person touches a hot pan and pulls their hand away before they feel any pain. Why is this response so fast?","o":["The impulse bypasses the conscious part of the brain, so the route is shorter","Motor neurones carry impulses much faster than sensory neurones do","Hormones carry the message through the blood straight to the muscle","The brain senses the danger and sends a stronger impulse to the muscle"],"a":0,"x":"Reflex actions are automatic and rapid. The coordinator passes the impulse straight to the effector without conscious thought, so the route is short. The trap is thinking the impulse is faster or stronger. Hormones would be slower.","w":["","Impulses do not travel faster along motor neurones. The reflex is quicker because the pathway is shorter.","Hormones travel in the blood and act more slowly than nerve impulses, so they cannot explain a fast reflex.","Impulse strength does not change the speed, and a reflex does not wait for the conscious brain to decide."]}
{"id":"gbi-167","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student uses the ruler drop test to see if caffeine changes reaction time. Which is a control variable in this investigation?","o":["The amount of caffeine in each drink","The distance the ruler falls before it is caught","Using the same hand to catch the ruler each time","The reaction time of the person being tested"],"a":2,"x":"A control variable is kept the same so the test is fair. Using the same hand each time is a control. The amount of caffeine is the independent variable. Distance fallen and reaction time are what is measured, so they are dependent.","w":["This is the independent variable, because the student deliberately changes it.","This is what the student measures, so it is the dependent variable, not something kept the same.","","This is the outcome of the test, so it is the dependent variable. It is not controlled."]}
{"id":"gbi-168","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Five reaction times (s) before caffeine: 0.24, 0.26, 0.25, 0.45, 0.25. The 0.45 s is anomalous. After caffeine the mean is 0.20 s. What is the percentage fall in mean reaction time?","o":["25%","20%","31%","5%"],"a":1,"x":"Ignore the anomaly: (0.24 + 0.26 + 0.25 + 0.25) ÷ 4 = 0.25 s. The fall is 0.05 s. 0.05 ÷ 0.25 × 100 = 20%. Divide by the original value, not the new one.","w":["This divides the 0.05 s fall by the new mean (0.20) instead of the original mean (0.25).","","This includes the anomalous 0.45 s in the mean (0.29 s), so the starting value is wrong.","This is the fall in seconds (0.05) written as a percentage. It was never converted using 0.05 ÷ 0.25."]}
{"id":"gbi-169","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which part of the brain controls unconscious activities such as heart rate and breathing?","o":["Cerebral cortex","Cerebellum","Medulla","Pituitary gland"],"a":2,"x":"The medulla controls unconscious activities such as heart rate and breathing. The trap is the cerebral cortex, which handles conscious thought and memory. The cerebellum coordinates muscular activity, and the pituitary is a hormone gland.","w":["The cerebral cortex controls conscious thought, language, memory and intelligence, not automatic body functions.","The cerebellum coordinates muscular activity and balance, not heart rate and breathing.","","The pituitary is an endocrine gland releasing hormones, not the brain region controlling heart rate and breathing."]}
{"id":"gbi-170","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which method lets scientists look at the structure and activity of a living person's brain without surgery?","o":["Putting electrodes onto the exposed surface of the cortex","Examining the brain after the person has died","Testing the person's reaction times","Using an MRI scanner"],"a":3,"x":"MRI scanning gives detailed images of a living person's brain without opening the skull. Electrical stimulation needs the brain to be exposed, and post-mortem examination is only possible after death.","w":["Stimulating the exposed cortex needs the skull opened, so it involves surgery.","Examining the brain after death cannot study a living brain.","Reaction times show how fast someone responds but show nothing of the brain's structure.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-171","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A short-sighted person can see near objects clearly but not distant ones. Where is light from a distant object focused, and which lens corrects this?","o":["In front of the retina; a concave lens","Behind the retina; a concave lens","In front of the retina; a convex lens","Behind the retina; a convex lens"],"a":0,"x":"In myopia, light from distant objects is focused in front of the retina. A concave lens spreads the light slightly first, so the focus moves back onto the retina. Mixing this up with long sight is the common trap.","w":["","Focusing behind the retina is the problem in long sight, not short sight.","A convex lens bends light inwards, making the focus fall even further in front of the retina.","This is long sight (hyperopia) and its correction, the wrong way round for short sight."]}
{"id":"gbi-172","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A person walks from a dim room into bright sunlight. What happens to the pupil, and why?","o":["It gets larger, so more light reaches the retina","It gets smaller, so less light reaches the retina","It gets smaller, so that the lens becomes thinner","It gets larger, so that the lens can focus the light better"],"a":1,"x":"In bright light the iris makes the pupil smaller, reducing the light reaching the retina and protecting it from damage. The trap is linking pupil size to lens shape, which the ciliary muscles control.","w":["A larger pupil is the response to dim light, and too much bright light could damage the retina.","","Pupil size controls how much light enters; the ciliary muscles, not the iris, change the lens shape.","A larger pupil is for dim light, and pupil size only controls how much light enters, not focusing."]}
{"id":"gbi-173","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A long-sighted person cannot focus on close objects. Why does a convex lens in their glasses help?","o":["It bends light outwards, moving the image forward onto the retina","It makes the eye's own lens thinner, so it bends light less","It bends light inwards before it enters the eye, so the image forms on the retina","It shortens the eyeball so the retina is closer to the lens"],"a":2,"x":"In long sight the image of a near object would form behind the retina. A convex lens bends light inwards before it enters the eye, so the eye can focus it onto the retina. Convex lenses do not bend light outwards.","w":["Convex lenses bend light inwards; spreading light outwards would push the focus even further back.","Glasses do not change the eye's own lens, and bending light less would make long sight worse.","","Glasses cannot change the eyeball's length; long sight often happens because the eyeball is too short."]}
{"id":"gbi-174","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which part of the body has receptors that monitor the temperature of the blood?","o":["The pancreas","The thermoregulatory centre in the brain","The thermoregulatory centre in the skin","The kidneys"],"a":1,"x":"The thermoregulatory centre in the brain monitors blood temperature. Separate receptors in the skin report skin temperature to it. The trap is thinking the skin monitors the blood.","w":["The pancreas monitors blood glucose, not temperature, so this mixes up two different types of control.","","The thermoregulatory centre is in the brain. Skin has receptors, but it does not contain the centre.","The kidneys control water and ion levels in the blood, not temperature."]}
{"id":"gbi-175","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Why is it important that core body temperature stays at about 37°C?","o":["Enzymes work best at their optimum temperature, which is about 37°C","Enzymes only work at exactly 37°C and at no other temperature","Body cells make enzymes only when the body is cold","Water in cells would freeze below 37°C"],"a":0,"x":"Enzymes control reactions in the body and have an optimum temperature close to 37°C. Away from it they work more slowly or may become denatured. The trap is saying they stop working at any other temperature.","w":["","Enzymes still work away from the optimum, though more slowly. 'Only at exactly 37°C' is too extreme.","Cells make enzymes all the time. Temperature affects how well enzymes work, not whether they are made.","Water freezes at 0°C, not 37°C. This confuses freezing with enzyme activity."]}
{"id":"gbi-176","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":2,"q":"How does sweating help to cool a person down on a hot day?","o":["Sweat is colder than the body, so it cools the skin directly","Sweating makes the blood vessels in the skin constrict","Sweating stops the muscles releasing heat by respiration","Energy is transferred from the skin to evaporate the sweat"],"a":3,"x":"Sweat evaporating from the skin takes energy from the body, which cools it. The trap is thinking sweat cools because it starts out cold. It leaves the body at body temperature.","w":["Sweat comes from the body at about body temperature. It cools by evaporating, not by being cold.","Constriction reduces heat loss and happens when cold. Sweating does not cause it.","Respiration in muscles is not stopped by sweating. Cooling comes from evaporation.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-177","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":2,"q":"After a long run on a hot day, a girl's skin looks red. Which statement best explains why this helps her?","o":["Blood vessels constrict, so less blood reaches the skin and less heat is lost","Blood vessels dilate, so more blood flows near the skin and more heat is lost","Blood vessels move closer to the skin surface, so heat escapes more easily","The body makes extra blood, which carries heat away in the sweat"],"a":1,"x":"Skin blood vessels dilate when hot. More blood flows through them near the surface, so more heat is transferred to the surroundings. The trap is saying vessels move closer to the skin. They do not move.","w":["Constriction would reduce blood flow and heat loss. It is the response to being cold, not hot.","","Blood vessels stay where they are. They get wider (dilate), which lets more blood flow near the skin.","The body does not make extra blood in this response. Heat loss depends on blood flow through dilated vessels."]}
{"id":"gbi-178","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A boy jumps into cold water. His skin temperature falls fast, but his blood is still 37°C. Which statement is correct?","o":["No response until blood temperature falls, as only blood is monitored","Skin receptors cause shivering directly, without any signal to the brain","Skin receptors send impulses to the brain, so responses start before blood cools","Skin receptors release a hormone into the blood that makes the muscles shiver"],"a":2,"x":"Skin receptors send nervous impulses to the thermoregulatory centre. The centre can then trigger responses such as shivering early, before core temperature falls. The trap is thinking only blood temperature is monitored.","w":["Skin temperature is also detected and reported to the brain, so responses can start before the blood cools.","Skin receptors do not cause shivering themselves. The thermoregulatory centre coordinates the response.","","Skin receptors send nervous impulses, not hormones. Shivering is triggered by nerve impulses to muscles."]}
{"id":"gbi-179","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which gland is often called the 'master gland' because its hormones control the release of hormones from other glands?","o":["Thyroid gland","Pituitary gland","Adrenal gland","Pancreas"],"a":1,"x":"The pituitary gland, in the brain, makes hormones that act on other glands. The trap is picking a gland that makes a well-known hormone. Those glands are controlled by the pituitary rather than controlling the others.","w":["The thyroid makes thyroxine for metabolic rate and is itself controlled by the pituitary, so it is not the master gland.","","The adrenal glands make adrenaline for fight or flight but do not control the release of hormones from other glands.","The pancreas makes insulin and glucagon to control blood glucose but does not control other glands."]}
{"id":"gbi-180","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly compares hormones with nerve impulses?","o":["Hormones travel as electrical impulses, so they act very quickly","Hormones travel in the blood, so they act quickly and only briefly","Hormones travel in the blood, so they act more slowly and for longer","Hormones travel along neurones, so they act more slowly and for longer"],"a":2,"x":"Hormones are chemicals carried in the blood plasma. This is slower than an electrical impulse, but the effect usually lasts longer. The trap is mixing up the speed and the route of the two systems.","w":["Electrical impulses belong to the nervous system, whereas hormones are chemical messengers carried in the blood.","Blood transport is slower than a nerve impulse, and hormone effects usually last longer rather than being brief.","","Neurones carry nerve impulses, whereas hormones are carried in the blood."]}
{"id":"gbi-181","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A person with type 1 diabetes eats a large pasta meal but does not inject insulin. What happens to their blood glucose, and why?","o":["It rises, because little glucose moves into cells or is stored as glycogen","It falls, because the pancreas releases too much glucagon","It stays normal, because the liver removes the extra glucose","It rises, because the pancreas makes too much insulin"],"a":0,"x":"In type 1 diabetes the pancreas makes too little insulin. Without insulin, glucose from the meal is not moved into cells or stored as glycogen, so blood glucose rises. The trap is thinking the pancreas makes too much insulin.","w":["","Glucagon raises blood glucose rather than lowering it, and glucagon is not the problem in type 1 diabetes.","The liver only stores glucose as glycogen when insulin is present, so without insulin glucose stays in the blood.","Type 1 diabetes means the pancreas makes too little insulin, not too much."]}
{"id":"gbi-182","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Several hours after eating, a person's blood glucose falls below the normal level. Which response brings it back up?","o":["Pancreas releases insulin, so the liver turns glycogen into glucose","Pancreas releases glucagon, so the liver turns glucose into glycogen","Pancreas releases insulin, so glucose moves from the blood into cells","Pancreas releases glucagon, so the liver turns glycogen into glucose"],"a":3,"x":"Low blood glucose makes the pancreas release glucagon. Glucagon makes the liver convert stored glycogen into glucose, which enters the blood. The trap is swapping insulin and glucagon, or swapping glucose and glycogen.","w":["Insulin lowers blood glucose and does not make the liver release glucose, so the hormone is wrong.","Glucagon is right, but turning glucose into glycogen would lower blood glucose rather than raise it.","Insulin moves glucose into cells and lowers blood glucose, so it would make a low level even lower.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-183","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A person with type 1 diabetes injects their usual insulin dose but then skips their meal. What is the most likely effect on their blood glucose?","o":["It rises, because insulin makes the liver release glucose","It falls too low, because insulin still moves glucose into cells and stores","It stays the same, because insulin only works straight after eating","It rises, because there is no food to use up the insulin"],"a":1,"x":"Insulin lowers blood glucose whether or not food has been eaten. With no meal to supply glucose, the level can drop too low. The trap is thinking insulin raises glucose or only works after food.","w":["Insulin lowers blood glucose; it is glucagon that makes the liver release glucose.","","Injected insulin works whenever it is in the blood, so it still lowers blood glucose without a meal.","Insulin is not 'used up' by food; it acts on glucose already in the blood and lowers it."]}
{"id":"gbi-184","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Urea is a waste product that the kidneys remove from the blood. Where is urea made, and from what?","o":["In the kidneys, from excess glucose","In the liver, from excess amino acids","In the kidneys, from excess amino acids","In the liver, from excess fat"],"a":1,"x":"The liver breaks down excess amino acids and makes urea. The urea goes into the blood and the kidneys remove it. The trap is thinking the kidneys make urea; they only filter it out.","w":["The kidneys do not make urea, and urea comes from amino acids, not glucose.","","The kidneys only filter urea out of the blood; the liver makes it.","Urea comes from breaking down amino acids; fat contains no nitrogen, so it cannot form urea."]}
{"id":"gbi-185","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does dialysis fluid contain glucose at the same concentration as healthy blood plasma?","o":["So glucose diffuses out of the blood with the urea","So the patient gains extra glucose for respiration","So the fluid is denser and pulls urea out faster","So there is no overall movement of glucose out of the blood"],"a":3,"x":"If the glucose concentration is equal on both sides, there is no net diffusion. The patient keeps their glucose, while urea (absent from the fluid) diffuses out. Dialysis must not remove useful substances.","w":["Glucose is a useful substance; the aim is to keep it in the blood, not remove it.","Equal concentrations give no net gain either; the aim is to stop glucose being lost.","Urea moves out because its concentration is lower in the fluid, not because of density.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-186","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":2,"q":"On a hot day, Mia sweats a lot and does not drink. What happens in her body?","o":["More ADH released; more water reabsorbed; small volume of dark urine","Less ADH released; more water reabsorbed; small volume of dark urine","More ADH released; less water reabsorbed; large volume of pale urine","Less ADH released; less water reabsorbed; large volume of pale urine"],"a":0,"x":"Sweating lowers the water level in her blood. The pituitary gland releases more ADH. This makes the kidney tubules reabsorb more water, so a small volume of concentrated urine is made.","w":["","ADH rises, not falls, when the blood is short of water, and less ADH would not increase reabsorption.","This mixes up ADH's effect: more ADH causes more water reabsorption, not less.","Low water in the blood triggers more ADH, not less; this would lose even more water."]}
{"id":"gbi-187","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A patient with kidney failure has a kidney transplant. Why must they take drugs for the rest of their life?","o":["To kill bacteria that live on the donor kidney","To stop the new kidney making too much urine","To stop the immune system attacking the donor kidney","To replace the ADH that the donor kidney cannot make"],"a":2,"x":"The donor kidney has different antigens, so the patient's immune system may treat it as foreign and reject it. Immunosuppressant drugs reduce this. The trap is thinking the drugs help the kidney work.","w":["The drugs are not antibiotics; the problem is the immune response, not bacteria on the kidney.","The drugs do not control urine volume; they prevent rejection by the immune system.","","ADH is made by the pituitary gland, not the kidney, so no replacement ADH is needed."]}
{"id":"gbi-188","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":3,"q":"In a dialysis machine, fresh dialysis fluid keeps flowing past the membrane. Why does this help to remove urea from the patient's blood?","o":["It warms the blood so urea moves out by active transport","It keeps the urea concentration in the fluid low, so urea keeps diffusing out","It adds urea to the fluid so both sides reach the same concentration","It pumps urea across the membrane using energy from the machine"],"a":1,"x":"Urea moves by diffusion, from high concentration in blood to low in the fluid. Fresh fluid keeps the urea concentration low, so the gradient stays steep. The trap is thinking the machine pumps urea.","w":["Urea crosses by diffusion, not active transport, and warming is not the purpose of the flow.","","Matching concentrations would stop net diffusion, so urea would stay in the blood.","Urea moves by diffusion down a concentration gradient; the machine does not pump it."]}
{"id":"gbi-189","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) is part of the control of the menstrual cycle. Which gland makes FSH?","o":["The ovary","The pituitary gland","The uterus","The pancreas"],"a":1,"x":"FSH is made in the pituitary gland in the brain. It travels in the blood to the ovaries. The trap is the ovary, which makes oestrogen and progesterone, not FSH.","w":["The ovary is where FSH acts and it makes oestrogen, but FSH itself comes from the pituitary gland.","","The uterus is where the lining builds up, and it does not make FSH.","The pancreas makes insulin and glucagon to control blood glucose, not hormones for the menstrual cycle."]}
{"id":"gbi-190","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which contraceptive method also helps to protect against sexually transmitted infections (STIs)?","o":["The contraceptive injection","Oral contraceptive pills","Male condoms","A progesterone implant"],"a":2,"x":"A condom is a barrier. It stops semen and body fluids passing between partners, so it also reduces the spread of STIs. Hormonal methods only change the cycle and give no protection against infection.","w":["The injection releases progesterone to prevent pregnancy but is not a barrier, so STIs can still pass on.","Pills use hormones to prevent pregnancy but are not a barrier, so STIs can still pass on.","","An implant slowly releases progesterone and is hormonal, not a barrier, so it gives no STI protection."]}
{"id":"gbi-191","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Some contraceptive pills contain oestrogen. How does this oestrogen help to prevent pregnancy?","o":["It inhibits FSH production, so no eggs mature in the ovaries","It stimulates LH production, so the egg is released too early","It makes the uterus lining break down every week","It kills sperm as they enter the vagina"],"a":0,"x":"Oestrogen in the pill inhibits the release of FSH. Without FSH, eggs do not mature, so none can be released. The trap is assuming the pill's oestrogen triggers egg release, as natural oestrogen helps do.","w":["","This confuses oestrogen's natural role in triggering LH with the pill, which stops eggs maturing instead.","The pill does not make the lining break down weekly; it works by stopping eggs maturing.","Killing sperm is the job of spermicide, not the hormones in the pill."]}
{"id":"gbi-192","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A woman has a regular 28-day cycle. Day 1 of her period is 3 March. Ovulation usually happens on day 14 of the cycle. On which date is day 14?","o":["14 March","17 March","31 March","16 March"],"a":3,"x":"If 3 March is day 1, then day 14 is 16 March, about when the egg is released. The traps are using 14 as the date, or adding 14 to 3, which treats 3 March as day 0.","w":["This uses 14 as the calendar date, but day 1 of the cycle is 3 March, not 1 March.","This adds 14 to 3, which wrongly counts 3 March as day 0 instead of day 1.","31 March is day 29, when the next period would start, not ovulation.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-193","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":3,"q":"The first contraceptive pills had high doses of oestrogen. Modern pills have lower doses. Which statement best explains this change?","o":["Lower doses are needed because oestrogen stimulates FSH production","Lower doses still inhibit FSH so no eggs mature, with fewer side effects","High doses stopped the uterus lining from ever forming","Lower doses let eggs mature but stop sperm reaching them"],"a":1,"x":"High oestrogen doses caused unpleasant side effects. Lower doses still inhibit FSH, so eggs still do not mature. The trap is thinking a lower dose must work in a different way, or be less effective.","w":["This reverses the effect, because oestrogen in the pill inhibits FSH rather than stimulating it.","","The pill does not work by stopping the lining forming; side effects were the reason for lowering doses.","Lower-dose pills still stop eggs maturing; blocking sperm is how barrier methods work."]}
{"id":"gbi-194","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.6","lv":3,"q":"A woman's FSH level is too low, so her eggs do not mature. A fertility drug containing FSH and LH is given. What do these two hormones do in her body?","o":["FSH stimulates egg release; LH makes the eggs mature","FSH thickens the uterus lining; LH stops more eggs maturing","FSH makes eggs mature in the ovary; LH stimulates egg release","FSH and LH both stop eggs being released until after fertilisation"],"a":2,"x":"FSH makes eggs mature in the ovary and stimulates oestrogen production. LH stimulates ovulation, the release of a mature egg. The common trap is swapping the roles of FSH and LH.","w":["This swaps the roles, because FSH causes egg maturation and LH causes egg release.","Oestrogen and progesterone, not FSH, build and maintain the lining, and LH does not stop eggs maturing.","","The fertility drug is meant to cause egg maturation and release, not block ovulation like a contraceptive."]}
{"id":"gbi-195","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.6","lv":3,"q":"Before IVF, a woman is given FSH and LH for several days. Why is this done before her eggs are collected?","o":["To fertilise her eggs inside her oviducts before they are removed","To stimulate several eggs to mature so that more can be collected","To stop her immune system from rejecting the embryos later","To thicken her uterus lining so that sperm can be injected into it"],"a":1,"x":"FSH and LH stimulate the ovaries to mature several eggs, so more can be collected. Fertilisation then happens in the lab. A common trap is thinking the hormones cause fertilisation or prevent rejection.","w":["In IVF, fertilisation happens in the lab, and FSH and LH do not cause fertilisation.","","FSH and LH do not affect the immune system; their role is to make eggs mature.","In IVF, eggs are fertilised in the lab and embryos are inserted, so sperm are not injected into the uterus."]}
{"id":"gbi-196","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.6","lv":3,"q":"A couple ask why their IVF clinic will only put one or two embryos into the uterus, not six, to improve their chances. What is the best reason?","o":["Extra embryos would be rejected by the mother's immune system as foreign","Extra embryos would stop the uterus lining from forming properly","Embryos from the same batch have identical genes, so more would not help","It raises the risk of multiple births, which is risky for mother and babies"],"a":3,"x":"Putting back many embryos could lead to a multiple pregnancy, which carries health risks for the mother and babies. Clinics balance a higher chance of success against this risk. The trap is thinking rejection is the reason.","w":["Embryos are not limited because of immune rejection; the real concern is the risk of multiple births.","The lining is built up by hormones before transfer, and more embryos do not stop it forming.","Each embryo comes from a different egg and sperm, so they have different genes.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-197","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.7","lv":3,"q":"A student hears a sudden loud bang and feels frightened. Adrenaline is released. Which statement best explains how adrenaline prepares the body for 'fight or flight'?","o":["Adrenaline makes muscles store glucose as glycogen, ready for use later","Adrenaline lowers the heart rate so less energy is wasted by the heart","Adrenaline raises heart rate, sending more oxygen and glucose to muscles","Adrenaline makes the thyroid release thyroxine, which stops the body responding"],"a":2,"x":"Adrenaline from the adrenal glands raises heart rate. More blood delivers oxygen and glucose to muscles and brain, so more respiration releases energy. The trap is thinking the body saves energy rather than preparing to use it.","w":["This is the opposite; adrenaline makes glucose available for immediate use, not stored for later.","Heart rate rises, not falls; the body needs faster blood flow, not energy saving.","","Adrenaline does not act through thyroxine; thyroxine controls metabolic rate and is not part of fight or flight."]}
{"id":"gbi-198","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.7","lv":3,"q":"A person's blood thyroxine level rises above normal. Which describes how the pituitary gland and thyroid gland bring it back to normal by negative feedback?","o":["The pituitary releases more TSH, so the thyroid releases less thyroxine","The pituitary releases less TSH, so the thyroid releases less thyroxine","The pituitary releases less TSH, so the thyroid releases more thyroxine","The thyroid releases more TSH, so the pituitary releases less thyroxine"],"a":1,"x":"High thyroxine inhibits TSH release from the pituitary. Less TSH means the thyroid is stimulated less, so it releases less thyroxine and the level falls. The trap is mixing up which gland makes TSH or what TSH does.","w":["More TSH would stimulate the thyroid to release more thyroxine, not less.","","Less TSH means less stimulation of the thyroid, so thyroxine release falls, not rises.","Glands are swapped; the pituitary makes TSH and the thyroid makes thyroxine."]}
{"id":"gbi-199","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.1","lv":1,"q":"What is a tropism in a plant?","o":["A fast movement caused by nerve impulses","A growth response towards or away from a stimulus","A chemical made in the leaves to store food","A change in the number of stomata in the dark"],"a":1,"x":"A tropism is a plant growth response to a stimulus such as light or gravity. The direction of growth depends on the direction of the stimulus. Plants have no nerves, so hormones such as auxin control it.","w":["Plants have no nerves, so this confuses slow, hormone-controlled plant growth with animal reflexes.","","A tropism is a growth response, not a food store, so this mixes it up with photosynthesis products.","Stomata number is not a tropism, which is directional growth towards or away from a stimulus."]}
{"id":"gbi-200","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A seedling root is placed horizontally. Where does auxin collect, and why does the root grow downwards?","o":["Upper side; cells elongate more, so the root bends down","Lower side; cells elongate more, so the root bends up","Lower side; elongation is slowed there, so the root bends down","Upper side; elongation is slowed there, so the root bends up"],"a":2,"x":"Auxin collects on the lower side due to gravity. In roots it slows cell elongation, unlike in shoots. The upper side grows longer, so the root bends downwards. The trap is assuming auxin always speeds growth.","w":["Gravity makes auxin collect on the lower side of the root, not the upper side.","Auxin is on the lower side, but in roots it slows elongation, so this uses the shoot effect.","","Auxin collects on the lower side, and roots grow towards gravity, not away from it."]}
{"id":"gbi-201","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A thin foil cap is put over the tip of a shoot. The shoot is then lit from the right only. What happens?","o":["It grows straight up, because the tip cannot detect the light","It bends to the right, because auxin still collects on the left","It bends to the left, because the foil makes the tip warmer","It stops growing, because the shoot needs light on the tip"],"a":0,"x":"The tip detects the light and makes the auxin. With the tip covered, auxin stays spread evenly, so both sides elongate equally and the shoot grows straight up. It does not stop growing.","w":["","Auxin only moves to the shaded side if the tip detects light, and the foil stops this.","Bending depends on uneven auxin distribution, not on temperature, and foil causes no bend away.","Auxin is still made so the shoot keeps growing, which confuses growth with direction of growth."]}
{"id":"gbi-202","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.1","lv":3,"q":"Seedlings are slowly rotated on a clinostat in the dark. Their roots grow straight instead of bending down. Why?","o":["Rotating the seedlings removes gravity completely","Rotation destroys the auxin in the root tips","Roots only bend in response to light, not to gravity","Gravity acts on all sides in turn, so auxin spreads evenly"],"a":3,"x":"Gravity still acts, but slow rotation means every side faces down equally often. Auxin does not build up on one side, so growth is even and the root stays straight. The trap is thinking gravity is switched off.","w":["Rotation cannot remove gravity; it just makes gravity act on every side in turn.","Rotation does not destroy auxin; the auxin is still made but spread evenly.","Roots do respond to gravity (gravitropism), which is why they grow downwards even in the dark.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-203","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.1","lv":2,"q":"In required practical 8, a student tests how light direction affects shoot growth. Which measurement best shows, in numbers, how much the shoots respond?","o":["Describe how bent each shoot looks","Measure each shoot's angle from vertical with a protractor","Count the leaves on each shoot","Weigh each shoot after the experiment"],"a":1,"x":"Measuring the angle of bending gives numbers that can be compared and averaged. Describing the bend is only qualitative. Leaf number and mass do not show the direction of growth.","w":["A description is qualitative, not numerical, so it is hard to compare fairly between seedlings.","","Leaf count does not show how far the shoot has bent towards the light.","Mass shows the amount of growth, not its direction, so it cannot show bending."]}
{"id":"gbi-204","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.2","lv":3,"q":"Bananas are picked green and shipped far away. The grower wants them ripe in the shop on a set day. Which plan uses plant hormones correctly?","o":["Pick them green, spray with gibberellin on the ship to ripen them on the way","Pick them ripe, then spray with auxin to stop them ripening on the ship","Pick them green, keep them cool on the ship, then treat with ethene just before sale","Pick them green, keep them with extra ethene on the ship so they ripen early"],"a":2,"x":"Ethene makes fruit ripen. Green fruit is firmer and survives transport. Growers delay ripening by keeping fruit cool, then add ethene near the shops. The trap is mixing up which hormone does which job.","w":["Gibberellins end seed dormancy, promote flowering and increase fruit size, but ethene is the hormone that ripens fruit.","Auxin does not stop ripening, and ripe fruit is soft so it would bruise and spoil during shipping.","","Extra ethene on the ship would ripen the fruit too early, so it would bruise and rot before reaching the shop."]}
{"id":"gbi-205","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A lawn is sprayed with a weedkiller containing a synthetic auxin. The broad-leaved daisies die but the narrow-leaved grass survives. What is the best explanation?","o":["The auxin makes the broad-leaved weeds grow in an uncontrolled way, so they die","The auxin stops cell division in the weeds' roots, so they cannot take in water","The auxin blocks photosynthesis in broad-leaved plants but not in narrow-leaved plants","The auxin kills every plant it touches, but grass grows back from its stem tip"],"a":0,"x":"Synthetic auxins are selective weedkillers. Broad-leaved weeds respond with fast, abnormal growth and die. Grass is not affected in the same way. The trap is thinking auxin simply stops growth.","w":["","Auxin does not stop cell division; it causes abnormal, uncontrolled growth, so this reverses its effect.","Auxin weedkillers do not work by blocking photosynthesis; they disrupt normal growth in broad-leaved weeds.","Auxin weedkillers are selective, so grass is not harmed and does not need to regrow."]}
{"id":"gbi-206","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.2","lv":3,"q":"Which row matches each plant hormone to a use by growers?","o":["Rooting cuttings: gibberellin; larger fruit: auxin; ripening fruit: ethene","Rooting cuttings: auxin; larger fruit: ethene; ripening fruit: gibberellin","Rooting cuttings: ethene; larger fruit: gibberellin; ripening fruit: auxin","Rooting cuttings: auxin; larger fruit: gibberellin; ripening fruit: ethene"],"a":3,"x":"Auxin in rooting powder helps cuttings grow roots. Gibberellins can increase fruit size. Ethene controls ripening. The trap is swapping the three hormones, so learn one job for each.","w":["Gibberellin and auxin are swapped, because auxin is used in rooting powder and gibberellin increases fruit size.","Ethene and gibberellin are swapped, because ethene ripens fruit and gibberellin increases fruit size.","Ethene and auxin are swapped, because auxin is used in rooting powder and ethene ripens fruit.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-207","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about asexual reproduction is correct?","o":["Offspring form when two gametes from two parents fuse","Cells divide by meiosis to produce the new offspring","Needs one parent, and offspring are genetically identical to it","Offspring show more variation than from sexual reproduction"],"a":2,"x":"Asexual reproduction needs one parent and uses mitosis. There is no fusion of gametes, so offspring are clones. The trap is mixing it up with sexual reproduction.","w":["Fusion of gametes happens in sexual reproduction; asexual reproduction uses no gametes.","Meiosis makes gametes for sexual reproduction, but asexual reproduction uses mitosis.","","Asexual offspring are clones, so they show less variation than offspring from sexual reproduction, not more."]}
{"id":"gbi-208","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":1,"q":"In humans, where does meiosis take place?","o":["In all body cells while the body is growing","In skin cells when a cut is repaired","In a fertilised egg just after it forms","In the ovaries and the testes"],"a":3,"x":"Meiosis only happens in the reproductive organs, the ovaries and testes, to make gametes. The common trap is thinking it is used for growth or repair, which use mitosis.","w":["Growth happens by mitosis, which makes identical body cells, while meiosis only makes gametes.","Repairing a cut uses mitosis to copy cells, not meiosis.","A fertilised egg divides by mitosis to form an embryo; meiosis happened earlier, when the gametes were made.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-209","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A farm grows thousands of banana plants from cuttings. A new fungal disease arrives. Why might the whole crop be wiped out?","o":["The plants are clones, so if one can catch it, all can","The plants have no genes that could help fight any disease","Asexual reproduction causes a new mutation in every plant","Plants from cuttings grow too fast to build any defences"],"a":0,"x":"Cuttings are clones, so there is no genetic variation. If one plant is open to the disease, they all are. Sexual reproduction gives variation, so some plants might survive.","w":["","Clones may carry some defence genes from the parent; the problem is that every plant has the same genes.","Asexual reproduction copies the parent's DNA by mitosis; it does not cause a mutation in every plant.","Speed of growth is not the reason; the key point is that clones have no genetic variation."]}
{"id":"gbi-210","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The malaria parasite is a protist. How does it reproduce?","o":["Sexually in humans and asexually in mosquitoes","Sexually in both humans and mosquitoes","Asexually in humans and sexually in mosquitoes","Asexually in both humans and mosquitoes"],"a":2,"x":"The malaria protist reproduces asexually inside the human host and sexually inside the mosquito. The trap is swapping the two hosts, or thinking it uses only one type of reproduction.","w":["This swaps the hosts, because the sexual stage happens in the mosquito and the asexual stage in humans.","The parasite uses both types, reproducing asexually in humans rather than sexually.","","The parasite uses both types, because sexual reproduction happens in the mosquito."]}
{"id":"gbi-211","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":3,"q":"A species has 8 chromosomes in each body cell. How many chromosomes are in a gamete, in the fertilised egg, and in a body cell of the embryo after mitosis?","o":["4; 4; 8","4; 8; 8","8; 16; 16","4; 8; 4"],"a":1,"x":"Meiosis halves the number, so gametes have 4. Fertilisation joins two gametes, giving 8. Mitosis makes identical copies, so embryo body cells also have 8. The trap is thinking mitosis halves the number.","w":["Fertilisation joins two gametes, so the fertilised egg has 4 + 4 = 8, not 4.","","Gametes have half the body cell number, so 4, not 8; this forgets that meiosis halves the chromosomes.","Mitosis makes identical cells, so embryo body cells keep 8 chromosomes, and only meiosis halves the number."]}
{"id":"gbi-212","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":1,"q":"What is meant by the genome of an organism?","o":["All the genes that code for proteins, but none of the other DNA","The entire genetic material of that organism","All the different proteins made in the organism's cells","The pair of sex chromosomes found in each body cell"],"a":1,"x":"The genome is all of an organism's genetic material. It includes genes and the non-coding DNA between them. The trap is thinking it only means the genes that code for proteins.","w":["The genome also includes non-coding DNA, so it is not just the protein-coding genes.","","The genome is genetic material (DNA), not the proteins. Proteins are made using the genes.","Sex chromosomes are only one pair of the 23 pairs; the genome is all the DNA."]}
{"id":"gbi-213","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":1,"q":"DNA is a polymer made of many repeating units called nucleotides. Which option correctly describes what one nucleotide contains?","o":["A sugar, a phosphate group and two bases","Three bases that together code for one amino acid","A sugar, an amino acid and one base","A sugar, a phosphate group and one base"],"a":3,"x":"Each nucleotide has one sugar, one phosphate group and one of four bases. Three bases in a row code for an amino acid, but that is a sequence of nucleotides, not one nucleotide.","w":["Each nucleotide has only one base attached, not two.","This describes a triplet code for an amino acid, which is made of three nucleotides, not one.","Amino acids make up proteins, not DNA. A nucleotide has a phosphate group, not an amino acid.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-214","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Some DNA does not code for proteins. A mutation happens in a non-coding region right next to a gene. What is a possible effect of this mutation?","o":["It may change whether the gene is switched on, so the amount of protein made changes","It changes the order of amino acids in the protein that the gene codes for","It changes the number of chromosomes in the cell","It has no possible effect, because non-coding DNA has no function"],"a":0,"x":"Some non-coding DNA helps to switch genes on and off. A mutation there can change how much protein is made. The trap is thinking non-coding DNA is useless or that only coding DNA matters.","w":["","The gene's own base sequence has not changed, so the amino acid order stays the same.","A change in the DNA base sequence does not alter how many chromosomes the cell has.","Some non-coding DNA controls genes, so a mutation there can matter. 'Junk' is the misconception."]}
{"id":"gbi-215","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":2,"q":"The Human Genome Project worked out the order of bases in human DNA. Which is a use of the results?","o":["Showing that every human has exactly the same DNA sequence","Proving that the environment has no effect on any disease","Searching for genes linked to inherited disorders","Replacing faulty genes in every patient as a standard treatment"],"a":2,"x":"Knowing the genome lets scientists search for genes linked to inherited disorders and understand them better. It also helps trace human migration. The trap is overclaiming: people differ, and gene replacement is not standard.","w":["People's DNA sequences differ, which is one cause of variation between individuals.","Many diseases are affected by both genes and the environment. The project did not show otherwise.","","Gene replacement is not a standard treatment. The project gave information, not an automatic cure."]}
{"id":"gbi-216","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.4","lv":3,"q":"Mutation X changes one base in a gene to a different base. Mutation Y deletes one base near the start of the same gene. Bases are read in groups of three. Which statement is most likely true?","o":["X changes more amino acids, as the new base appears in every group of three","Y can change many amino acids, as all later bases are read in new groups of three","X and Y each change exactly one amino acid, as each affects one base","Y changes no amino acids, as a deleted base is not part of any group of three"],"a":1,"x":"Deleting a base shifts the grouping of every base after it, so many amino acids can change. Swapping one base changes only one group of three, so at most one amino acid. The trap is counting only the one base altered.","w":["A swapped base affects only the one group of three it sits in, so at most one amino acid changes.","","A deletion shifts every later group of three, so it can change many amino acids, not just one.","The deleted base was part of a group of three. Removing it changes the groups that follow."]}
{"id":"gbi-217","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":1,"q":"In pea plants, the allele for tall (T) is dominant to the allele for short (t). A plant has the alleles Tt. Which description is correct?","o":["Homozygous, with the short phenotype","Homozygous, with the tall phenotype","Heterozygous, with the tall phenotype","Heterozygous, with the short phenotype"],"a":2,"x":"Tt has two different alleles, so it is heterozygous. The dominant allele T is expressed even with t present, so the phenotype is tall. The trap is thinking the recessive allele shows.","w":["Tt has two different alleles so it is heterozygous, and the dominant T makes the plant tall, not short.","Tt is not homozygous because its two alleles differ; only TT or tt are homozygous.","","The plant is heterozygous, but the dominant T is expressed, so it is tall, not short."]}
{"id":"gbi-218","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about sex determination in humans is correct?","o":["Every egg cell carries an X chromosome","Half of all egg cells carry a Y chromosome","Every sperm cell carries a Y chromosome","The egg cell decides if a baby is male or female"],"a":0,"x":"Females are XX, so every egg carries an X. Males are XY, so half of sperm carry X and half carry Y. The sperm therefore decides the sex of the baby.","w":["","Females are XX with no Y chromosome, so no egg can carry Y; this confuses eggs with sperm.","Males are XY, so only half of sperm carry Y and the other half carry X.","Every egg carries X, so it is the sperm, carrying X or Y, that decides the baby's sex."]}
{"id":"gbi-219","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":2,"q":"Polydactyly (extra fingers or toes) is caused by a dominant allele, D. A parent with the alleles Dd has a child with a parent who has the alleles dd. What is the chance the child has polydactyly?","o":["25%","75%","100%","50%"],"a":3,"x":"Dd × dd gives Dd, Dd, dd and dd. Two of the four outcomes have the dominant allele D, so the chance is 50%. The trap is forgetting the parent is heterozygous.","w":["25% is the chance of an ff child from Ff × Ff, not the result of a Dd × dd cross.","75% comes from a Dd × Dd cross, but here one parent is dd and cannot pass on D.","100% wrongly assumes D is always passed on, but a Dd parent passes on D only half the time.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-220","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":2,"q":"Two parents both have polydactyly, caused by a dominant allele (D). Their first child does not have polydactyly. What can you say about the parents?","o":["Both parents are homozygous dominant (DD)","Both parents are heterozygous (Dd)","One parent is homozygous recessive (dd)","One parent is DD and the other is Dd"],"a":1,"x":"A child without polydactyly is dd. It must get one d from each parent. The parents have the disorder, so each must also have D. Both are Dd. The trap is mixing up dominant and recessive.","w":["A DD parent always passes on D, so their child would have to have polydactyly.","","A dd parent would not have polydactyly, but both parents do have it.","A DD parent could only pass on D, so the child could not be dd and unaffected."]}
{"id":"gbi-221","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":3,"q":"Two parents are both carriers of cystic fibrosis (Ff). What is the chance that a child of theirs is a girl with cystic fibrosis?","o":["1 in 8","1 in 2","1 in 4","1 in 16"],"a":0,"x":"The chance of cystic fibrosis (ff) is 1 in 4. The chance of a girl is 1 in 2. These are independent, so multiply: 1/4 × 1/2 = 1/8. The trap is ignoring one of the two conditions.","w":["","1 in 2 is only the chance of a girl and ignores the chance of inheriting ff.","1 in 4 is only the chance of ff and ignores that the child must also be a girl.","1 in 16 multiplies 1/4 by 1/4, but the chance of a girl is 1/2, not 1/4."]}
{"id":"gbi-222","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which characteristic of a person is caused only by the environment and not by their genes?","o":["Eye colour","A scar on the knee from a fall","Blood group","Natural hair colour"],"a":1,"x":"A scar comes from an injury, so it is an environmental effect and is not inherited. Eye colour, blood group and natural hair colour are controlled by genes passed on from parents.","w":["Eye colour is controlled by inherited genes, so it is not caused by the environment alone.","","Blood group is inherited through genes, so the environment does not cause it.","Natural hair colour is inherited from parents. Only dyeing hair would be an environmental effect."]}
{"id":"gbi-223","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Mutations happen all the time. Which statement about their effect on phenotype is correct?","o":["Most have no effect; very rarely one gives a new phenotype","Every mutation produces a new phenotype","Mutations always make an organism less likely to survive","Most give a phenotype better suited to the environment"],"a":0,"x":"Mutations occur continuously, but most have no effect on phenotype. Very rarely one gives a new phenotype, which may suit a changed environment. The trap is thinking every mutation changes the organism.","w":["","Most mutations do not change the phenotype at all, so not every one gives a new phenotype.","Most mutations have no effect, and a rare one can even help survival.","Useful mutations are very rare; most mutations have no effect on the phenotype."]}
{"id":"gbi-224","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A group of beetles is split into two populations living on separate islands. Over many generations they change. What must happen for the beetles to be called two new species?","o":["They can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring","Beetles on one island become a different colour","The two populations live in different places","The two populations have different numbers of offspring"],"a":0,"x":"A new species forms when populations become so different that they cannot interbreed to produce fertile offspring. Different colour or place alone is not enough, because such beetles could still interbreed.","w":["","Colour difference is just variation. Beetles of different colours can often still interbreed, so they remain one species.","Living apart helps cause speciation, but separation alone does not make a new species.","Different numbers of offspring do not show that the populations cannot interbreed."]}
{"id":"gbi-225","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Identical twins grow up in different towns. At age 30 one weighs 62 kg and the other weighs 70 kg. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["Identical twins have different genes, so the difference is genetic","Mutations always cause weight differences between twins","Same genes, so the difference is mainly environmental, e.g. diet","Weight is controlled only by genes, so the measurements are wrong"],"a":2,"x":"Identical twins have the same genes. A difference in weight is therefore most likely caused by the environment, such as diet and exercise. The trap is thinking all characteristics are controlled by genes alone.","w":["Identical twins come from one fertilised egg, so they have the same genes.","Mutations are random and rare. They do not always cause differences, so 'always' is wrong.","","Weight is affected by both genes and environment, so a difference is not a mistake in measuring."]}
{"id":"gbi-226","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":3,"q":"A student says: 'Bacteria became resistant to an antibiotic because they were exposed to it.' Which is the best correction?","o":["The antibiotic caused the bacteria to mutate so they became resistant","Each bacterium slowly got used to the antibiotic during its life","Some already had a random resistance mutation; they survived and bred","Resistant bacteria changed their DNA on purpose to survive"],"a":2,"x":"Mutations are random and happen before exposure. The antibiotic does not cause resistance. It selects bacteria that already have the allele, which survive, reproduce and pass it on. The trap is thinking the environment causes the mutation.","w":["Antibiotics do not cause the useful mutation. It arises randomly and the antibiotic only selects for it.","Individual bacteria do not adapt in their lifetime and pass it on. This is a Lamarck-style mistake.","","Mutations are random, not deliberate. Bacteria cannot choose to change their DNA."]}
{"id":"gbi-227","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A farmer wants cows that make more milk. What is the first step in selective breeding?","o":["Choose cows and bulls from high-milk lines to breed together","Add a milk gene from another species into cow eggs","Breed any cows and bulls that live in the same field","Wait for a mutation to make the cows produce more milk"],"a":0,"x":"Selective breeding starts with choosing parents that have the desired feature. Adding genes from another species is genetic engineering, not selective breeding.","w":["","That is genetic engineering, which changes genes directly; selective breeding only chooses the parents.","Random breeding is not selection; parents must be chosen for the feature wanted.","Selective breeding does not wait for chance mutations; humans choose which animals breed."]}
{"id":"gbi-228","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an example of selective breeding?","o":["Cutting shoots from a plant to grow identical copies","Moving a gene for vitamin A into rice cells","Breeding wheat with the most disease resistance over many generations","Spraying crops with pesticide to protect them"],"a":2,"x":"Selective breeding means choosing parents with useful features and repeating this over generations. Taking cuttings is cloning, and moving genes is genetic engineering.","w":["This is cloning by cuttings; the copies are genetically identical and no parents are chosen.","This is genetic engineering, because a gene is moved directly into the cells.","","Pesticide protects the crop but does not change which alleles the plants pass on."]}
{"id":"gbi-229","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Farmers grow only one selectively bred wheat variety. All the plants are very similar genetically. Why is this a risk?","o":["The plants will all mutate in the same way each year","A new disease could kill most plants, as few or none may be resistant","The plants will stop making seeds after one generation","The yield will always fall in every new generation"],"a":1,"x":"Selective breeding reduces variation. If a new disease appears, few plants may carry resistance alleles, so a whole crop could be lost. Yield does not always fall.","w":["Mutations are random, so similar plants do not all mutate in the same way.","","Selective breeding does not make plants infertile; the problem is a lack of variation.","Selective breeding usually raises yield; the risk is loss of variation, not a certain fall."]}
{"id":"gbi-230","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares selective breeding with natural selection?","o":["In both, the environment decides which organisms survive to breed","In selective breeding, new alleles are put in by genetic engineering","In natural selection, humans choose which features are passed on","In selective breeding, humans choose which organisms breed, not the environment"],"a":3,"x":"In selective breeding, humans choose the parents for features they want. In natural selection, the environment decides who survives and breeds. Genetic engineering is a separate process.","w":["Only natural selection works this way; in selective breeding, humans choose the parents.","Selective breeding uses alleles already in the population; adding alleles is genetic engineering.","This is reversed; in natural selection the environment decides, not humans.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-231","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.3","lv":3,"q":"One tomato plant has very large fruit but gets disease. Another resists disease but has small fruit. A grower wants both features. What should the grower do?","o":["Breed only large-fruit plants until disease resistance appears","Cross the two plants, then keep breeding offspring that show both features","Cross the two plants once, as all offspring will have both features","Grow the plants in disease-free soil so both features are passed on"],"a":1,"x":"The grower crosses the two plants, picks offspring showing both features, and repeats over generations. Not all offspring will have both, and the environment does not change inherited features.","w":["Selecting only for large fruit does not select for resistance, which is unlikely to appear.","","Offspring vary, so not all will have both features; selection must be repeated.","Soil conditions do not change which alleles are passed on or add resistance."]}
{"id":"gbi-232","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement describes genetic engineering?","o":["Breeding the best individuals together over many generations","Making an exact copy of an organism from one of its body cells","Changing an organism's genome by adding a gene from another organism","Changing an organism's features by giving it extra nutrients"],"a":2,"x":"Genetic engineering changes the genome of an organism by introducing a gene from another organism. Breeding over generations is selective breeding. Making exact copies is cloning.","w":["This describes selective breeding, which uses natural variation and does not move genes between organisms directly.","This describes cloning, which copies the whole genome rather than adding a gene from another organism.","","Nutrients change growth, not the genome. This mixes up environmental effects with genetic changes."]}
{"id":"gbi-233","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Scientists put a human gene into a bacterial plasmid. The plasmid is then put into a bacterium. What is the job of the plasmid?","o":["It is a vector that carries the gene into the bacterial cell","It is an enzyme that cuts the gene out of the human DNA","It joins the gene onto a chromosome inside the human cell","It makes the bacterium divide faster so more cells are made"],"a":0,"x":"A plasmid is a small ring of DNA used as a vector. It carries the required gene into the bacterial cell. Cutting out the gene is done by enzymes, not by the plasmid.","w":["","Enzymes cut out the gene. A plasmid is a ring of DNA, not an enzyme, so this mixes up two different roles.","The gene is moved into a bacterium, not into a human cell. This confuses the source of the gene with the host.","Plasmids do not speed up cell division. This invents a role that is not part of genetic engineering."]}
{"id":"gbi-234","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Which is a concern that some people have about GM crops, rather than an advantage of them?","o":["The crops can be made resistant to insect attack","The fruit can be made bigger and better","The crops can be made resistant to disease","They may affect wild flower and insect populations"],"a":3,"x":"Some people worry that GM crops could harm wild flowers and insects. The other three options are advantages, as GM crops can resist pests and disease or give better fruit. Read for 'concern' not 'benefit'.","w":["This is an advantage because less of the crop is eaten. Reading it as a concern is a slip.","This is an advantage, as bigger, better fruit is a reason for making GM crops.","This is an advantage, as healthier plants give more food. It is not a concern.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-235","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"Genes are usually transferred into animal or plant cells at an early stage of development, such as an embryo. Why is this done?","o":["Older cells cannot take up any DNA, so engineering is impossible later","The gene is copied into all the body cells as the organism grows","The embryo will grow faster than a normal organism","The gene will be passed on only if the organism is male"],"a":1,"x":"At an early stage the organism is a few cells. As these divide, every new cell gets a copy of the gene. The organism then develops with the desired characteristic throughout its body.","w":["This is too strong. The point is about copying the gene into all cells, not that older cells cannot take up DNA.","","The inserted gene does not make the embryo grow faster. Growth speed is not the reason for timing.","Inheritance of a gene does not depend on the sex of the organism in this way."]}
{"id":"gbi-236","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A gene giving resistance to insect attack is put into a crop plant. Which is a likely benefit to farmers?","o":["The crop makes more food in the same light by photosynthesis","The insects' genes change, so the insects stop being pests","Less of the crop is eaten, so yield may be higher","The crop no longer needs minerals from the soil"],"a":2,"x":"Insect-resistant plants lose less to pests, so yield can be higher and less insecticide may be needed. The gene does not change photosynthesis, the insects' genes or the plant's need for minerals.","w":["The gene gives insect resistance, not faster photosynthesis. This links a gene to the wrong characteristic.","The gene is in the crop, not the insects. The insects' DNA is not changed.","","A new gene for insect resistance does not remove the plant's need for minerals."]}
{"id":"gbi-237","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":1,"q":"Why are plants grown from cuttings genetically identical to the parent plant?","o":["The cuttings are grown in the same soil as the parent plant","The new plant's cells are made by mitosis from the parent's cells","The cuttings are pollinated by pollen from the parent plant","The new plant's cells are made by meiosis from the parent's cells"],"a":1,"x":"A cutting grows by mitosis, which copies the parent's cells exactly, so the new plant has the same genes. No gametes are involved. The trap is thinking the environment or meiosis decides the genes.","w":["Soil affects how well a plant grows, but it does not decide its genes.","","Cuttings are not grown from seeds, so no pollination takes place.","Meiosis makes gametes with different genes, so it would not give identical offspring."]}
{"id":"gbi-238","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":1,"q":"In an embryo transplant, why are the cells of the embryo split apart at a very early stage?","o":["Early cells have not been fertilised, so they can be given a new nucleus","Later cells are too large to be moved into the womb of a host mother","Early cells have different genes, so each one gives a different animal","Early cells have not yet specialised, so each can grow into a whole animal"],"a":3,"x":"Early embryo cells are unspecialised, so each can develop into a complete animal. The animals are identical and are grown in host mothers. The trap is mixing this up with adult cell cloning, which uses a nucleus transfer.","w":["This describes adult cell cloning; embryo splitting does not add a new nucleus.","Cell size is not the reason; the reason is that later cells have specialised.","The cells came from one embryo, so they have identical genes, not different ones.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-239","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A fertilised egg from a prize cow and a prize bull is split into four embryos. Each is placed in a different host cow. Which statement about the four calves is correct?","o":["They are clones of each other, but not of the host cows or parents","They are clones of the host cows that carried them","They have different genes because they had different host mothers","They are clones of the cow that supplied the egg"],"a":0,"x":"All four calves came from one fertilised egg, so they are identical to each other. Their genes came from both the bull and the cow, not from the host mothers. The trap is thinking the womb gives the genes.","w":["","The host cow only provides a womb; it gives no genes to the calf.","Genes come from the embryo, not the host mother, so the calves are still identical.","The bull also gave genes through the sperm, so the calves are not clones of the cow."]}
{"id":"gbi-240","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":2,"q":"In adult cell cloning, the nucleus from an udder cell of sheep A is put into an egg cell from sheep B. The egg's own nucleus has been removed. Sheep C carries the embryo. Which sheep is the lamb a clone of?","o":["Sheep B, because the egg cell came from sheep B","Sheep C, because sheep C gave birth to the lamb","Sheep A, because its nucleus supplied the genes","A mixture of sheep A and sheep B, because both gave cells"],"a":2,"x":"The nucleus holds the genes. The lamb has the nucleus of sheep A, so it is a clone of A. Sheep B only gave an empty egg cell and C only gave a womb. The trap is thinking the egg donor or mother gives the genes.","w":["The egg's own nucleus was removed, so the lamb's nucleus has no genes from sheep B.","Sheep C only provides the womb, so it does not supply any genes.","","Only one nucleus is used, and it is from A, so there is no mixing of genes."]}
{"id":"gbi-241","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.5","lv":3,"q":"Only three plants of a rare species survive. Scientists use tissue culture to grow 500 new plants from them. What is a problem for the long-term survival of the species?","o":["The 500 plants will be too different from each other to survive","The 500 plants will have little variation, so one disease could kill them all","The 500 plants will not be able to flower because they are clones","The 500 plants will have damaged DNA because tissue culture causes mutations"],"a":1,"x":"The 500 plants are clones of just three, so there is very little variation. A new disease or change in climate could harm them all. The trap is thinking cloning helps survival by making many plants, when it limits variation.","w":["Clones are identical to their parents, so they are not very different from each other.","","Cloned plants can still flower; being a clone does not stop reproduction.","Tissue culture copies cells by mitosis and does not cause mutations in every plant."]}
{"id":"gbi-242","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Mendel bred pea plants and counted the offspring. What did he conclude about how characteristics are inherited?","o":["Characteristics blend, so offspring always look halfway between the parents","Characteristics are controlled by units passed on unchanged from parents to offspring","Characteristics gained during a parent's life are passed on to its offspring","Characteristics are controlled by chromosomes made of DNA"],"a":1,"x":"Mendel showed that characteristics are controlled by separate units (later called genes) passed on unchanged. He did not know about chromosomes or DNA. Blending and passing on gained features are common wrong ideas.","w":["Mendel's results did not show blending; the units stayed separate and unchanged, so this is the blending misconception.","","That is Lamarck's idea about gained features, not Mendel's conclusion from his pea plant results.","Chromosomes and DNA were linked to genes after Mendel's lifetime, so he could not have concluded this."]}
{"id":"gbi-243","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is the best test of whether two populations belong to different species?","o":["They look very different from each other","They live in different habitats","They eat different types of food","They cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring"],"a":3,"x":"Different species cannot interbreed to produce fertile offspring. Looks, habitat and diet can differ within one species, so they are not a reliable test.","w":["Members of one species can look very different, such as dog breeds, so appearance alone is not the test.","One species can live in several habitats, so habitat is not the test; this confuses place with species.","One species can eat different foods in different places, so diet does not decide species.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-244","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Many scientists did not accept Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection at first. Which is one reason?","o":["Nobody yet knew how characteristics were passed from parents to offspring","Wallace had shown that species never change over time","Scientists already knew that genes were made of DNA","No fossils had been found at that time"],"a":0,"x":"The mechanism of inheritance and variation was not understood until about 50 years after Darwin published. Wallace agreed with natural selection, DNA's structure was found in the 1950s, and fossils were already known.","w":["","Wallace independently proposed natural selection, so he supported Darwin; this mixes up the people involved.","DNA's structure was only found in the 1950s, long after Darwin, so this is the wrong time period.","Fossils were already known in the 1800s; the problem was not a lack of fossils at all."]}
{"id":"gbi-245","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A new river splits a beetle population in two. The beetles cannot cross it. The two sides have different conditions. How could a new species form?","o":["Beetles choose to change to suit their side, then pass the change on","The river makes beetles on one side mutate into a new species at once","Natural selection differs on each side until they cannot produce fertile young","Being split by the river alone makes them separate species straight away"],"a":2,"x":"Isolation stops gene flow. Different conditions mean natural selection favours different alleles on each side. After many generations the populations may not interbreed to produce fertile offspring, so they are new species.","w":["Beetles do not choose to change; this is Lamarck's idea of features gained in life being inherited.","The river does not cause mutations; mutations are random and happen in both populations, and no species forms at once.","","Isolation is needed, but species form only after many generations of selection, not straight away."]}
{"id":"gbi-246","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.1","lv":3,"q":"Put these in the order they happened. P: Mendel suggests inherited units. Q: chromosomes seen in cell division. R: units linked to chromosomes (genes). S: structure of DNA found.","o":["P, R, Q, S","P, Q, R, S","Q, P, R, S","P, Q, S, R"],"a":1,"x":"Mendel came first (1860s). Chromosomes were seen dividing in the late 1800s. Early 1900s scientists linked units to chromosomes. DNA's structure came in the 1950s. Trap: thinking DNA came before genes on chromosomes.","w":["Genes could not be linked to chromosomes before chromosomes had been seen in cell division.","","Mendel's work came first, in the 1860s, before chromosomes were observed dividing.","The idea of genes on chromosomes (early 1900s) came before DNA's structure was found in the 1950s."]}
{"id":"gbi-247","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A whole insect is found in amber (hardened tree resin). Its body is almost perfectly preserved. Why has it not decayed?","o":["Amber contains chemicals that slowly turn the insect into stone","Microorganisms that cause decay could not get to the insect","Insects have no soft tissue, so nothing is left to decay","The insect was squashed flat by layers of sedimentary rock"],"a":1,"x":"Fossils can form where decay is prevented. Amber seals the insect from air, moisture and decay microorganisms. Do not confuse this with fossils where minerals replace the hard parts.","w":["That describes minerals replacing hard parts; amber preserves the insect by sealing it in resin, not by turning it to stone.","","Insects do have soft tissues; they stay preserved only because decay microorganisms are kept out.","Amber is hardened resin from trees, not sedimentary rock, and the insect is preserved in 3D, not squashed."]}
{"id":"gbi-248","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A flightless bird lives only on a small island. It dies out after rats arrive on a ship and eat its eggs. Which cause of extinction is this?","o":["A new competitor","A new disease","A catastrophic event","A new predator"],"a":3,"x":"The rats eat the bird's eggs, so they act as a new predator. A competitor would use the same food or space instead. Do not pick competitor just because the rats are new.","w":["Competitors fight for the same resources like food or space; here the rats eat the eggs directly.","No illness is mentioned; the rats cause the harm by eating the eggs.","A catastrophic event is a sudden event like an asteroid or volcano, not the slow arrival of rats.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-249","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.4","lv":2,"q":"MRSA is a bacterium that antibiotics cannot easily kill. Which statement best explains how it became common in hospitals?","o":["Some had a random resistance mutation; they survived antibiotics and reproduced","Antibiotics made the bacteria mutate so they became resistant","Bacteria sensed antibiotics and chose to change so they could survive","Patients became resistant to antibiotics and passed resistance to others"],"a":0,"x":"Resistance starts by random mutation. Antibiotics then act as the selection pressure: non-resistant bacteria die, resistant ones survive, reproduce and pass on the gene. Antibiotics do not cause the mutation.","w":["","Mutations happen randomly before exposure; antibiotics only select the resistant bacteria that already exist.","Bacteria cannot choose to change; natural selection acts on random variation.","The bacteria become resistant, not the patients; people do not pass on resistance by being exposed."]}
{"id":"gbi-250","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Fossils of an early horse in older rock layers are small and have several toes. Fossils in newer layers are larger with one toe. What do these fossils suggest?","o":["Horses grew bigger in their lifetime by using their toes less","All the horse types lived at the same time in different places","Horses changed gradually over millions of years","Newer rock layers formed from the bodies of the older horses"],"a":2,"x":"Older rock lies below newer rock, so a series of fossils shows how a species changed over time. This is evidence for gradual evolution. Traits gained in one lifetime are not inherited.","w":["This is the idea that features gained in life are passed on; fossils show change over generations, not in one lifetime.","The fossils come from different layers, so they show different ages, not the same time.","","Rock layers form from sediment, not from animal bodies; the fossils are just remains trapped in them."]}
{"id":"gbi-251","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.4","lv":3,"q":"A patient stops taking antibiotics after two days because they feel better. Later the infection returns and the same antibiotic no longer works. What is the best explanation?","o":["The antibiotic caused the remaining bacteria to mutate and become resistant","Resistant bacteria survived, reproduced and passed on resistance","The patient's immune system became resistant to the antibiotic","The weakened bacteria became immune to the antibiotic by being exposed to it"],"a":1,"x":"Two days of treatment killed many non-resistant bacteria, leaving resistant survivors. These reproduced and passed on the resistance gene. Finishing the course kills more bacteria. Antibiotics select for resistance; they do not cause the mutation.","w":["Mutations are random and occur before treatment; the drug only selects resistant bacteria already present.","","Resistance is a feature of bacteria, not of the patient's immune system.","Bacteria do not gain immunity from exposure; the resistant ones simply survive because they already had the gene."]}
{"id":"gbi-252","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":1,"q":"Why can bacteria evolve resistance to an antibiotic in only a few years?","o":["Antibiotics make their mutation rate much higher","They reproduce very quickly, so a useful mutation spreads fast","Each bacterium changes its own DNA on purpose to survive","They have a long life, so mutations build up over many decades"],"a":1,"x":"Bacteria can divide very quickly. A chance mutation that gives resistance can soon be passed to huge numbers of offspring. The trap is thinking the antibiotic causes the mutation.","w":["Antibiotics do not raise mutation rates; mutations are random and the antibiotic only selects survivors.","","Mutations are random, not deliberate; bacteria cannot choose to change their DNA.","Bacteria have very short lives, not long ones; fast reproduction is what speeds evolution."]}
{"id":"gbi-253","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":1,"q":"Which action would help to slow the spread of antibiotic-resistant bacteria?","o":["Taking antibiotics for a cold to stop it getting worse","Stopping a course as soon as the symptoms go away","Using antibiotics on healthy farm animals to prevent illness","Doctors only prescribing antibiotics when they are really needed"],"a":3,"x":"Less use of antibiotics means less selection for resistant strains. Colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics do not kill. The trap is thinking antibiotics are harmless to use 'just in case'.","w":["Colds are viral, so antibiotics do not work, and needless use selects for resistant bacteria.","Stopping early can leave surviving bacteria, which may be resistant, to multiply.","Routine use on healthy animals gives more chances for resistant bacteria to be selected.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-254","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":2,"q":"One resistant bacterium divides into two every 30 minutes. If none die, how many resistant bacteria are there after 5 hours?","o":["20","512","1024","100"],"a":2,"x":"5 hours is 10 lots of 30 minutes, so 10 divisions. The number doubles each time: $2^{10} = 1024$. The trap is adding 2 each time instead of doubling.","w":["This adds 2 per division (10 × 2); bacteria double each time, so growth is exponential.","This is $2^9$; it counts only 9 divisions instead of 10.","","This multiplies 10 by 10 instead of doubling the number 10 times."]}
{"id":"gbi-255","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":2,"q":"A patient feels better on day 3 of a 7-day antibiotic course and stops taking it. Why could this lead to more resistant bacteria?","o":["Surviving bacteria may be the less easily killed ones, and they multiply","The antibiotic left in the body makes the bacteria resistant","The patient's own body cells become resistant to the antibiotic","The bacteria that were killed come back to life"],"a":0,"x":"Feeling better does not mean all the bacteria are dead. Survivors are more likely to be resistant, and they can reproduce. The trap is thinking the antibiotic causes the resistance.","w":["","Antibiotics do not turn bacteria resistant; resistance comes from random mutation and is then selected.","Body cells are not the problem; it is the bacteria that become resistant, not human cells.","Killed bacteria stay dead; the danger comes from survivors reproducing."]}
{"id":"gbi-256","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":3,"q":"A new antibiotic kills a strain of MRSA. Why might resistance to the new antibiotic still appear if it is used widely?","o":["Bacteria resistant to one antibiotic are always resistant to all others","Random mutations may give some bacteria resistance, and they would be selected","Bacteria slowly learn to resist the new antibiotic during each infection","New antibiotics only work on viruses, so bacteria are not affected"],"a":1,"x":"Mutations are random and happen in any large bacterial population. Widespread use kills non-resistant bacteria, so any resistant mutants survive and reproduce. The trap is thinking bacteria learn to resist a new antibiotic.","w":["Resistance is usually to particular antibiotics; a new one can work on bacteria resistant to an older one.","","Bacteria cannot learn; resistance comes from random mutation and selection across generations.","Antibiotics work on bacteria, not viruses; this mixes up the two types of pathogen."]}
{"id":"gbi-257","s":"biology","t":"4.6.4","lv":1,"q":"Which list puts the groups used in Linnaeus's classification in order, from the largest group to the smallest group?","o":["Kingdom, phylum, order, class, family, genus, species","Species, genus, family, order, class, phylum, kingdom","Kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species","Kingdom, phylum, class, order, genus, family, species"],"a":2,"x":"The order is kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species. Each group is split into smaller groups. A kingdom is the largest group and a species is the smallest. Swapping neighbouring groups is a common slip.","w":["Class and order are swapped. Class comes before order.","This is the smallest-to-largest order. The question asks for largest to smallest.","","Family and genus are swapped. Family is a bigger group than genus."]}
{"id":"gbi-258","s":"biology","t":"4.6.4","lv":1,"q":"Carl Woese proposed a 'three-domain system' of classification. Which list shows the three domains?","o":["Archaea, bacteria and eukaryota","Plants, animals and fungi","Bacteria, viruses and eukaryota","Prokaryota, protista and eukaryota"],"a":0,"x":"Woese's three domains are archaea, bacteria and eukaryota. Plants, animals and fungi are kingdoms inside the eukaryota. Do not mix up kingdoms with domains.","w":["","These are kingdoms, not domains. All three are inside the domain eukaryota.","Viruses are not placed in any of the three domains. Archaea is missing.","Protista is not a domain. The domains are archaea, bacteria and eukaryota."]}
{"id":"gbi-259","s":"biology","t":"4.6.4","lv":2,"q":"Why did scientists change from Linnaeus's system to the three-domain system?","o":["They found that Linnaeus's binomial names were all wrong","Fossils showed that bacteria evolved from plants","Microscopes showed that archaea cells have a nucleus like animal cells","Chemical analysis showed that some prokaryotes were very different from other bacteria"],"a":3,"x":"Better evidence, such as chemical analysis of cells, showed that archaea differ greatly from bacteria. So a new level above kingdom was added. The trap is thinking the old naming system was simply wrong.","w":["Binomial names are still used today. The change was about higher groups, not names.","There is no fossil evidence that bacteria evolved from plants. This is made up.","Archaea are prokaryotes and have no nucleus. This is a false claim.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-260","s":"biology","t":"4.6.4","lv":2,"q":"Wolves are named Canis lupus. Coyotes are named Canis latrans. What do these names show?","o":["They are the same species with two names","They are in the same genus but are different species","They are in different genera but the same species","They are in different genera and so not closely related"],"a":1,"x":"The first word is the genus and the second word is the species. Both animals are Canis, so they share a genus. The second words differ, so they are different species. Students often read the names as one species.","w":["The species names lupus and latrans are different, so these are two species.","","This has it backwards. Same first word means same genus; different second word means different species.","Both have the first word Canis, so they are in the same genus and closely related."]}
{"id":"gbi-261","s":"biology","t":"4.6.4","lv":3,"q":"An evolutionary tree has four species. Branch point P splits into D and a branch Q. Q splits into C and a branch R. R splits into A and B. Which statement is correct?","o":["D is more closely related to A than C is","A is the ancestor of B","C and B share a more recent common ancestor than D and B do","All four species share a common ancestor only at Q"],"a":2,"x":"C and B meet at Q. D and B meet further back at P. A more recent shared ancestor means a closer relationship. Species on a tree are not ancestors of each other. They sit at the ends of branches.","w":["D splits off first, at P. So C is closer to A than D is.","A and B are at the ends of separate branches from R. Neither is the ancestor of the other.","","D joins at P, which is further back than Q. So all four share an ancestor at P."]}
{"id":"gbi-262","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Two plants grow close together in a field. Which pair of resources are they most likely to compete for?","o":["Mates and territory","Food and oxygen","Light and water","Prey and shelter"],"a":2,"x":"Plants compete for light, space, water and mineral ions from the soil. Mates, territory and prey are things animals compete for. Plants make their own food, so they do not compete for it.","w":["Mates and territory are things animals compete for, not plants.","Plants make their own food by photosynthesis, so they do not compete for food.","","Plants do not hunt prey, and prey is an animal resource, not a plant one."]}
{"id":"gbi-263","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A lizard shelters in a burrow during the hottest part of the day. What type of adaptation is this?","o":["Structural","Behavioural","Functional","Competitive"],"a":1,"x":"Behavioural adaptations are things an organism does to survive. Sheltering from heat is an action. Structural adaptations are body features, and functional adaptations are processes inside the body.","w":["A structural adaptation is a body feature such as fur or spines, not an action.","","A functional adaptation is a body process, such as making concentrated urine, not an action.","Competitive is not a type of adaptation; competition is an interaction between organisms."]}
{"id":"gbi-264","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A disease kills most of the ladybirds in a garden. Ladybirds eat aphids. Aphids feed on rose plants. What is most likely to happen to the aphids first?","o":["Their numbers increase because fewer are eaten","Their numbers decrease because there are fewer ladybirds","Their numbers stay the same because they are not linked","Their numbers decrease because the roses grow better"],"a":0,"x":"Species in a community depend on each other. Fewer predators means fewer aphids are eaten, so aphid numbers rise. The trap is thinking that losing one species always lowers numbers of the others.","w":["","Fewer predators means fewer aphids are eaten, so aphid numbers rise, not fall.","Aphids and ladybirds are linked as prey and predator, so the change in ladybirds affects aphids.","Roses are the aphids' food, so better rose growth would not cut aphid numbers, and this ignores the predator link."]}
{"id":"gbi-265","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A new fish is added to a lake. It eats the same insects as a native fish. Native fish numbers then fall. What is the best explanation?","o":["The new fish is a predator that eats the native fish","The water temperature must have risen, which is a biotic factor","A new pathogen must have arrived, which is an abiotic factor","The new fish competes for food, which is a biotic factor"],"a":3,"x":"The two fish eat the same insects, so they compete for food. Competition between living things is a biotic factor. The question gives no sign of predation, and temperature is abiotic.","w":["The fish share a food source and nothing says the new fish eats the native fish, so this wrongly assumes predation.","Temperature is an abiotic factor, not biotic, and nothing says it changed.","A pathogen is a living thing so it would be biotic, and nothing says a pathogen arrived.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-266","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":3,"q":"Which row correctly names the type of each adaptation: a polar bear's thick fat layer, a camel making little concentrated urine, birds migrating to warmer places?","o":["Behavioural, functional, structural","Structural, behavioural, functional","Structural, functional, behavioural","Functional, structural, behavioural"],"a":2,"x":"A fat layer is a body feature, so it is structural. Making concentrated urine is a body process, so it is functional. Migrating is an action, so it is behavioural. The trap is mixing up functional with behavioural.","w":["A fat layer is a body feature, so it is structural, and migration is an action, not a body feature.","Making concentrated urine is a body process, not an action, and migrating is not functional.","","A fat layer is a physical feature, not a body process, and urine production is a process, not a body part."]}
{"id":"gbi-267","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"In ecology, what is a community?","o":["All the members of one species living in a habitat","All the living and non-living things in an area","All the different populations living together in a habitat","One organism and the place where it lives"],"a":2,"x":"A community is all the populations of different species living together in one habitat. One species alone is a population. Living plus non-living parts make an ecosystem.","w":["This describes a population, which is only one species, not a whole community.","This describes an ecosystem, which includes non-living (abiotic) factors as well as the living organisms.","","This describes one individual and its habitat, not all the populations living together."]}
{"id":"gbi-268","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Look at this food chain: grass → grasshopper → frog → heron. Which organism is the secondary consumer?","o":["Frog","Grasshopper","Heron","Grass"],"a":0,"x":"The grasshopper eats the producer, so it is the primary consumer. The frog eats the grasshopper, so it is the secondary consumer. The heron is tertiary. The trap is counting the producer as a consumer.","w":["","The grasshopper is second in the chain, but it eats the producer, so it is the primary consumer.","The heron is the tertiary consumer. It is last in the chain, not the secondary one.","Grass is the producer. It makes food by photosynthesis and is not a consumer."]}
{"id":"gbi-269","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"In a predator-prey cycle, the predator peak comes a little after the prey peak. What is the best reason for this delay?","o":["Predators only eat prey when prey numbers are falling","Prey numbers fall as soon as any new predators are born","Predators and prey compete for exactly the same food","Predators need time to breed after their food supply rises"],"a":3,"x":"More prey means more food for predators, but predators take time to breed and raise young, so their peak lags behind. A common trap is thinking predator numbers respond instantly.","w":["Predators eat prey all the time. Their numbers rise when there is more prey, not only when it falls.","Newborn predators do not instantly cut prey numbers. The slip is ignoring the time delay.","Predators eat prey, so they do not compete with it for the same food. This confuses predation and competition.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-270","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student wants to find how the number of daisies changes from a hedge outwards across a lawn. Which method is best?","o":["Place quadrats at random positions all over the lawn","Place quadrats at regular intervals along a tape from the hedge","Use one large quadrat right next to the hedge","Count daisies in one quadrat in the middle of the lawn"],"a":1,"x":"A line transect shows change along a gradient: quadrats at regular intervals along a tape. Random quadrats are for estimating a population in an area, not for showing a trend with distance.","w":["Random quadrats estimate the total population. They cannot show how numbers change with distance from the hedge.","","One quadrat gives no pattern across the lawn. The student needs several samples along a line.","One quadrat in one place gives no comparison with distance and is a very small sample."]}
{"id":"gbi-271","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Rabbits and foxes: Year 1: 400 rabbits, 30 foxes. Year 2: 600, 30. Year 3: 500, 55. Year 4: 250, 70. What best explains the fall in rabbits in Year 4?","o":["Foxes decreased after Year 2, so the rabbits lost their food","Rabbits fell because foxes were fewer than rabbits in every year","Fox numbers rose in Years 3 and 4, so more rabbits were eaten","Rabbits fell because foxes ran out of food in Year 4"],"a":2,"x":"Foxes rose from 30 to 55 to 70, so more rabbits were eaten and rabbit numbers fell. Foxes are predators, and the fall in prey follows the rise in predators. Do not reverse cause and effect.","w":["Foxes did not decrease; they rose. Foxes also do not provide food for rabbits, which is a predator-prey mix-up.","Fox numbers compared with rabbits do not cause a fall. The change in predator numbers matters.","","Foxes were not short of food, as they increased in number. This reverses the cause of the rabbit fall."]}
{"id":"gbi-272","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Water vapour rises, cools and turns into tiny droplets of liquid water that form clouds. What is this process called?","o":["Evaporation","Condensation","Precipitation","Melting"],"a":1,"x":"Condensation is water vapour cooling and changing into liquid droplets. Evaporation is the opposite change. Precipitation is water falling from clouds as rain, snow or hail.","w":["Evaporation is liquid turning into vapour, the reverse change, so this mixes up the direction.","","Precipitation is water falling from clouds, not the cooling that forms the droplets, so the order of steps is confused.","Melting is a solid such as ice turning to liquid. It is not how clouds form from vapour."]}
{"id":"gbi-273","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":1,"q":"A rabbit eats grass. In what form is the carbon passed from the grass to the rabbit?","o":["As carbon dioxide gas stored inside the grass","As pure carbon, like charcoal","In carbon compounds such as carbohydrates and proteins","As carbon monoxide gas inside the grass"],"a":2,"x":"Plants use carbon dioxide to make carbon compounds such as glucose, starch, proteins and fats. Animals get carbon by eating these compounds. The trap is thinking carbon passes along food chains as a gas or as pure carbon.","w":["Plants take in carbon dioxide but turn it into other compounds, so the rabbit does not eat it as a gas.","Living things do not contain pure carbon; their carbon is part of larger compounds.","","Carbon monoxide is a poisonous gas from incomplete burning. It is not passed along food chains."]}
{"id":"gbi-274","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Sea water is salty, but rain is fresh water. Which process in the water cycle leaves the salt behind in the sea?","o":["Evaporation","Condensation","Precipitation","Decay"],"a":0,"x":"When the Sun heats the sea, water evaporates as vapour. The dissolved salt does not evaporate, so it stays behind. This is why rain is fresh water that plants and animals on land can use.","w":["","Condensation happens in the air after the water has already left the salt behind.","Precipitation is water falling as rain; the salt was already left in the sea before this.","Decay is the breakdown of dead material by microorganisms. It does not separate salt from water."]}
{"id":"gbi-275","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Coal formed from plants millions of years ago. What happens to its carbon when a power station burns the coal?","o":["It is taken in by plants for photosynthesis straight away","It stays in the ash and is not released","It is changed into water vapour","It joins with oxygen and is released as carbon dioxide"],"a":3,"x":"Combustion of fossil fuels joins carbon with oxygen and releases carbon dioxide into the air. This returns carbon locked away for millions of years. The trap is thinking the carbon is removed or stays behind.","w":["Burning releases carbon dioxide into the air first; plants can only take it in afterwards.","Most of the carbon leaves as gas; only a little ash remains, so this ignores combustion.","Burning fuels also makes water, but the carbon forms carbon dioxide, not water.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-277","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A farm uses a biogas generator. Microbes break down waste without oxygen. Which gas made in the generator is burned as a fuel?","o":["Oxygen","Methane","Hydrogen","Nitrogen"],"a":1,"x":"Anaerobic decay makes methane, which is the fuel in biogas. Oxygen is the gas needed for burning, not the fuel. It is also missing from the generator.","w":["Oxygen helps fuels burn but is not a fuel, and none is present in anaerobic decay.","","Hydrogen is not the main gas made by anaerobic decay; the fuel is methane.","Nitrogen does not burn, so it cannot be the fuel."]}
{"id":"gbi-278","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A student tests how temperature affects the decay of fresh milk. Milk samples are kept at 10°C, 20°C, 30°C and 40°C. Which is a control variable?","o":["The temperature of the water bath","The rate at which the pH falls","The volume of milk in each tube","The time taken for the pH to change"],"a":2,"x":"A control variable is kept the same so the test is fair. The volume of milk must be equal in every tube. Temperature is the independent variable. The rate of pH change is the dependent variable.","w":["Temperature is the independent variable being changed, not a control variable.","The rate of pH fall is the result measured, so it is the dependent variable.","","Time is measured as the result, so it is not kept the same."]}
{"id":"gbi-279","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A gardener's compost heap is wet, packed down and smells bad. Little air reaches the middle. What is the best way to speed up decay?","o":["Turn the heap to mix in air","Add a lot more water","Cover it tightly with plastic sheet","Press the heap down more firmly"],"a":0,"x":"Packed, wet waste lacks oxygen, so slow anaerobic decay makes smelly products. Turning the heap adds air so aerobic microbes can work faster. More water or tight covering would reduce oxygen further.","w":["","The heap is already wet; more water would push out more air and slow decay.","Tight covering keeps air out, which keeps decay slow and smelly.","Pressing down removes air spaces, so oxygen is even lower."]}
{"id":"gbi-280","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":3,"q":"A biogas generator makes much less methane after a leak lets air into the tank. What is the best explanation?","o":["Methane is made only when oxygen is present","Air makes the waste too cold for microbes","Oxygen turns the methane into a solid","Microbes respire aerobically, making carbon dioxide instead"],"a":3,"x":"Methane comes from anaerobic decay. With air present, microbes respire aerobically and make carbon dioxide and water. The trap is thinking methane needs oxygen. Fuel production falls because anaerobic conditions are lost.","w":["Methane is made without oxygen; thinking it needs oxygen is the slip.","Air does not automatically cool the waste, and cold is not the reason for less methane.","Oxygen does not make methane solid; this has no link to decay.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-281","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"The rate of decay in a compost heap rises with temperature, then falls sharply above about 50°C. What is the best reason for the fall?","o":["Enzymes freeze and stop working","Enzymes change shape so the substrate no longer fits","Enzymes are used up in the reaction","Molecules move too slowly to collide"],"a":1,"x":"Very high temperatures denature the enzymes of decay microbes. Their active sites change shape, so the substrate no longer fits. Slow molecules and freezing are effects of cold, not heat.","w":["Freezing happens at low temperatures, not high ones.","","Enzymes are not used up in reactions; they can be reused until denatured.","Slow-moving molecules happen at low temperatures; heat makes them move faster."]}
{"id":"gbi-282","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.4","lv":3,"q":"Since 1990, a butterfly species has been recorded further north in the UK each decade. Average UK temperatures rose over the same period. Which statement is best supported by this evidence?","o":["Rising temperature is the only cause, because it moved north as temperatures rose.","Individual butterflies changed their needs to cope with warmth, so they moved north.","Warming may let it survive further north, but other factors could also matter.","It has died out in the south, so it is now only found further north."],"a":2,"x":"The link between temperature and distribution suggests warming lets the species live further north. The trap is treating a correlation as proof of a single cause. Food supply or habitat change could also play a part.","w":["A correlation does not prove one cause; other factors such as food or habitat could also explain the movement.","Individual animals do not change their needs because of the weather; species spread when conditions start to suit them.","","The data show the range spreading north; nothing says the butterfly has died out in the south."]}
{"id":"gbi-283","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A frog species breeds in ponds. Its tadpoles need water for about 12 weeks. Over 20 years, rainfall in a region falls and many ponds now dry up in summer. What is the most likely effect on the frog's distribution?","o":["Mainly found near ponds that hold water for at least 12 weeks.","Spread across the whole region, because adult frogs can live on land.","Mainly found near ponds that dry fast, as tadpoles grow quicker there.","No change, because fewer ponds means less competition between frogs."],"a":0,"x":"Water supply limits where a species can breed. Tadpoles die if a pond dries before they develop, so frogs survive mainly near ponds that last long enough. The trap is thinking adult frogs alone decide distribution.","w":["","Adults can live on land, but breeding still needs water, so distribution follows water that lasts.","Tadpoles in ponds that dry quickly would die before they develop, so frogs could not breed there.","Fewer ponds means fewer places where tadpoles survive; competition is not the main limit here."]}
{"id":"gbi-284","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.4","lv":3,"q":"A student looks at lichens along a line from a city centre to the countryside. Only crusty lichens grow at the centre. Crusty, leafy and shrubby lichens grow at 10 km. Which conclusion is best?","o":["Air pollution is the same at both sites, because crusty lichens grow at both.","Shrubby lichens need sulfur dioxide, so its level is highest at 10 km.","Oxygen is lowest at 10 km, because more types of lichen grow there.","Sulfur dioxide is probably highest in the centre, where sensitive lichens are absent."],"a":3,"x":"Lichens are indicator species. Leafy and shrubby lichens are sensitive to polluting gases such as sulfur dioxide, so their absence suggests more pollution. The trap is using pollution-tolerant crusty lichens, which tell you little.","w":["Crusty lichens tolerate pollution, so growing at both sites shows nothing; the sensitive types are missing in the centre.","Sensitive lichens do not need sulfur dioxide; they die where it is high, so it would be lower at 10 km.","More lichen types suggest cleaner air, not less oxygen; oxygen levels in air hardly differ between the sites.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-285","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Why does a high biodiversity make an ecosystem more stable?","o":["Species in a diverse ecosystem never compete with each other","Fewer species means less competition for food and space","Each species depends less on any one other species for food and shelter","All the species are producers, so food is always available"],"a":2,"x":"With many species, each one has other sources of food and shelter, so it depends less on any single species. The trap is thinking stability comes from fewer species or no competition.","w":["Species in diverse ecosystems still compete for food, space and other resources.","High biodiversity means more species, not fewer, so this does not explain stability.","","Ecosystems contain producers, consumers and decomposers, not just producers."]}
{"id":"gbi-286","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which human activity directly reduces the land available for wild plants and animals?","o":["Digging a quarry to get stone","Making a wildlife corridor between woods","Recycling glass bottles","Using compost that has no peat in it"],"a":0,"x":"Quarrying, building, farming and dumping waste take over land and destroy habitats. Recycling and peat-free compost help reduce harm to the environment.","w":["","A wildlife corridor links habitats, so it helps wildlife rather than reducing land for it.","Recycling cuts waste and the need for new materials, so it does not take land from wildlife.","Peat-free compost protects peat bogs, so it helps habitats rather than reducing them."]}
{"id":"gbi-287","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Peat bogs are dug up to make compost for gardens. Why does this add to global warming?","o":["Peat bogs give out oxygen, so less oxygen is left in the air","Peat contains CFCs, which are released when it is dug up","Peat bogs absorb heat, so the soil around them gets warmer","Peat decays or is burnt, which releases carbon dioxide into the air"],"a":3,"x":"Peat stores carbon. When it is dug up it decays or is burnt, releasing carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas. The trap is thinking the gas is oxygen or a harmful chemical in the peat.","w":["Oxygen is not a greenhouse gas, and the problem is carbon dioxide being released.","Peat does not contain CFCs; it stores carbon, which is released as carbon dioxide.","Peat bogs do not work by absorbing heat; the warming comes from released carbon dioxide.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-288","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A field had 12 plant species. After pesticide use, a survey found only 5 species. What is the percentage decrease in the number of species? Give your answer to the nearest whole number.","o":["42%","58%","140%","7%"],"a":1,"x":"The decrease is 12 − 5 = 7 species. Percentage decrease = 7 ÷ 12 × 100 = 58%. The trap is dividing by the new number or giving the raw decrease.","w":["This is 5 ÷ 12 × 100, the percentage left, not the percentage decrease.","","This divides the decrease by the final number (7 ÷ 5) instead of the starting number.","This is only the number of species lost, not a percentage."]}
{"id":"gbi-289","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":3,"q":"Which statement correctly explains how burning fossil fuels can reduce biodiversity in a lake?","o":["Carbon dioxide dissolves in the lake and heats the water until fish die","Sulfur dioxide dissolves in rain, and the acidic water kills sensitive species","Smoke makes the lake water hotter, so oxygen is released and fish die","Acidic gases dissolve in the soil only, so lake species are not affected"],"a":1,"x":"Burning fossil fuels releases acidic gases such as sulfur dioxide. They dissolve in rain, making acid rain. Acidic lake water kills sensitive species, so biodiversity falls. Trap: mixing up acid rain with global warming.","w":["Dissolved carbon dioxide does not heat water; the harm comes from acidic gases forming acid rain.","","Smoke does not release oxygen into lakes; the damage comes from acidic gases forming acid rain.","Acid rain also falls into lakes and rivers, so water species can be harmed."]}
{"id":"gbi-290","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which two gases are greenhouse gases that human activity adds to the atmosphere?","o":["Oxygen and nitrogen","Carbon dioxide and methane","Nitrogen and methane","Oxygen and carbon dioxide"],"a":1,"x":"Carbon dioxide and methane trap heat in the atmosphere, so they are greenhouse gases. Oxygen and nitrogen make up most of the air but do not trap heat in this way.","w":["Oxygen and nitrogen are the main gases in air but are not greenhouse gases.","","Nitrogen is not a greenhouse gas; only methane in this pair traps heat.","Oxygen is not a greenhouse gas; only carbon dioxide in this pair traps heat."]}
{"id":"gbi-291","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Gardeners use compost made from peat. Why does digging up peat bogs add to global warming?","o":["Peat traps heat from the Sun, so removing it warms the air","Bog plants make oxygen, so removing them leaves less oxygen to cool the air","Peat is made of nitrogen, which turns into a greenhouse gas when dug up","Peat stores carbon, released as carbon dioxide when it decays or burns"],"a":3,"x":"Peat is made of partly decayed plants and stores lots of carbon. When bogs are drained and the peat is used or burnt, microbes and fire release carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas.","w":["Peat does not trap heat from the Sun; the problem is carbon released as CO₂.","Oxygen does not cool the air; the issue is stored carbon escaping as CO₂.","Peat is mostly carbon-rich plant material, not nitrogen; this names the wrong gas and source.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-292","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Tropical forest is cleared to make room for cattle and rice fields. How do the cattle and rice fields add to global warming?","o":["They both release methane, a greenhouse gas","They both release extra oxygen, which traps heat","They both release nitrogen, which traps heat","They both heat the ground by blocking sunlight"],"a":0,"x":"Cattle and rice paddy fields both release methane, a greenhouse gas. This adds to the carbon dioxide already released by clearing and burning the forest.","w":["","Oxygen is not a greenhouse gas; the gas released is methane.","Nitrogen is not a greenhouse gas and is not the gas released here.","Blocking sunlight is not the cause; the problem is methane released."]}
{"id":"gbi-293","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":3,"q":"Over 50 years, a bird species has begun breeding much further north. Which statement best links this to global warming?","o":["Deforestation in the south left the birds with less oxygen to breathe","Greenhouse gases are poisonous to birds, so they moved away from the south","Rising average temperatures changed where the conditions the birds need are found","Rising sea levels covered all the land where the birds once bred"],"a":2,"x":"Global warming changes temperatures, so suitable habitats and food sources shift. Species may change their distribution or migration patterns. Beware blaming oxygen or poison; neither is the effect of warming.","w":["Deforestation does not reduce breathable oxygen to harmful levels; the link is temperature change.","Greenhouse gases warm the air; they do not poison birds, so this is a wrong mechanism.","","Sea level rise may flood some habitats, but not all breeding land; shifting habitats is the better link."]}
{"id":"gbi-294","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A rainforest covers 5000 hectares. 12% is cleared and burnt. Burning releases 150 tonnes of carbon per hectare. How much carbon is released?","o":["750 000 tonnes","90 000 tonnes","9000 tonnes","1800 tonnes"],"a":1,"x":"12% of 5000 = 600 hectares. Then 600 × 150 = 90 000 tonnes. The trap is forgetting to find the cleared area first.","w":["This uses all 5000 hectares and ignores the 12% that was cleared.","","This slips a decimal place by using 60 hectares instead of 600.","This multiplies 12 by 150, treating the percentage as hectares."]}
{"id":"gbi-295","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.6","lv":1,"q":"Some farmers plant new hedgerows around fields where only one crop is grown. How does this help biodiversity?","o":["It stops all pests from reaching the crop","It lets farmers use more fertiliser on the field","It gives wild plants, insects and birds food and shelter","It makes the field bigger so that more crop is grown"],"a":2,"x":"A field with only one crop gives few habitats. Hedgerows add food and shelter, so more types of organism can live there. The trap is thinking hedgerows are mainly there to help the crop.","w":["Hedgerows cannot stop all pests reaching a crop; their biodiversity value is providing habitats for wildlife.","Hedgerows do not allow more fertiliser use, and extra fertiliser would reduce biodiversity anyway.","","Hedgerows take up land, so they make the growing area smaller, not bigger."]}
{"id":"gbi-296","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.6","lv":1,"q":"Why does recycling resources, rather than dumping waste in landfill, help to maintain biodiversity?","o":["Recycled waste turns into fertiliser that helps wild plants","Less land is needed for landfill, so fewer habitats are destroyed","Recycling takes carbon dioxide directly out of the air","Recycling means fewer people need to be fed"],"a":1,"x":"Landfill sites take over land that could be habitat. Recycling reduces the amount of waste buried, so less land is lost. The trap is choosing an unrelated benefit.","w":["Recycling does not turn waste into fertiliser for wild plants; this invents a link that does not exist.","","Recycling does not remove carbon dioxide from the air; plants do that by photosynthesis.","Recycling does not change how many people need feeding; its benefit is less land used for waste."]}
{"id":"gbi-297","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.6","lv":2,"q":"A council wants to turn a wetland into a protected nature reserve. Which is an argument AGAINST doing this?","o":["Rare species in the wetland will be less likely to die out","The wetland will keep storing carbon in its plants","Visitors can learn about the wildlife that lives there","The land cannot be used for housing or farming, so income is lost"],"a":3,"x":"Protecting a habitat stops other uses of the land, such as building or farming, which has an economic cost. The trap is picking a benefit, which is an argument for the reserve.","w":["Fewer rare species dying out is a benefit, so it is an argument for the reserve.","Keeping carbon stored is a benefit of protecting the habitat, so it supports the reserve.","Education for visitors is a benefit of a reserve, so it is an argument for it.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-298","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.6","lv":2,"q":"A zoo runs a breeding programme for an endangered rhino. Why is it also important to protect the rhino's natural habitat?","o":["Rhinos released into the wild need a suitable place to live and feed","Protected habitats make rhinos breed faster in zoos","Habitats are needed so that zoos can sell the animals","Protected habitats stop wild rhinos from ever dying"],"a":0,"x":"Breeding programmes only save a species if animals can later live in the wild, which needs a protected habitat with food and shelter. The trap is thinking breeding alone is enough.","w":["","Protecting wild habitat does not change how fast rhinos breed inside a zoo.","Breeding programmes aim to conserve species, not to sell animals for profit.","Habitat protection improves survival chances but cannot stop every rhino from dying."]}
{"id":"gbi-299","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.6","lv":2,"q":"A farm had 15 insect species in its field edges. After hedgerows were planted it had 24 species. What is the percentage increase in the number of insect species?","o":["37.5%","160%","60%","9%"],"a":2,"x":"Increase = 24 − 15 = 9. Percentage increase = 9 ÷ 15 × 100 = 60%. The trap is dividing by the new number, 24, which gives 37.5%.","w":["This divides the increase by the new value, 24, instead of the original value, 15.","This is 24 ÷ 15 × 100, the new number as a percentage of the old, not the increase.","","9 is the actual increase in species, not the increase as a percentage of the original 15."]}
{"id":"gbi-300","s":"biology","t":"4.7.4.1","lv":1,"q":"Look at this food chain: grass → grasshopper → frog → snake. Which organism is the secondary consumer?","o":["Grass","Frog","Grasshopper","Snake"],"a":1,"x":"The frog eats the grasshopper, which is a primary consumer, so the frog is the secondary consumer. Grass is the producer. The trap is counting the producer as a consumer.","w":["Grass is the producer, making its own food by photosynthesis, so it is not a consumer at all.","","The grasshopper eats the producer, so it is the primary consumer, one level below secondary.","The snake eats the frog, so it is the tertiary consumer, one level too high."]}
{"id":"gbi-301","s":"biology","t":"4.7.4.1","lv":1,"q":"Why do most food chains have only four or five trophic levels?","o":["Top predators are too big for anything else to eat","Producers cannot make enough glucose to feed consumers","Decomposers break animals down before they are eaten","Too little biomass is left after losses at each level"],"a":3,"x":"Biomass is lost at every stage through waste, respiration and uneaten parts. After four or five stages so little is left that another level cannot be supported. The trap is blaming size or decomposers.","w":["Size is not the reason, because the biomass runs out before another level could be supported.","Producers make plenty of glucose, and the limit comes from biomass lost between levels.","Decomposers feed on dead material and waste, so they do not stop living animals being eaten.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-302","s":"biology","t":"4.7.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A pond has 2000 kg of producer biomass. 10% passes to primary consumers. Then 15% of the primary consumers' biomass passes to secondary consumers. How much biomass do the secondary consumers gain?","o":["30 kg","300 kg","170 kg","230 kg"],"a":0,"x":"Primary consumers gain 10% of 2000 = 200 kg. Secondary consumers gain 15% of 200 = 30 kg. The trap is applying 15% to the producers instead of the primary consumers.","w":["","This is 15% of 2000, but the 15% applies to the primary consumers' 200 kg.","This is 200 − 30, the biomass lost at the second step, not the biomass gained.","This adds 200 and 30, but the question asks only for the secondary consumers' gain."]}
{"id":"gbi-303","s":"biology","t":"4.7.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is NOT a reason why biomass is lost between trophic levels in a food chain?","o":["Some parts of the prey, such as bones, are not eaten by the predator","Waste materials such as faeces and urine are lost","Some biomass is absorbed and used to build new cells in the consumer","Glucose is used in respiration, which releases CO₂ and water"],"a":2,"x":"Biomass used to build new cells is the biomass that is passed on. It is not lost. Uneaten parts, waste and respiration all remove biomass. The trap is thinking all use of biomass is a loss.","w":["This is a real loss, because uneaten bones never enter the next trophic level.","This is a real loss, because faeces and urine carry biomass out of the organism.","","This is a real loss, because respiration turns glucose into CO₂ and water that leave the body."]}
{"id":"gbi-304","s":"biology","t":"4.7.4.1","lv":3,"q":"A pyramid of biomass has 4000 kg of producers, 360 kg of primary consumers, 54 kg of secondary consumers and 6 kg of tertiary consumers. Which transfer is the most efficient?","o":["Producers to primary consumers","Primary to secondary consumers","Secondary to tertiary consumers","All are equally efficient because the pyramid narrows steadily"],"a":1,"x":"Producers to primary: 360 ÷ 4000 = 9%. Primary to secondary: 54 ÷ 360 = 15%. Secondary to tertiary: 6 ÷ 54 ≈ 11%. So 15% is the highest. The trap is judging by the biomass lost.","w":["This is only 9%; it loses the most kg, but efficiency is a percentage of what was available.","","This is about 11%; it leaves the least biomass, but its percentage is lower than 15%.","A narrowing pyramid does not mean equal percentages; these steps give 9%, 15% and about 11%."]}
{"id":"gbi-305","s":"biology","t":"4.7.5.1","lv":1,"q":"A new strain of fungus spreads through a country's wheat crops. Which is the best reason this threatens food security?","o":["It adds carbon dioxide to the air, so the wheat grows too fast","It lowers the wheat yield, so less food is available for people","It kills the helpful bacteria in the soil that make the wheat grow","It increases the number of people who need to be fed"],"a":1,"x":"Pests and pathogens damage crops, so the yield falls and there is less food for people. New strains are a threat because farmers may have no treatment ready. Food security means having enough food.","w":["Fungal disease does not add carbon dioxide to the air. The harm comes from damaged crops.","","The fungus infects the wheat plants. It is not a reason about soil bacteria dying.","Confuses supply with demand; a plant disease does not change how many people there are."]}
{"id":"gbi-306","s":"biology","t":"4.7.5.1","lv":2,"q":"Chickens on an intensive farm are kept in warm sheds with little room to move. Why does this increase the mass of meat made from each kg of feed?","o":["The chickens stop respiring, so all the feed becomes meat","Warm sheds make the chickens digest food more slowly","Chickens kept in sheds eat less food, so the meat is cheaper","Less energy is lost by movement and keeping warm, so more goes into growth"],"a":3,"x":"Warm sheds and limited movement mean less energy is transferred to the surroundings as heat and movement. More energy from the feed goes into biomass. The trap is thinking respiration stops; it still happens.","w":["Chickens still respire to stay alive. Respiration is reduced, not stopped, so some energy is always lost.","Digestion speed is not the reason. The gain comes from less energy being lost to heat and movement.","This mixes up cost with efficiency. The key idea is how much energy becomes growth, not how little they eat.",""]}
{"id":"gbi-307","s":"biology","t":"4.7.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Bacteria can be used to make human insulin to treat diabetes. How is this possible?","o":["The human insulin gene is put into the bacterial DNA","Bacteria naturally make human insulin in warm fermenters","Human pancreas cells are grown inside the bacteria","Bacteria are fed insulin so they copy it when they divide"],"a":0,"x":"Genetic modification inserts the human insulin gene into bacterial DNA. The bacteria then make insulin as they grow. The trap is thinking bacteria make it naturally. They only do so after the gene is added.","w":["","Bacteria do not make human insulin on their own. They need the human gene added first.","Whole pancreas cells are not put inside bacteria. Only the gene is transferred.","Only DNA is copied when cells divide, not proteins. The bacteria need the gene to make insulin."]}
{"id":"gbi-308","s":"biology","t":"4.7.5.1","lv":2,"q":"Mycoprotein is a food made for people to eat. Which statement describes how it is produced?","o":["Fusarium grows in sunlit tanks with carbon dioxide so it can photosynthesise","Fusarium bacteria grow without oxygen on animal waste","Fusarium fungus grows in a fermenter with glucose syrup and a supply of air","Fusarium grows in fields and is then ground into flour"],"a":2,"x":"Mycoprotein comes from the fungus Fusarium. It is grown in a fermenter on glucose syrup with oxygen (aerobic conditions). The biomass is then harvested and purified. The trap is treating it as a plant or a bacterium.","w":["Fusarium is a fungus and cannot photosynthesise. It gets energy from the glucose.","Fusarium is a fungus, not a bacterium, and the process needs air (aerobic conditions).","","It is not field-grown. It is made in controlled fermenters."]}
{"id":"gbi-309","s":"biology","t":"4.7.5.1","lv":3,"q":"A country uses land to grow crops for people to eat instead of feeding cattle for beef. More people can be fed from the same land. Which is the best explanation?","o":["Beef contains no energy because cattle lose it all by respiration","Energy is lost at each stage of a food chain, so eating crops directly wastes less","Crops capture more light energy when people eat them than when cattle do","Cattle are at the start of the food chain, so they use more of the land's energy"],"a":1,"x":"Energy is lost at each trophic level as heat, movement and waste. If people eat crops directly, there is one fewer stage, so more of the energy reaches people. Beef still contains energy. Much less of the original energy is left.","w":["Some energy stays in the cattle as biomass, so beef does contain energy. The problem is how much is lost.","","Crops capture light energy as they grow. It does not depend on who eats them later.","Cattle are consumers, not producers. Plants are at the start of the food chain."]}
{"id":"gbi-fig-015","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Two people ate the same meal. Look at the graph at the time when person A's blood glucose is at its highest. How much higher is person B's blood glucose at that time?","o":["7 mmol/dm³ higher","8 mmol/dm³ higher","6 mmol/dm³ higher","14 mmol/dm³ higher"],"a":2,"x":"A's highest value is at 1 hour. At 1 hour, A is at 8 and B is at 14 mmol/dm³. The difference is 14 − 8 = 6 mmol/dm³.","w":["Used the highest point of B (at 2 hours) instead of B's value at the time A peaks.","Read only A's peak value and gave it as the difference.","","Read only B's value at that time and did not subtract A's value."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,4],"y":[0,16],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time after meal / hours","ylabel":"blood glucose concentration / mmol/dm³","lines":[{"pts":[[0,5],[1,8],[2,6],[3,5],[4,5]],"label":"Person A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,9],[1,14],[2,15],[3,14],[4,12]],"label":"Person B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-016","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":3,"q":"Person A has no diabetes. B and C both had high blood glucose after a sugary drink; one has type 1 diabetes, one type 2. The graph shows their insulin levels. Which person has type 2, and why?","o":["B, because the pancreas makes too little insulin","C, because insulin is made but the cells do not respond","B, because its insulin is lower than in person A","C, because the pancreas makes too little insulin"],"a":1,"x":"Person A is the healthy comparison. B has very little insulin, so B has type 1. C has as much insulin as A or more, yet glucose is high. So the cells are not responding: type 2.","w":["B's low insulin does show a pancreas making too little, but that is type 1, not type 2.","","Thought low insulin means type 2. Low insulin is the sign of type 1.","Picked C correctly but gave the type 1 reason. C's bar is high, so the pancreas is making insulin."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["A","B","C"],"vals":[30,5,40],"y":[0,40],"ystep":5,"xlabel":"person","ylabel":"insulin concentration / arbitrary units"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-017","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A person with type 1 diabetes injects insulin when their blood glucose is at its highest. Use points P and Q on the graph to find the mean rate at which their glucose falls.","o":["2 mmol/dm³ per hour","0.5 mmol/dm³ per hour","6 mmol/dm³ per hour","3 mmol/dm³ per hour"],"a":0,"x":"P is at 1 hour and 14 mmol/dm³. Q is at 4 hours and 8 mmol/dm³. Fall = 6 mmol/dm³ in 3 hours. Rate = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 mmol/dm³ per hour.","w":["","Divided the time by the fall (3 ÷ 6), so the gradient is upside down.","Found the fall in glucose but did not divide by the time taken.","Used the time gap of 3 hours as the rate instead of dividing the fall by it."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,16],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / hours","ylabel":"blood glucose concentration / mmol/dm³","lines":[{"pts":[[0,8],[1,14],[4,8],[6,8]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[1,14],"label":"P"},{"at":[4,8],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-003","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Potato cylinders were left in sugar solutions of different concentrations. Use the graph to find the concentration of sugar solution that matches the concentration inside the potato cells.","o":["0.0 mol/dm³","0.4 mol/dm³","0.2 mol/dm³","0.8 mol/dm³"],"a":1,"x":"There is no net water movement when the mass does not change. The line crosses 0% change at 0.4 mol/dm³. So the solution and the cell contents have the same concentration.","w":["Chose pure water, thinking no sugar means no change. Pure water makes the cylinder gain mass, about 20% on the graph.","","At 0.2 mol/dm³ the line is still at +10%, so the cells are still gaining water. Look for 0% change.","Chose the end of the line. The cylinder loses the most mass here, so water is moving out a lot."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,0.8],"y":[-20,20],"xstep":0.1,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"sugar concentration / mol/dm³","ylabel":"change in mass / %","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[0.2,10],[0.4,0],[0.6,-10],[0.8,-20]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-004","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Each potato cylinder started with a mass of 5.0 g. The graph shows the mass after 1 hour. What is the percentage change in mass for the cylinder in the 0.4 mol/dm³ solution?","o":["+20%","−25%","−1.0%","−20%"],"a":3,"x":"The bar for 0.4 mol/dm³ is at 4 g. Change = 4 − 5 = −1 g. Percentage change = −1 ÷ 5.0 × 100 = −20%. Water left the cells, so the mass fell.","w":["Lost the minus sign. The mass fell, so the change is negative.","Divided the change by the final mass (4 g) instead of the starting mass (5.0 g).","Gave the change in grams but did not turn it into a percentage of the starting mass.",""],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["0.0","0.2","0.4","0.6"],"vals":[6,5,4,3],"y":[0,7],"ystep":1,"xlabel":"sugar concentration / mol/dm³","ylabel":"mass after 1 hour / g"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-005","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A potato cylinder was put in a strong sugar solution and weighed over time. Assume all the mass change is water. What is the mean rate of water loss between 10 and 20 minutes?","o":["0.05 g/min","0.5 g/min","0.10 g/min","0.45 g/min"],"a":0,"x":"At 10 min the mass is 5.0 g. At 20 min it is 4.5 g. Loss = 0.5 g in 10 min. Rate = 0.5 ÷ 10 = 0.05 g/min. The line is flatter than at the start, so the rate is slower.","w":["","Gave the mass lost (0.5 g) and forgot to divide by the 10 minutes.","Used the first 10 minutes (6.0 to 5.0 g) instead of the 10 to 20 minute section.","Divided the mass at 20 min (4.5 g) by 10 min instead of using the change in mass."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,40],"y":[3,7],"xstep":10,"ystep":0.5,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"mass of cylinder / g","lines":[{"pts":[[0,6],[10,5],[20,4.5],[30,4.5],[40,4.5]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-006","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows two proteases. One works in the stomach, where it is acidic. Which curve is the stomach protease, and what is its rate of reaction at pH 4?","o":["Curve B, rate 0","Curve A, rate 8","Curve A, rate 4","Curve B, rate 4"],"a":2,"x":"The stomach is acidic, so its protease has a low optimum pH. That is curve A, which peaks at pH 2. At pH 4, curve A is at a rate of 4.","w":["Chose the small intestine protease, curve B, and read its rate at pH 4.","Read the peak of curve A instead of the rate at the pH asked for.","","Chose the wrong curve. Curve B only reaches a rate of 4 at pH 6, not pH 4."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[0,10],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"pH","ylabel":"rate of reaction / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,2],[2,8],[4,4],[6,0],[12,0]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[4,0],[6,4],[8,8],[10,4],[12,0]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-007","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student tested amylase at five pH values. The graph shows the time taken to digest all the starch. How many times faster is the reaction at the best pH than at pH 5?","o":["2 times faster","6 times faster","1.5 times faster","3 times faster"],"a":3,"x":"The shortest time is the fastest reaction. The best pH is 7, at 40 s. At pH 5 it takes 120 s. 120 ÷ 40 = 3, so it is 3 times faster.","w":["Compared the best pH with the pH 6 bar (80 s) instead of the pH 5 bar.","Used the pH 4 bar (240 s) instead of the pH 5 bar.","Compared the pH 5 and pH 6 bars (120 s and 80 s) and missed the best pH.",""],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["pH 4","pH 5","pH 6","pH 7","pH 8"],"vals":[240,120,80,40,120],"y":[0,240],"ystep":40,"xlabel":"pH of buffer","ylabel":"time for starch to disappear / s"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-008","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the pH of a lipase and milk mixture, with and without bile. How much sooner does the pH reach 6 with bile, and why does bile cause this?","o":["2 min sooner, because bile emulsifies lipids","2 min sooner, because bile is an enzyme that digests lipids","2 min sooner without bile, because bile makes it more alkaline","6 min sooner, because bile emulsifies lipids"],"a":0,"x":"With bile the pH reaches 6 at 4 min. Without bile it takes 6 min. So it is 2 min sooner. Bile emulsifies lipids, so there is a bigger surface area for lipase.","w":["","Got the time right but the reason wrong. Bile is not an enzyme. It emulsifies lipids.","Swapped the curves. The curve with bile reaches pH 6 first, and bile is alkaline but that is not why.","Gave the time without bile (6 min) instead of how much sooner it was."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[4,10],"xstep":2,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"pH of mixture","lines":[{"pts":[[0,9],[2,7],[4,6],[6,5],[10,5]],"label":"with bile","c":0},{"pts":[[0,9],[2,8],[4,7],[6,6],[8,5],[10,5]],"label":"no bile","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-013","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a student's heart rate before, during and after a run. The run stopped when the heart rate was highest. How long did the heart rate take to return to its resting value after the run stopped?","o":["12 minutes","10 minutes","6 minutes","4 minutes"],"a":2,"x":"The peak is at 6 min, when the run stopped. Heart rate is back to the resting 60 beats per minute at 12 min. Recovery time = 12 − 6 = 6 minutes.","w":["You read the time on the axis where it reaches rest, but did not subtract the time when the run stopped.","You measured from the start of exercise at 2 min, not from when the run stopped.","","You stopped at 80 beats per minute, not the resting rate. That point is at 10 min, so 10 − 6 = 4."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[40,180],"xstep":2,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"heart rate / beats per minute","lines":[{"pts":[[0,60],[2,60],[4,120],[6,160],[8,120],[10,80],[12,60]],"c":0}]}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-014","s":"biology","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows blood lactic acid after four activities. How much more lactic acid was there after the sprint than after the slow jog, and what does this show?","o":["12 mmol/dm³ more; more anaerobic respiration in the sprint","8 mmol/dm³ more; more anaerobic respiration in the sprint","8 mmol/dm³ more; more aerobic respiration in the sprint","10 mmol/dm³ more; more anaerobic respiration in the sprint"],"a":1,"x":"Sprint bar is 12 and slow jog bar is 4 mmol/dm³. 12 − 4 = 8 more. Lactic acid is made in anaerobic respiration, so the sprint used more anaerobic respiration.","w":["You read only the sprint bar and forgot to subtract the slow jog value.","","Lactic acid comes from anaerobic respiration, not aerobic. Aerobic respiration makes carbon dioxide and water.","You subtracted the resting value instead of the slow jog value."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Rest","Slow jog","Fast run","Sprint"],"vals":[2,4,6,12],"y":[0,14],"ystep":2,"xlabel":"activity","ylabel":"lactic acid in blood / mmol/dm³"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-026","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Foxes eat rabbits. The graph shows both populations over time. Which statement about the first rabbit peak and the first fox peak is correct?","o":["Foxes peak 2 years after rabbits, because more rabbits means more food","Foxes peak 1 year after rabbits, because more rabbits means more food","Foxes peak 1 year before rabbits, because more foxes means more rabbits","Foxes and rabbits peak together, because they depend on each other"],"a":1,"x":"The rabbit line peaks at 2 years. The fox line peaks at 3 years. So foxes peak 1 year later. More rabbits give foxes more food, so fox numbers rise after the rabbits.","w":["Counted the gap wrongly. The two peaks are only 1 gridline apart on the time axis.","","Swapped the lines. The rabbit line peaks first, so foxes cannot peak before rabbits.","Ignored the time axis. The peaks are at different times, so the populations do not peak together."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,100],"xstep":1,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / years","ylabel":"number of animals","lines":[{"pts":[[0,40],[1,60],[2,80],[3,60],[4,40],[5,20],[6,40]],"label":"Rabbits","c":0},{"pts":[[0,20],[1,20],[2,40],[3,60],[4,40],[5,20],[6,20]],"label":"Foxes","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-027","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Woodlice were put in a choice chamber with four different parts. The graph shows how many were in each part. What percentage of all the woodlice were in the damp, dark part?","o":["24%","40%","60%","75%"],"a":2,"x":"The bars are 4, 4, 8 and 24, so there are 40 woodlice in total. The damp, dark bar is 24. 24 ÷ 40 × 100 = 60%.","w":["Read the bar height as a percentage. There are 40 woodlice in total, so 24 must be divided by 40.","Used the other three bars (16 woodlice) as the part, not the damp, dark bar of 24.","Divided 24 by the two damp parts only (32), not by all the woodlice in the chamber.",""],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Dry, light","Dry, dark","Damp, light","Damp, dark"],"vals":[4,4,8,24],"y":[0,28],"ystep":4,"xlabel":"conditions in part of chamber","ylabel":"number of woodlice"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-028","s":"biology","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Plant A lives in a desert. Plant B lives in a wet habitat. At 30 °C, how much less water does plant A lose each hour than plant B?","o":["35 mg per hour","10 mg per hour","45 mg per hour","25 mg per hour"],"a":3,"x":"At 30 °C, plant B loses 35 mg per hour and plant A loses 10 mg per hour. The difference is 35 − 10 = 25 mg per hour. Plant A is adapted to save water.","w":["Read only the line for plant B. The question asks for the difference between the two plants.","Read only the line for plant A. The question asks for how much less it loses than B.","Added the two readings together instead of taking the smaller from the larger.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[10,40],"y":[0,40],"xstep":5,"ystep":5,"xlabel":"temperature / °C","ylabel":"water loss / mg per hour","lines":[{"pts":[[10,5],[20,5],[30,10],[40,15]],"label":"Plant A","c":0},{"pts":[[10,10],[20,20],[30,35],[40,40]],"label":"Plant B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-001","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how the rate of diffusion into a cell changes with the concentration difference. What is the increase in the rate of diffusion between point P and point Q?","o":["12 arbitrary units","3 arbitrary units","6 arbitrary units","2 arbitrary units"],"a":2,"x":"Read the rate at each point on the y-axis. P is at 6 and Q is at 12. The increase is 12 − 6 = 6 arbitrary units. A bigger concentration difference gives faster diffusion.","w":["Read the rate at Q only (12) instead of finding the change between P and Q.","Used the change along the x-axis (concentration difference) instead of the change in rate on the y-axis.","","Gave the gradient of the line (rate ÷ concentration difference) instead of the increase in rate."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,16],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"conc. difference / arbitrary units","ylabel":"rate of diffusion / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[8,16]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[3,6],"label":"P"},{"at":[6,12],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-002","s":"biology","t":"4.1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows ion uptake by root hair cells in three conditions. What is the change in uptake in nitrogen compared with air, and what explains it?","o":["A 25% fall, because less energy is released for active transport","A 75% fall, because less energy is released for active transport","A 75% fall, because less oxygen means ions diffuse more slowly","A 300% fall, because less energy is released for active transport"],"a":1,"x":"Air gives 16 and nitrogen gives 4. The fall is 12, and 12 ÷ 16 × 100 = 75%. Without oxygen, less respiration happens. That means less energy for active transport.","w":["Worked out 4 as a percentage of 16 (what is left) instead of the fall from 16.","","Percentage is right, but ions are taken up by active transport, which needs energy from respiration. It is not slowed diffusion.","Divided the fall (12) by the new value (4) instead of the starting value (16)."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Air","Nitrogen","Air, 5 °C"],"vals":[16,4,8],"y":[0,20],"ystep":4,"xlabel":"condition","ylabel":"ion uptake / arbitrary units"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-031","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the area of forest cleared in one country each year. What was the percentage increase in the area cleared from 2017 to 2019?","o":["60%","150%","250%","40%"],"a":1,"x":"Read the bars: 2017 is 4 and 2019 is 10 thousand hectares. Increase = 10 − 4 = 6. Percentage increase = 6 ÷ 4 × 100 = 150%.","w":["Divided the increase by the 2019 bar (the new value) instead of the 2017 bar (the starting value).","","Worked out 10 ÷ 4 × 100 and forgot to take away the original 100%, so it is not an increase.","Divided the 2017 bar by the 2019 bar. This gives 4 as a percentage of 10, not the increase."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["2016","2017","2018","2019","2020"],"vals":[6,4,8,10,12],"y":[0,12],"ystep":2,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"forest cleared / thousand hectares"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-032","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the carbon dioxide concentration in the air over 60 years. What is the mean rate of increase between points P and Q?","o":["40 ppm per year","0.5 ppm per year","1 ppm per year","2 ppm per year"],"a":3,"x":"Read P: 2000 at 360 ppm. Read Q: 2020 at 400 ppm. Rise = 40 ppm over 20 years. Rate = 40 ÷ 20 = 2 ppm per year.","w":["Gave the rise in concentration only and did not divide by the 20 years.","Turned the gradient upside down by working out 20 ÷ 40 (time ÷ rise).","Read the rate from the section between 1960 and 1980, not between P and Q.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[1960,2020],"y":[300,420],"xstep":10,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"carbon dioxide concentration / ppm","lines":[{"pts":[[1960,320],[1980,340],[2000,360],[2020,400]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[2000,360],"label":"P"},{"at":[2020,400],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-022","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the share of dark peppered moths in a wood near a new factory. When did the share rise most, and what is the best reason for the rise?","o":["1880 to 1900: dark moths were better camouflaged, so more survived to breed","1860 to 1880: moths turned dark because they needed to hide from birds","1860 to 1880: dark moths were better camouflaged, so more survived to breed","1840 to 1860: dark moths were better camouflaged, so more survived to breed"],"a":2,"x":"The biggest step up is from 20% to 60%, a rise of 40 points, between 1860 and 1880. Soot darkened the trees, so dark moths were camouflaged, survived and bred more. Other steps are smaller or zero.","w":["The rise from 1880 to 1900 is only 20 points. The 1860 to 1880 rise is 40, so the wrong pair of bars was compared.","The years are right, but the reason is wrong. Moths do not change colour because they need to. Mutations occur first, then selection acts.","","Bars for 1840 and 1860 are the same height, so there is no rise. The step between bars was not checked."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["1840","1860","1880","1900","1920"],"vals":[20,20,60,80,80],"y":[0,100],"ystep":20,"labels":["year","dark moths in the wood / %"],"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"dark moths in the wood / %"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-023","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows beak depth in finches on an island before and after a drought. What happened to the most common beak depth, and what is the best explanation?","o":["It rose by 10 mm because each bird's beak grew deeper during the drought","It fell by 2 mm because birds with shallower beaks survived and bred more","It rose by 10 mm because birds with deeper beaks survived and bred more","It rose by 2 mm because birds with deeper beaks survived and bred more"],"a":3,"x":"The peak is at 8 mm before the drought and 10 mm after, so the most common depth rose by 2 mm. Deeper-beaked birds could eat tough seeds, survived, bred and passed on their alleles.","w":["The change was read as the peak position, not the difference. Also, individual birds do not change their beaks to suit the environment.","The two curves were swapped, so the shift looks like a fall. The peak moves right to a deeper beak.","The change is the difference between the peaks, 2 mm. The value 10 mm is just where the after peak sits.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[4,14],"y":[0,40],"xstep":2,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"beak depth / mm","ylabel":"number of finches","lines":[{"pts":[[4.0,1.14262],[4.0625,1.275792],[4.125,1.422014],[4.1875,1.582245],[4.25,1.757477],[4.3125,1.94873],[4.375,2.157048],[4.4375,2.383493],[4.5,2.629141],[4.5625,2.895076],[4.625,3.18238],[4.6875,3.492128],[4.75,3.825378],[4.8125,4.18316],[4.875,4.56647],[4.9375,4.976257],[5.0,5.413411],[5.0625,5.878753],[5.125,6.373023],[5.1875,6.896865],[5.25,7.450819],[5.3125,8.035303],[5.375,8.650607],[5.4375,9.296873],[5.5,9.974088],[5.5625,10.682073],[5.625,11.420469],[5.6875,12.188726],[5.75,12.986099],[5.8125,13.811636],[5.875,14.664172],[5.9375,15.542325],[6.0,16.444492],[6.0625,17.368845],[6.125,18.313334],[6.1875,19.27569],[6.25,20.253425],[6.3125,21.24384],[6.375,22.244035],[6.4375,23.250921],[6.5,24.261226],[6.5625,25.27152],[6.625,26.278223],[6.6875,27.27763],[6.75,28.265931],[6.8125,29.239233],[6.875,30.193584],[6.9375,31.125001],[7.0,32.029496],[7.0625,32.903102],[7.125,33.741906],[7.1875,34.542072],[7.25,35.299876],[7.3125,36.01173],[7.375,36.674214],[7.4375,37.2841],[7.5,37.838379],[7.5625,38.334288],[7.625,38.769329],[7.6875,39.141296],[7.75,39.448285],[7.8125,39.688718],[7.875,39.861352],[7.9375,39.965293],[8.0,40.0],[8.0625,39.965293],[8.125,39.861352],[8.1875,39.688718],[8.25,39.448285],[8.3125,39.141296],[8.375,38.769329],[8.4375,38.334288],[8.5,37.838379],[8.5625,37.2841],[8.625,36.674214],[8.6875,36.01173],[8.75,35.299876],[8.8125,34.542072],[8.875,33.741906],[8.9375,32.903102],[9.0,32.029496],[9.0625,31.125001],[9.125,30.193584],[9.1875,29.239233],[9.25,28.265931],[9.3125,27.27763],[9.375,26.278223],[9.4375,25.27152],[9.5,24.261226],[9.5625,23.250921],[9.625,22.244035],[9.6875,21.24384],[9.75,20.253425],[9.8125,19.27569],[9.875,18.313334],[9.9375,17.368845],[10.0,16.444492],[10.0625,15.542325],[10.125,14.664172],[10.1875,13.811636],[10.25,12.986099],[10.3125,12.188726],[10.375,11.420469],[10.4375,10.682073],[10.5,9.974088],[10.5625,9.296873],[10.625,8.650607],[10.6875,8.035303],[10.75,7.450819],[10.8125,6.896865],[10.875,6.373023],[10.9375,5.878753],[11.0,5.413411],[11.0625,4.976257],[11.125,4.56647],[11.1875,4.18316],[11.25,3.825378],[11.3125,3.492128],[11.375,3.18238],[11.4375,2.895076],[11.5,2.629141],[11.5625,2.383493],[11.625,2.157048],[11.6875,1.94873],[11.75,1.757477],[11.8125,1.582245],[11.875,1.422014],[11.9375,1.275792],[12.0,1.14262],[12.0625,1.021574],[12.125,0.911767],[12.1875,0.812352],[12.25,0.722521],[12.3125,0.641508],[12.375,0.568592],[12.4375,0.503089],[12.5,0.44436],[12.5625,0.391806],[12.625,0.344868],[12.6875,0.303027],[12.75,0.2658],[12.8125,0.232743],[12.875,0.203443],[12.9375,0.177523],[13.0,0.154637],[13.0625,0.134467],[13.125,0.116726],[13.1875,0.10115],[13.25,0.0875],[13.3125,0.07556],[13.375,0.065137],[13.4375,0.056054],[13.5,0.048154],[13.5625,0.041296],[13.625,0.035353],[13.6875,0.030213],[13.75,0.025775],[13.8125,0.021951],[13.875,0.018662],[13.9375,0.015838],[14.0,0.013419]],"c":0,"label":"Before"},{"dash":true,"pts":[[4.0,0.010064],[4.0625,0.011879],[4.125,0.013997],[4.1875,0.016463],[4.25,0.019331],[4.3125,0.02266],[4.375,0.026515],[4.4375,0.030972],[4.5,0.036116],[4.5625,0.042041],[4.625,0.048853],[4.6875,0.05667],[4.75,0.065625],[4.8125,0.075862],[4.875,0.087544],[4.9375,0.100851],[5.0,0.115978],[5.0625,0.133142],[5.125,0.152582],[5.1875,0.174557],[5.25,0.19935],[5.3125,0.22727],[5.375,0.258651],[5.4375,0.293854],[5.5,0.33327],[5.5625,0.377317],[5.625,0.426444],[5.6875,0.481131],[5.75,0.54189],[5.8125,0.609264],[5.875,0.683825],[5.9375,0.766181],[6.0,0.856965],[6.0625,0.956844],[6.125,1.06651],[6.1875,1.186684],[6.25,1.318108],[6.3125,1.461548],[6.375,1.617786],[6.4375,1.78762],[6.5,1.971856],[6.5625,2.171307],[6.625,2.386785],[6.6875,2.619096],[6.75,2.869033],[6.8125,3.13737],[6.875,3.424853],[6.9375,3.732193],[7.0,4.060058],[7.0625,4.409065],[7.125,4.779767],[7.1875,5.172649],[7.25,5.588114],[7.3125,6.026477],[7.375,6.487955],[7.4375,6.972654],[7.5,7.480566],[7.5625,8.011555],[7.625,8.565351],[7.6875,9.141544],[7.75,9.739574],[7.8125,10.358727],[7.875,10.998129],[7.9375,11.656744],[8.0,12.333369],[8.0625,13.026634],[8.125,13.735001],[8.1875,14.456768],[8.25,15.190068],[8.3125,15.93288],[8.375,16.683027],[8.4375,17.438191],[8.5,18.19592],[8.5625,18.95364],[8.625,19.708667],[8.6875,20.458223],[8.75,21.199448],[8.8125,21.929425],[8.875,22.645188],[8.9375,23.343751],[9.0,24.022122],[9.0625,24.677327],[9.125,25.306429],[9.1875,25.906554],[9.25,26.474907],[9.3125,27.008798],[9.375,27.505661],[9.4375,27.963075],[9.5,28.378784],[9.5625,28.750716],[9.625,29.076997],[9.6875,29.355972],[9.75,29.586214],[9.8125,29.766538],[9.875,29.896014],[9.9375,29.97397],[10.0,30.0],[10.0625,29.97397],[10.125,29.896014],[10.1875,29.766538],[10.25,29.586214],[10.3125,29.355972],[10.375,29.076997],[10.4375,28.750716],[10.5,28.378784],[10.5625,27.963075],[10.625,27.505661],[10.6875,27.008798],[10.75,26.474907],[10.8125,25.906554],[10.875,25.306429],[10.9375,24.677327],[11.0,24.022122],[11.0625,23.343751],[11.125,22.645188],[11.1875,21.929425],[11.25,21.199448],[11.3125,20.458223],[11.375,19.708667],[11.4375,18.95364],[11.5,18.19592],[11.5625,17.438191],[11.625,16.683027],[11.6875,15.93288],[11.75,15.190068],[11.8125,14.456768],[11.875,13.735001],[11.9375,13.026634],[12.0,12.333369],[12.0625,11.656744],[12.125,10.998129],[12.1875,10.358727],[12.25,9.739574],[12.3125,9.141544],[12.375,8.565351],[12.4375,8.011555],[12.5,7.480566],[12.5625,6.972654],[12.625,6.487955],[12.6875,6.026477],[12.75,5.588114],[12.8125,5.172649],[12.875,4.779767],[12.9375,4.409065],[13.0,4.060058],[13.0625,3.732193],[13.125,3.424853],[13.1875,3.13737],[13.25,2.869033],[13.3125,2.619096],[13.375,2.386785],[13.4375,2.171307],[13.5,1.971856],[13.5625,1.78762],[13.625,1.617786],[13.6875,1.461548],[13.75,1.318108],[13.8125,1.186684],[13.875,1.06651],[13.9375,0.956844],[14.0,0.856965]],"c":1,"label":"After"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-035","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows sweat lost by a resting person at different air temperatures. How much more sweat is lost at 40°C than at 20°C?","o":["70 cm³ per hour","30 cm³ per hour","50 cm³ per hour","20 cm³ per hour"],"a":2,"x":"Read the 40°C bar: 70 cm³ per hour. Read the 20°C bar: 20 cm³ per hour. Subtract: 70 − 20 = 50 cm³ per hour. More sweat helps to cool the body when it is hot.","w":["Reads only the 40°C bar and forgets to subtract the amount lost at 20°C.","Compares the 40°C bar with the 30°C bar instead of the 20°C bar.","","Reads only the 20°C bar. This is the sweat lost at 20°C, not the difference."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["10","20","30","40"],"vals":[10,20,40,70],"y":[0,80],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"air temperature / °C","ylabel":"sweat lost / cm³ per hour"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-036","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Two people exercise for the same time. Using the graph, how much higher is B's core temperature than A's at the end, and what explains the shape of line A?","o":["1.0°C higher; A sweats and their skin blood vessels dilate, losing heat","1.5°C higher; A's skin blood vessels constrict, so less heat is lost","0.5°C higher; A sweats and their skin blood vessels dilate, losing heat","1.5°C higher; A sweats and their skin blood vessels dilate, losing heat"],"a":3,"x":"At the end, B is at 39.5°C and A is at 38.0°C, so B is 1.5°C higher. A levels off because sweating and dilated skin blood vessels lose heat as fast as exercise makes it.","w":["Reads the gap at 30 minutes (39.0 − 38.0) instead of at the end of the exercise.","The difference is right, but constriction reduces heat loss. It would make A's temperature keep rising, not level off.","Reads the gap at 20 minutes (38.5 − 38.0) instead of at the end of the exercise.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,40],"y":[36.5,40],"xstep":10,"ystep":0.5,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"core body temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,37],[10,37.5],[20,38],[30,38],[40,38]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,37],[10,37.5],[20,38.5],[30,39],[40,39.5]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-024","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":2,"q":"A bacterium was grown on agar. Paper discs soaked in four antibiotics were placed on it. The graph shows the clear zone round each disc. Which statement is correct?","o":["The bacteria are resistant to D, as it has the biggest clear zone","The bacteria are resistant to C, as its clear zone is the smallest shown","The bacteria are resistant to B, and D is the best antibiotic to use","Antibiotic B works best, as the bacteria grow closest to its disc"],"a":2,"x":"A clear zone means bacteria were killed. Antibiotic B has a zone of 0 mm, so the bacteria are resistant to it. D has the widest zone, so it kills the most bacteria and is the best choice.","w":["Thinks a big clear zone means resistance. A big zone means the antibiotic killed bacteria well.","Ignores B, which has no clear zone at all. B is the smallest, so resistance is to B, not C.","","Thinks growth right up to the disc shows the antibiotic works. It shows the antibiotic has no effect."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["A","B","C","D"],"vals":[15,0,10,20],"y":[0,25],"ystep":5,"xlabel":"antibiotic","ylabel":"diameter of clear zone / mm"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-025","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows the percentage of samples of one bacterium that were resistant to an antibiotic, in two hospitals. How many percentage points per year faster did resistance rise in A than in B?","o":["4 percentage points per year","2 percentage points per year","20 percentage points per year","0.5 percentage points per year"],"a":1,"x":"Hospital A rises from 10% to 50% in 10 years: 4 per year. Hospital B rises from 10% to 30%: 2 per year. The difference in rates is $4 - 2 = 2$ percentage points per year.","w":["Took the gap between the lines over one 2-year gridline step, not per year.","","Used the gap between the lines in 2010 and forgot to divide by the 10 years.","Turned the gradient upside down, working out years per percentage point instead of percentage points per year."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[2000,2010],"y":[0,60],"xstep":2,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"resistant samples / %","lines":[{"pts":[[2000,10],[2002,18],[2004,26],[2006,34],[2008,42],[2010,50]],"label":"Hospital A","c":0},{"pts":[[2000,10],[2002,14],[2004,18],[2006,22],[2008,26],[2010,30]],"label":"Hospital B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-020","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.7","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows thyroxine and TSH in one person's blood. At point P on the thyroxine line, what is the thyroxine level, and what does this show about negative feedback?","o":["6 units; high thyroxine makes the pituitary release more TSH","3 units; low thyroxine makes the pituitary release less TSH","3 units; low thyroxine makes the pituitary release more TSH","3 units; low thyroxine makes the thyroid release more TSH"],"a":2,"x":"At P (hour 4), the thyroxine line reads 3 units, which is low. TSH is at its peak. Low thyroxine makes the pituitary release more TSH. TSH then stimulates the thyroid, and thyroxine rises back to normal.","w":["Reads the TSH line (6) instead of the thyroxine line, then calls the level high.","Reads thyroxine correctly but reverses the response. Low thyroxine increases TSH release; it does not reduce it.","","Reads thyroxine correctly but mixes up the glands. The pituitary releases TSH. The thyroid releases thyroxine."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,9],"y":[0,8],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"time / hours","ylabel":"hormone level / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,6],[2,6],[3,3],[5,3],[7,6],[9,6]],"label":"Thyroxine","c":0},{"pts":[[0,2],[3,2],[4,6],[7,6],[8,2],[9,2]],"label":"TSH","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[4,3],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-021","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.7","lv":3,"q":"A person is frightened and adrenaline is released. Use the graph to find the percentage increase in heart rate caused by the fright, compared with resting.","o":["An increase of 33%","An increase of 50%","An increase of 150%","An increase of 30%"],"a":1,"x":"Resting heart rate is 60 beats/min. Frightened it is 90 beats/min. The increase is 30. Percentage increase = 30 ÷ 60 × 100 = 50%.","w":["Divides the increase by the frightened value (90) instead of the resting value (60).","","Works out 90 ÷ 60 × 100 and forgets to subtract the resting value first.","Gives the rise in beats per minute (30) and writes it as a percentage."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Resting","Frightened","10 min later"],"vals":[60,90,60],"y":[0,120],"ystep":30,"xlabel":"situation","ylabel":"heart rate / beats per min"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-018","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows hormone levels over one menstrual cycle. Which line shows the hormone that triggers ovulation, and on which day is that hormone at its highest?","o":["Line A (progesterone), day 21","Line B (LH), day 14","Line C (oestrogen), day 14","Line B (LH), day 8"],"a":1,"x":"Line B has the sharp, narrow peak. That is LH, which triggers ovulation. Read down from the peak to the x-axis: day 14. Line A is progesterone, which peaks later.","w":["Chose the later peak. Line A is progesterone, which keeps the uterus lining in place. It does not trigger ovulation.","","Line C is oestrogen. It builds up before ovulation, but the LH surge is what triggers the release of the egg.","Right line, but read the height (8 units) off the y-axis instead of the day off the x-axis."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,28],"y":[0,10],"xstep":7,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"day of cycle","ylabel":"hormone level / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,2],[14,2],[17,4],[21,6],[24,5],[28,2]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,1],[7,1],[12,2],[13,4],[14,8],[15,3],[16,2],[21,1],[28,1]],"label":"B","c":1},{"pts":[[0,1],[7,2],[10,4],[12,6],[14,4],[16,2],[21,4],[24,4],[28,3]],"label":"C","c":2}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-019","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows how many women in every 100 become pregnant in one year using each method. If 1000 women use the pill for a year, how many are expected to become pregnant?","o":["8","40","80","120"],"a":2,"x":"Read the pill bar: 8 pregnancies per 100 women. 1000 women is 10 groups of 100. So 8 × 10 = 80 expected pregnancies.","w":["Read the pill bar correctly but forgot to scale up from 100 women to 1000 women.","Read the injection bar instead of the pill bar, then multiplied by 10.","","Read the condom bar instead of the pill bar, then multiplied by 10."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Condom","Pill","Injection","Withdrawal"],"vals":[12,8,4,20],"y":[0,20],"ystep":4,"xlabel":"contraceptive method","ylabel":"pregnancies per 100 women per year"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-029","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Lines A and B on the graph show the numbers of a prey animal and its predator over time. Which line is the predator, and what on the graph shows this?","o":["A, because its peak is higher than the peak of B","B, because its peak comes 1 year after the peak of A","A, because its peak comes first, so it is eating B","B, because its numbers are lower than A at all times"],"a":1,"x":"Line A peaks at year 2. Line B peaks at year 3. A predator's numbers rise after its prey's numbers rise, so its peak comes later. B is the predator, with a 1 year lag.","w":["Judged by peak height. Predators are not always the lower or higher curve; the order of the peaks is what matters.","","Reversed cause and effect. The curve that peaks first is the prey. The predator rises after its food supply rises.","Right line, wrong reason. B is above A in year 4, so it is not lower at all times."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,600],"xstep":1,"ystep":100,"xlabel":"time / years","ylabel":"number of animals","lines":[{"pts":[[0,300],[1,400],[2,500],[3,300],[4,100],[5,300],[6,500]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,200],[1,100],[2,200],[3,300],[4,200],[5,100],[6,200]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-030","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows daisies counted in five random quadrats, each 0.5 m by 0.5 m. Use the graph to estimate the number of daisies on a lawn of area 80 m².","o":["320","1920","1280","6400"],"a":2,"x":"Counts: 3, 5, 2, 6, 4. Total 20, so the mean is 4 per quadrat. Quadrat area is 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25 m², so 16 per m². Then 16 × 80 = 1280.","w":["Used the mean of 4 per quadrat × 80 m². Forgot that each quadrat covers only 0.25 m².","Used the tallest bar (6) instead of the mean. 6 ÷ 0.25 × 80 gives this.","","Used the total of 20, not the mean. 20 ÷ 0.25 × 80 gives this."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["1","2","3","4","5"],"vals":[3,5,2,6,4],"y":[0,8],"ystep":1,"xlabel":"quadrat number","ylabel":"number of daisies"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-009","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":3,"q":"On the graph, point P is on the 0.04% CO₂ curve. What limits the rate at P, and what is the rate on the 0.12% CO₂ curve at the same light intensity?","o":["Light intensity; 20","Carbon dioxide; 30","Carbon dioxide; 20","Light intensity; 30"],"a":1,"x":"At P the 0.04% curve is flat, so more light does not help. Carbon dioxide is limiting. At the same light intensity the 0.12% curve reads 30.","w":["Thinks light limits where the line is flat, and reads the 0.04% curve instead of the 0.12% curve.","","Correctly says carbon dioxide limits, but reads the value from the 0.04% curve (20) instead of the 0.12% curve.","Reads the right curve (30), but thinks light limits where the line is flat. It is flat because light is no longer limiting."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,100],"y":[0,50],"xstep":20,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"light intensity / arbitrary units","ylabel":"rate of photosynthesis / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[40,20],[100,20]],"label":"0.04% CO₂","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[80,40],[100,40]],"label":"0.12% CO₂","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[60,20],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-010","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A student moves the lamp from 40 cm to 20 cm away from the pondweed. Use the graph to find the percentage increase in the bubble rate.","o":["300%","67%","200%","10%"],"a":2,"x":"At 40 cm the rate is 5 bubbles per minute. At 20 cm it is 15. The increase is 10. Percentage increase = $10 \\div 5 \\times 100 = 200\\%$.","w":["Worked out 15 ÷ 5 = 3 and called it 300%. That is the new value as a percentage of the old, not the increase.","Divided the increase by the new value (15) instead of the starting value (5).","","Gave the increase of 10 bubbles as a percentage without dividing by the starting value."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["10","20","30","40"],"vals":[20,15,10,5],"y":[0,25],"ystep":5,"xlabel":"distance of lamp from pondweed / cm","ylabel":"bubbles per minute"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-011","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows oxygen collected from pondweed with a lamp 10 cm away and 30 cm away. How much more oxygen per minute is made with the lamp at 10 cm than at 30 cm?","o":["2 cm³ per minute","20 cm³ per minute","4 cm³ per minute","0.25 cm³ per minute"],"a":0,"x":"Rate is the gradient. Lamp 10 cm: $40 \\div 10 = 4$ cm³ per minute. Lamp 30 cm: $20 \\div 10 = 2$ cm³ per minute. The difference is 2 cm³ per minute.","w":["","Took the difference in volume at 10 minutes (40 − 20) and did not divide by the time.","Gave the rate for the 10 cm lamp only and did not subtract the rate for the 30 cm lamp.","Turned the gradients upside down (time ÷ volume), giving 0.25 for the difference."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,40],"xstep":2,"ystep":5,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"volume of oxygen / cm³","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,40]],"label":"Lamp 10 cm","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[10,20]],"label":"Lamp 30 cm","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-012","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the rate of photosynthesis at different temperatures. Over which 10 °C step does the rate rise the most?","o":["0 to 10 °C","30 to 40 °C","40 to 50 °C","20 to 30 °C"],"a":3,"x":"Find the biggest rise in rate. From 20 to 30 °C the rate goes from 6 to 12, a rise of 6. Other rises are only 2 or 4. The drop from 40 to 50 °C is a fall.","w":["Picked the start of the line, but the rate only rises by 2 here.","Picked the steps with the highest rates, but the rise here is only 2.","Picked the biggest change, but this is a fall (14 to 6), not a rise. Enzymes are being denatured.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,60],"y":[0,16],"xstep":10,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"temperature / °C","ylabel":"rate of photosynthesis / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,2],[20,6],[30,12],[40,14],[50,6],[60,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-033","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1.6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the clear zones from four antiseptic discs on a bacterial lawn. Find the area of the clear zone for the most effective antiseptic. Use area = $\\pi r^2$.","o":["1257 mm²","314 mm²","63 mm²","100 mm²"],"a":1,"x":"The tallest bar is antiseptic B, with a diameter of 20 mm. Radius = 10 mm. Area = $\\pi \\times 10^2$ = 314 mm².","w":["Used the diameter as the radius, so worked out $\\pi \\times 20^2$.","","Worked out the circumference, $2\\pi r$, instead of the area.","Squared the radius but forgot to multiply by $\\pi$."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["A","B","C","D"],"vals":[12,20,16,8],"y":[0,24],"ystep":4,"xlabel":"antiseptic","ylabel":"diameter of clear zone / mm"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-034","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1.6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows two bacterial cultures. Culture A is kept warmer than culture B. At the last time on the graph, A has how many times as many bacteria as B?","o":["48 times as many","0.25 times as many","4 times as many","2 times as many"],"a":2,"x":"At 120 min, P (culture A) is at 64 thousand and Q (culture B) is at 16 thousand. 64 ÷ 16 = 4. The warmer culture divides faster, so it has 4 times as many bacteria.","w":["Subtracted the two values (64 − 16 = 48) instead of dividing them.","Divided the wrong way round (16 ÷ 64), so got B compared with A.","","Read the value for B as 32, which is the wrong gridline, then divided 64 by it."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,120],"y":[0,64],"xstep":20,"ystep":8,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"number of bacteria / thousands","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,1.0],[0.75,1.026334],[1.5,1.053361],[2.25,1.0811],[3.0,1.109569],[3.75,1.138789],[4.5,1.168777],[5.25,1.199556],[6.0,1.231144],[6.75,1.263565],[7.5,1.29684],[8.25,1.33099],[9.0,1.36604],[9.75,1.402013],[10.5,1.438934],[11.25,1.476826],[12.0,1.515717],[12.75,1.555631],[13.5,1.596597],[14.25,1.638641],[15.0,1.681793],[15.75,1.726081],[16.5,1.771535],[17.25,1.818186],[18.0,1.866066],[18.75,1.915207],[19.5,1.965641],[20.25,2.017404],[21.0,2.07053],[21.75,2.125055],[22.5,2.181015],[23.25,2.23845],[24.0,2.297397],[24.75,2.357896],[25.5,2.419988],[26.25,2.483716],[27.0,2.549121],[27.75,2.616249],[28.5,2.685145],[29.25,2.755855],[30.0,2.828427],[30.75,2.90291],[31.5,2.979355],[32.25,3.057813],[33.0,3.138336],[33.75,3.220981],[34.5,3.305801],[35.25,3.392856],[36.0,3.482202],[36.75,3.573902],[37.5,3.668016],[38.25,3.764609],[39.0,3.863745],[39.75,3.965492],[40.5,4.069919],[41.25,4.177095],[42.0,4.287094],[42.75,4.399989],[43.5,4.515858],[44.25,4.634777],[45.0,4.756828],[45.75,4.882094],[46.5,5.010658],[47.25,5.142607],[48.0,5.278032],[48.75,5.417022],[49.5,5.559673],[50.25,5.70608],[51.0,5.856343],[51.75,6.010562],[52.5,6.168843],[53.25,