12.772774],[111.0,12.996038],[111.75,13.223205],[112.5,13.454343],[113.25,13.68952],[114.0,13.928809],[114.75,14.17228],[115.5,14.420007],[116.25,14.672065],[117.0,14.928528],[117.75,15.189474],[118.5,15.454981],[119.25,15.72513],[120.0,16.0]],"c":1,"label":"B"}],"points":[{"at":[120,64],"label":"P"},{"at":[120,16],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-046","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Two people were tested for six hours. One drank water at the start. The other drank nothing. Use the graph to find the greatest difference in urine volume per hour between them.","o":["250 cm³ per hour","150 cm³ per hour","200 cm³ per hour","100 cm³ per hour"],"a":2,"x":"The gap is biggest at 2 hours. The water drinker makes 250 cm³ per hour. The other person makes 50 cm³ per hour. 250 − 50 = 200 cm³ per hour. The kidneys reabsorb less water, so more urine is made.","w":["Read only the peak of the water drinker's line and forgot to subtract the other person's value.","Used the gap at 3 hours. That is not the biggest gap on the graph.","","Used the gap at 1 hour. The gap is still growing then and keeps getting bigger until 2 hours."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,300],"xstep":1,"ystep":50,"xlabel":"time after drinking / hours","ylabel":"urine volume / cm³ per hour","lines":[{"pts":[[0,50],[1,150],[2,250],[3,200],[4,100],[5,50],[6,50]],"label":"1 L water","c":0},{"pts":[[0,50],[1,50],[2,50],[3,50],[4,50],[5,50],[6,50]],"label":"No water","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-048","s":"biology","t":"4.5.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A grower sprays a synthetic auxin on wheat and weeds. Weed growth must fall to 20% or less of normal, and wheat must keep at least 80%. Which range meets both, using the graph?","o":["From 0 to 2 mg per dm³","From 2 to 4 mg per dm³","From 4 to 6 mg per dm³","From 6 to 10 mg per dm³"],"a":2,"x":"The weed curve is at or below 20% from 4 mg per dm³ onwards. The wheat curve is at or above 80% up to 6 mg per dm³. Both conditions are met only from 4 to 6.","w":["Used only the wheat curve, which is healthy here. The weed is still at 60% or more, so it is not controlled.","Looked at where the weed curve falls steeply. The weed only reaches 20% at 4, so below that it is still above 20%.","","Used only the weed curve, which is low here. Wheat drops below 80% after 6, so it is damaged too."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,100],"xstep":2,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"auxin concentration / mg per dm³","ylabel":"growth / % of untreated plants","lines":[{"pts":[[0,100],[2,60],[4,20],[6,0],[8,0],[10,0]],"label":"Weed","c":0},{"pts":[[0,100],[2,100],[4,100],[6,80],[8,60],[10,40]],"label":"Wheat","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-045","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.1","lv":2,"q":"The dashed lines mark the normal range for blood glucose. Use the graph to decide which curve shows insulin, and when blood glucose first returns to the normal range.","o":["A; 2 hours","B; 1 hour","B; 2 hours","B; 3 hours"],"a":2,"x":"Curve A peaks first, at 1 hour, then falls while curve B is still rising, so B is insulin, released in response to high glucose. Glucose (A) first falls back to the top of the normal range, 6 units, at 2 hours.","w":["Curve A starts in the normal range and peaks first, so it is glucose from the meal, not insulin.","Reads the time when glucose is at its peak. Glucose is still far above the normal range then.","","Waits until glucose is well inside the range. It first reaches the normal range earlier, at the dashed line."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,4],"y":[0,9],"xstep":0.5,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"time after meal / hours","ylabel":"concentration / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,5],[0.5,7],[1,8],[1.5,7],[2,6],[3,5],[4,5]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,1],[0.5,3],[1,5],[1.5,6],[2,5],[3,3],[4,2]],"label":"B","c":1},{"pts":[[0,6],[4,6]],"dash":true,"c":2},{"pts":[[0,4],[4,4]],"dash":true,"c":3}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-039","s":"biology","t":"4.2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows water lost per hour from a plant in four conditions. How many times more water was lost in the windy condition than in the humid condition?","o":["2 times","3 times","4 times","6 times"],"a":2,"x":"Read the windy bar: 8 g per hour. Read the humid bar: 2 g per hour. Divide: 8 ÷ 2 = 4. Wind carries water vapour away from the leaves, so more water is lost.","w":["Compared the windy bar with the still and dry bar (8 ÷ 4) instead of the humid bar.","Used the warm bar (6) instead of the windy bar, then divided by the humid bar (6 ÷ 2).","","Subtracted the two values (8 − 2 = 6) and gave the difference as the number of times."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Still, dry","Windy","Humid","Warm"],"vals":[4,8,2,6],"y":[0,10],"ystep":2,"xlabel":"condition","ylabel":"water lost / g per hour"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-042","s":"biology","t":"4.4.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Point P is marked on the graph. Which statement best explains why the rate of photosynthesis at P is lower than at the peak of the curve?","o":["The temperature is too low, so enzymes have too little energy to react.","The temperature is above the optimum, so enzymes are denatured.","Light is the limiting factor, so a higher temperature cannot help.","Carbon dioxide is used up, so the rate falls as the temperature rises."],"a":1,"x":"The peak is at 30 °C. P is on the falling part of the curve, to the right of the peak. Above the optimum temperature the enzymes' active sites change shape. The enzymes are denatured and the rate falls.","w":["Reads P as being on the rising part of the curve. P is to the right of the peak, so the temperature is too high, not too low.","","Light is not the cause here. Only temperature changes on this graph, and P is past the peak, where the temperature is too high.","Carbon dioxide does not get used up because the temperature rises. Rising temperature is what the graph shows, and it denatures the enzymes."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,60],"y":[0,12],"xstep":10,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"temperature / °C","ylabel":"rate of photosynthesis / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[5,0.5],[10,2],[15,4],[20,6],[25,8.5],[30,10],[35,8],[40,4],[45,1],[50,0],[60,0]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[40,4],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-037","s":"biology","t":"4.1.1.6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows clear zone diameters for a control disc and three antiseptics on a bacterial lawn. How much wider is the clear zone of the most effective antiseptic than that of the least effective one?","o":["4 mm","20 mm","8 mm","32 mm"],"a":2,"x":"A bigger clear zone means a more effective antiseptic. Y is the most effective at 20 mm. X is the least effective antiseptic at 12 mm. The control is not an antiseptic. 20 − 12 = 8 mm.","w":["You compared Y (20 mm) with Z (16 mm), not with the least effective antiseptic, X.","You read the height of the tallest bar and did not subtract the smallest antiseptic bar.","","You added the two diameters instead of finding the difference between them."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Control","X","Y","Z"],"vals":[0,12,20,16],"y":[0,24],"ystep":4,"xlabel":"disc","ylabel":"diameter of clear zone / mm"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-051","s":"biology","t":"4.6.3.7","lv":2,"q":"A population of bacteria was treated with an antibiotic in rounds. The graph shows resistant and non-resistant (sensitive) bacteria. After which round were resistant bacteria first more numerous?","o":["Before treatment (round 0)","After treatment round 1","After treatment round 2","After treatment round 3"],"a":2,"x":"The lines cross at round 1, where both counts are equal. At round 2 the resistant line is higher than the sensitive line, so resistant bacteria are first more numerous after round 2.","w":["Reads the starting point, where the sensitive bacteria are far more numerous than the resistant ones.","Picks the crossing point, but there the two counts are equal, so resistant bacteria do not yet outnumber the others.","","Picks the round where the sensitive bacteria have gone, not the first round where resistant bacteria are ahead."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,4],"y":[0,90],"xstep":1,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"treatment round","ylabel":"number of bacteria / thousand","lines":[{"pts":[[0,80],[1,30],[2,10],[3,0],[4,0]],"label":"Sensitive","c":0},{"pts":[[0,10],[1,30],[2,60],[3,80],[4,90]],"label":"Resistant","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-044","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a person going into freezing air and then back to a warm room. How far does skin temperature fall in the cold, and what helps keep core temperature level?","o":["12 °C; sweating cools the blood so core temperature stays level","8 °C; vessels near the skin narrow and shivering warms the body","8 °C; vessels near the skin widen so more heat reaches the skin","12 °C; vessels near the skin narrow and shivering warms the body"],"a":1,"x":"Skin falls from 33 °C at the point marked Enter to 25 °C, a fall of 8 °C. Core stays at 37 °C because skin vessels narrow, so less heat is lost, and shivering releases heat.","w":["Used core minus skin (37 − 25) instead of the fall in skin temperature, and chose sweating, which cools the body and is not a response to cold.","","Read the fall correctly but chose vasodilation. Wider vessels lose more heat, so this response is for being too hot, not too cold.","Subtracted the lowest skin value from the core line instead of from the starting skin value, although the mechanism is right."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,60],"y":[23,39],"xstep":10,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,37],[10,37],[20,37],[40,37],[50,37],[60,37]],"label":"Core","c":0},{"pts":[[0,33],[10,33],[20,25],[40,25],[50,33],[60,33]],"label":"Skin","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[10,33],"label":"Enter"},{"at":[40,25],"label":"Leave"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-041","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a drug trial. One group of patients took the drug. The other group took a placebo. How many percentage points higher is the improvement with the drug than with the placebo?","o":["20 percentage points","60 percentage points","40 percentage points","80 percentage points"],"a":2,"x":"The placebo bar is at 20%. The drug bar is at 60%. The placebo group is the control, so the effect of the drug is the difference: 60 − 20 = 40 percentage points.","w":["Read only the placebo bar. This is the improvement without the drug, not the extra improvement from the drug.","Read only the drug bar. This forgets that some patients improve with a placebo, so the drug does not get all the credit.","","Added the two bars instead of finding the difference. Comparing a drug with a control needs a subtraction."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Placebo","Drug"],"vals":[20,60],"y":[0,80],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"treatment given","ylabel":"patients who improved / %"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-038","s":"biology","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the rate of starch breakdown by amylase at different pH values. Which answer gives the optimum pH and the best explanation for the rate at pH 2?","o":["pH 10; the active site changes shape, so starch cannot fit","pH 7; the acid kills the enzyme, so it cannot break down starch","pH 7; the active site changes shape, so starch cannot fit","pH 7; the acid changes the shape of the starch, so it cannot fit"],"a":2,"x":"The curve peaks at pH 7, so that is the optimum. At pH 2 the rate is 0. Strong acid changes the shape of the enzyme's active site (it is denatured), so starch no longer fits.","w":["Read the rate value at the peak (10) as if it were a pH. The optimum pH is on the x-axis, at the top of the curve.","Enzymes are proteins, not living things, so they cannot be killed. Acid denatures the enzyme by changing its active site.","","Mixed up enzyme and substrate. The acid changes the shape of the enzyme's active site, not the starch."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[1,10],"y":[0,12],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"pH","ylabel":"rate of reaction / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[1,0],[2,0],[3,2],[4,4.5],[5,7.5],[6,9.5],[7,10],[8,8],[9,4],[10,1]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-043","s":"biology","t":"4.5.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows mean reaction times from a ruler drop test. How much longer was the mean reaction time when texting than with no distraction?","o":["0.10 s","0.35 s","0.15 s","0.55 s"],"a":2,"x":"Read the two bars. Texting is 0.35 s and no distraction is 0.20 s. The difference is 0.35 − 0.20 = 0.15 s.","w":["Compared texting with the music bar (0.25 s) instead of the no distraction bar.","Gave the height of the texting bar only. The question asks for the difference between two bars.","","Added the two bar heights (0.35 + 0.20) instead of subtracting them."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["No distraction","Music","Talking","Texting"],"vals":[0.2,0.25,0.3,0.35],"y":[0,0.4],"ystep":0.05,"xlabel":"condition","ylabel":"mean reaction time / s"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-040","s":"biology","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A vaccination programme for this disease started at the beginning of 2018. Use the graph to find how many fewer cases there were in 2020 than in 2017.","o":["40 cases","80 cases","60 cases","100 cases"],"a":2,"x":"Read the 2017 bar: 100 cases. Read the 2020 bar: 40 cases. The fall is 100 − 40 = 60 cases. The fall began after vaccination started in 2018, which is consistent with vaccination reducing spread.","w":["Read only the 2020 bar and did not subtract it from the 2017 value.","Read the 2018 bar (80 cases) instead of finding the difference between 2017 and 2020.","","Read only the 2017 bar and gave the starting value, not the fall."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["2015","2016","2017","2018","2019","2020","2021","2022"],"vals":[80,100,100,80,60,40,40,20],"y":[0,120],"ystep":20,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"cases of disease per year"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-053","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Milk with indicator was kept at different temperatures. The graph shows the rate of decay. Which temperature gave the fastest decay, and why does the rate fall after it?","o":["30 °C; the enzymes of the microorganisms are denatured above this","40 °C; the microorganisms have run out of oxygen above this","40 °C; the enzymes of the microorganisms are denatured above this","50 °C; the enzymes of the microorganisms are denatured above this"],"a":2,"x":"The highest point on the graph is at 40 °C, where the rate is 8. Above this the rate falls steeply because the enzymes in the decay microorganisms denature, so decay slows.","w":["Picks 30 °C, where the rate is only 6. This point is still on the rising part of the curve, not the peak.","Reads the peak correctly but gives oxygen as the cause. Oxygen is not changed by the temperature here; the fall is due to enzymes denaturing.","","Picks 50 °C, where the curve has already fallen to half its peak value. The highest point is at 40 °C."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,60],"y":[0,10],"xstep":10,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"temperature / °C","ylabel":"rate of decay / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,2],[20,4],[30,6],[40,8],[50,4],[60,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-052","s":"biology","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows hare and lynx populations over time. Find the time from a peak in hare numbers to the next peak in lynx numbers. Which option gives this time and the reason for it?","o":["4 years; lynx need time to breed once there are many hares to eat","8 years; lynx need time to breed once there are many hares to eat","2 years; lynx need time to breed once there are many hares to eat","2 years; hares increase in number because there are many lynx to eat them"],"a":2,"x":"The hare peak is at year 2. The next lynx peak is at year 4. The lag is 4 − 2 = 2 years. Lynx numbers rise only after hares (their food) become plentiful, and lynx need time to breed.","w":["Reads the year of the lynx peak (4) instead of the gap between the two peaks.","Uses the gap between two hare peaks (one full cycle) instead of the gap between hare and lynx peaks.","","Gets the lag right but reverses the cause. Hares do not increase because of lynx. Lynx increase because of hares."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,16],"y":[0,80],"xstep":2,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / years","ylabel":"population / thousands","lines":[{"pts":[[0,40],[2,70],[4,40],[6,10],[8,40],[10,70],[12,40],[14,10],[16,40]],"label":"Hare","c":0},{"pts":[[0,10],[2,25],[4,40],[6,25],[8,10],[10,25],[12,40],[14,25],[16,10]],"label":"Lynx","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-054","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows CO₂ in the air, measured by NOAA at Mauna Loa, Hawaii (rounded values). Which statement about the rate of rise from P to Q and from Q to R is correct?","o":["P to Q rose faster, as it rose by more ppm in total","P to Q rose faster, at 0.67 against 0.5 for Q to R (years per ppm)","Q to R rose faster, at 2 ppm per year, against 1.5 ppm per year for P to Q","Both rose at the same rate, as the line is always going up"],"a":2,"x":"P to Q: 370 − 340 = 30 ppm in 20 years = 1.5 ppm per year. Q to R: 390 − 370 = 20 ppm in 10 years = 2 ppm per year. The rise is speeding up, linked to burning fossil fuels and deforestation.","w":["Compared the total rise only. The two periods are different lengths, so you must divide each rise by its time.","Worked out years per ppm, which is upside down. More years per ppm means a slower rise, so Q to R is faster.","","Saw that the line always rises but ignored that the line gets steeper, so the rate is not the same."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[1980,2020],"y":[340,420],"xstep":10,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"year","ylabel":"CO₂ concentration / ppm","lines":[{"pts":[[1980,340],[1990,354],[2000,370],[2010,390],[2020,414]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[1980,340],"label":"P"},{"at":[2000,370],"label":"Q"},{"at":[2010,390],"label":"R"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-055","s":"biology","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows global temperature anomaly (NASA) against CO₂ concentration (NOAA, Mauna Loa) for several years, rounded, with a line of best fit. P is on the line. Which statement is correct?","o":["Positive correlation; at P the anomaly is 0.8 °C, which proves CO₂ causes warming","Positive correlation; at P the anomaly is 0.8 °C, but this does not prove cause","Positive correlation; at P the anomaly is 0.6 °C, but this does not prove cause","No correlation, as points are off the line; at P the anomaly is 0.8 °C"],"a":1,"x":"Read up from P: the line is at 0.8 °C. The points rise from left to right, so there is positive correlation. Correlation alone does not prove that CO₂ causes the rise; other factors may act.","w":["Read P correctly but said correlation proves cause. A link in a graph does not by itself show that one thing causes the other.","","Misread the vertical scale by one gridline. P sits on the 0.8 gridline, not the 0.6 one.","Thought points must all lie on the line for a correlation. Points close to the line still show a clear trend."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[300,420],"y":[0,1.2],"xstep":20,"ystep":0.2,"xlabel":"CO₂ concentration / ppm","ylabel":"temperature anomaly / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[317,0.0],[326,0.0],[339,0.3],[354,0.5],[369,0.4],[390,0.7],[414,1.0]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[320,0],[420,1.0]],"label":"Best fit","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[400,0.8],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-047","s":"biology","t":"4.5.3.4","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows LH levels in a normal cycle and in a cycle on the combined pill. How much higher is LH at P than at Q on day 14?","o":["40 arbitrary units","5 arbitrary units","35 arbitrary units","30 arbitrary units"],"a":2,"x":"Read P on the normal line: 40. Read Q on the pill line: 5. Difference: 40 − 5 = 35 arbitrary units. On the pill there is no LH surge, so ovulation does not happen.","w":["Reads only the value at P and does not subtract the value at Q.","Reads only the value at Q on the pill line and does not compare it with P.","","Subtracts the normal line's starting value of 10 from P instead of using the value at Q."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,28],"y":[0,40],"xstep":7,"ystep":5,"xlabel":"day of cycle","ylabel":"LH level / arbitrary units","lines":[{"pts":[[0,10],[7,10],[12,15],[14,40],[16,15],[21,10],[28,10]],"label":"Normal","c":0},{"pts":[[0,5],[28,5]],"label":"Pill","dash":true,"c":1}],"points":[{"at":[14,40],"label":"P"},{"at":[14,5],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-049","s":"biology","t":"4.6.1.6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows offspring from crossing two heterozygous tall pea plants. Tall is dominant. What is the observed ratio of tall to dwarf, and how does it compare with the expected 3:1?","o":["1:4, so fewer tall plants than expected","3:1, exactly matching the expected ratio for this cross","4:1, slightly more tall plants than the 3:1 expected","4:1, which is fewer tall plants than the 3:1 expected"],"a":2,"x":"Read the observed bars: tall 80, dwarf 20. 80 ÷ 20 = 4, so the ratio is 4:1. The expected ratio is 3:1, so there are slightly more tall plants than expected. Small differences are normal because fertilisation is random.","w":["Turned the ratio upside down, putting dwarf first. Tall is the larger bar, so the ratio is tall to dwarf, 4:1.","Used the expected ratio instead of the observed bars. The observed bars show 80 and 20, which is 4:1.","","Worked out 4:1 correctly but compared it wrongly. A bigger first number means more tall plants than 3:1, not fewer."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Tall observed","Dwarf observed","Tall expected","Dwarf expected"],"vals":[80,20,75,25],"y":[0,80],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"phenotype and result","ylabel":"number of offspring"}}
{"id":"gbi-fig-050","s":"biology","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows bacteria in a patient's body. An antibiotic was first used at generation 2. By how much did the percentage of resistant bacteria increase between generation 2 and generation 5?","o":["20 percentage points","80 percentage points","60 percentage points","40 percentage points"],"a":2,"x":"At generation 2 the line is at 20%. At generation 5 it is at 80%. The increase is 80 − 20 = 60 percentage points. Resistant bacteria survive the antibiotic and breed, so the allele becomes more common.","w":["Reads only the value at generation 2 and does not subtract it from the generation 5 value.","Reads only the value at generation 5 and forgets to take away the starting percentage.","","Starts from generation 3 (40%) instead of generation 2 (20%), so the start value is wrong."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,100],"xstep":1,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / generations","ylabel":"bacteria with resistance allele / %","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[2,20],[3,40],[4,60],[5,80],[6,100],[7,100],[8,100]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
